/* Machine translations of Baumgarten, Metaphysica (1757), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['baum/guyer'] = {"baum:1":["Metaphysics (*metaphysica*) is the science (*scientia*) of the first principles (*principia*) in human cognition (*cognitio*).",[]],"baum:2":["Ontology (*ontologia*), cosmology (*cosmologia*), psychology (*psychologia*), and natural theology (*theologia naturalis*) are referred to metaphysics.",[]],"baum:3":["Natural metaphysics (*metaphysica naturalis*) is the cognition (*cognitio*) of things occurring in metaphysics acquired by use alone, to which it is useful that the artificial one defined in §. 1 be added: 1) on account of the development of concepts (*conceptus*). 2) On account of the determination (*determinatio*) and conception of the first propositions. 3) On account of the continuation and certainty of proofs (*probationes*), e.c.",[]],"baum:4":["Ontology@@1@@ (*ontosophia*, *metaphysica*, cf. §. 1, *metaphysica universalis*, *architectonica*, *philosophia prima*,) is the science (*scientia*) of the more general predicates (*praedicata*) of a being (*ens*).",[]],"baum:5":["The more general predicates (*praedicata*) of a being (*ens*) are the first principles (*principia*) of human cognition (*cognitio*), therefore ontology (*ontologia*) is referred, §. 2, with reason, to metaphysics (*metaphysica*), §. 1, 4.",[]],"baum:6":["Ontology (*ontologia*) contains the predicates (*praedicata*) of a being (*ens*), §. 4, I) internal, 1) universal, which are in single things, 2) disjunctive, of which one or the other is in single things; II) relative.",[]],"baum:7":["The negative nothing (*nihil negativum*), cf. §. 54, the unrepresentable, the impossible, the repugnant, (the absurd, cf. §. 13,) involving, implying a contradiction (*contradictio*), the contradictory, is A and not-A, or, of contradictory predicates none is a subject, or, it is nothing, and it is not. 0 = A + not-A. This proposition is called the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*), and absolutely first.",[]],"baum:8":["Something not nothing is a something (*aliquid*)@@1@@: the representable (*repraesentabile*), whatever does not involve a contradiction, whatever is not A and not-A, is possible (*possibile*)@@2@@, §. 7.",[]],"baum:9":["A and not-A is not a something, §. 8, hence it is nothing and contradictory, §. 7, or an implying subject has no predicates, or, whatever is and is not, is nothing. A + not-A = 0.",[]],"baum:10":["Every possible (*possibile*) is either A, or not-A, or neither, §. 8; now neither is nothing, because it would be both, §. 9. Therefore every possible either is A, or not-A, or, to every subject, of all contradictory predicates one or the other belongs. This proposition is called the principle of the excluded third (*principium exclusi tertii*), or of the middle, between two contradictories.",[]],"baum:11":["Every possible (*possibile*) A is A, or, whatever is, that is, or, every subject is a predicate of itself. If you deny it: some possible A is not-A, §. 10, hence A and not-A, or nothing, §. 7, which is impossible, §. 9. This proposition is called the principle of position (*principium positionis*), or of identity.",[]],"baum:12":["When the impossible is posited, a contradiction (*contradictio*) arises@@1@@. What not only seems, but also is, is called true (*verum*)@@2@@; what only seems, is not, is called apparent (*apparens*)@@3@@. Hence a contradiction that has arisen is either true, or apparent.",[]],"baum:13":["When A and not-A are posited, a contradiction (*contradictio*) arises, §. 9, 12. When A and B are posited, by the positing of which not-A is posited, the impossible is posited, §. 9, hence a contradiction arises, §. 12, the former is called manifest (*patens*)@@1@@ (direct, immediate, and explicit), the latter latent (*latens*)@@2@@ (indirect, cryptic, mediate, and implicit). That in which a true contradiction is manifest is absurd (*absurdum*)@@3@@ (*absonum*).",[]],"baum:14":["Ground (*ratio*)@@1@@, cf. §. 640, (condition, hypothesis,) is that from which it is cognizable why something is. What has a ground, or, of which something is the ground, is called its grounded (*rationatum*)@@2@@, and dependent (*dependens*)@@3@@ on it. A predicate by which something is either a ground, or a grounded, or both, is a nexus (*nexus*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:15":["What is considered, but not in a nexus with those things that are posited outside it, is considered in itself (*in se*)@@1@@. What is not representable even when considered in itself is impossible in itself (*impossibile in se*)@@2@@, (intrinsically, simply, absolutely, through itself). What when considered in itself is possible, is possible in itself (*possibile in se*)@@3@@ (intrinsically, absolutely, through itself, simply).",[]],"baum:16":["What, in a nexus even with some of those things that are posited outside it, is nonetheless possible, is hypothetically possible (*possibile hypothetice*)@@1@@ (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and in a certain respect).",[]],"baum:17":["What is impossible only in some nexus with those things that are posited outside it, is hypothetically impossible (*impossibile hypothetice*)@@1@@ (respectively, relatively, extrinsically, through another, and in a certain respect).",[]],"baum:18":["Nothing absolutely impossible is hypothetically possible, §. 15, 16. Therefore nothing hypothetically possible is absolutely impossible. Everything hypothetically impossible and possible is possible in itself, §. 17, 15. Therefore things absolutely impossible are neither hypothetically possible nor impossible. Something absolutely possible is hypothetically impossible.",[]],"baum:19":["The possible in a nexus, i.e. that in which there is a nexus, to which a nexus belongs, is connected (*connexum*)@@1@@ (rational), the impossible in a nexus is irrational (*irrationale*)@@2@@ (unconnected, incoherent). Hence irrational things are either impossible in themselves, or hypothetically impossible, §. 15, 17.",[]],"baum:20":["Every possible either has a ground (*ratio*), or not, §. 10. If it has a ground, something is its ground, §. 8. If it does not have one, nothing is its ground, §. 7. Therefore of every possible the ground is either nothing, or something, §. 10. If nothing were the ground of some possible, it would be cognizable from nothing why that is, §. 14, hence nothing itself would be representable and a something, §. 8, nothing a something, §. 14, 8. Hence some possible is impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Therefore of every possible something is the ground, or every possible is a grounded (*rationatum*), or nothing is without ground, or, when something is posited, something is posited as its ground. This proposition is called the principle of ground (*principium rationis*), which you may also gather from §. 265, 279, partly by abstracting, partly by avoiding a circle.",[]],"baum:21":["The ground (*ratio*) of single things in something is its sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*)@@1@@ (complete, total); the ground of only some things in the same is its insufficient ground (*ratio insufficiens*)@@2@@ (incomplete, partial).",[]],"baum:22":["Nothing is without a sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*), or, when something is posited, something is posited as its sufficient ground. Single things in every possible (*possibile*) have a ground, §. 20, hence every possible has a sufficient ground, §. 21. This proposition is called the principle of sufficient ground (*principium rationis sufficientis*) (of agreement).",[]],"baum:23":["Every possible (*possibile*) is a ground (*ratio*), or nothing is without a grounded (*rationatum*), nothing without a corollary and a pledge, nothing wholly sterile, idle, and unfruitful, or when something is posited something grounded by it is posited. For every possible either has a grounded, or not, §. 10. If it has, something is its grounded, §. 8; if it does not, nothing is its grounded, §. 7. Therefore of every possible the grounded is either nothing, or something, §. 10. If nothing were the grounded of some possible, it could be cognized from this, §. 14, hence it would be a something, §. 8, and therefore some possible would be impossible, §. 7, 8, which is absurd, §. 9. Let this proposition be called the principle of the grounded (*principium rationati*).",[]],"baum:24":["Every possible (*possibile*) is a ground (*ratio*) and a grounded (*rationatum*), §. 20, 23, hence by a double nexus (*nexus*), §. 14, connected and rational, §. 19, cognizable both a priori and a posteriori. Let this proposition be called the principle of things connected on both sides (*principium utrimque connexorum*) (from the side before, and from the side after).",[]],"baum:25":["The ground (*ratio*) A of the ground B, is the ground of the grounded (*rationatum*) C. From the ground of B it can be cognized why C is, §. 23, hence A is the ground of C, §. 14.",[]],"baum:26":["The grounded (*rationatum*) C of the grounded B, is the grounded of the ground (*ratio*) A, §. 25, 14.",[]],"baum:27":["The ground (*ratio*) A of some B, on which C depends, is the mediate ground (*ratio mediata*)@@1@@ (further, remote) of this C; a ground that is not mediate is immediate (*immediata*)@@2@@ (nearest).",[]],"baum:28":["A ground (*ratio*) that still has a further one is called a ground in a certain respect (*secundum quid*)@@1@@ (intermediate,); one that does not have one is such simply (*simpliciter talis*)@@2@@ (ultimate). The grounds and groundeds (*rationata*) of something are either considered also as grounds and groundeds among themselves, subordinate (*subordinata*)@@3@@, or not, coordinate (*coordinata*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:29":["When the grounded (*rationatum*) is posited, some ground (*ratio*) of it is posited, §. 20, 14, and a sufficient one, §. 22, or from the grounded to the ground, and the sufficient one, V. C.",[]],"baum:30":["When the ground (*ratio*) is posited, hence also the sufficient one, §. 21, the grounded (*rationatum*) is posited, §. 23, or from the ground, and the sufficient one, to the grounded V. C.",[]],"baum:31":["When the ground (*ratio*), and the sufficient one, is removed, some grounded (*rationatum*) is removed, because if this were posited, that would also be posited, §. 29.",[]],"baum:32":["When the grounded (*rationatum*) is removed, its ground, and the sufficient one, is removed, because if this were posited, that would also be posited, §. 30.",[]],"baum:33":["A and B connected (*connexa*) with a third C are connected among themselves; A is connected with C, which is connected with B; there is therefore in A, concerning which it can be cognized from B why it is, hence A and B are connected, §. 19.",[]],"baum:34":["What is either posited to be A, or posited not to be A, is determined (*determinatum*)@@1@@. But what is only posited to be either A, or not A, is undetermined (*indeterminatum*)@@2@@. Or, if concerning a subject with respect to contradictory predicates nothing is posited, except that one or the other of them belongs to it, that subject with respect to these predicates is undetermined; but it is determined, if one or the other is posited in the subject. What can be determined is determinable (*determinabile*)@@3@@. That, therefore, of which either it can be posited that it is A, or that it is not A, is determinable.",[]],"baum:35":["The ground of determining (*ratio determinandi*) is the determining (*determinans*)@@1@@. Every ground (*ratio*), therefore, a sufficient one sufficiently, an insufficient one insufficiently, determines, §. 34, 21. Hence when the determining is posited, the determined is posited, §. 30, and vice versa, §. 29. When the determined is removed, the determining is removed, §. 32, and vice versa, §. 31.",[]],"baum:36":["What is posited in something by determining (notes and predicates) are determinations (*determinationes*)@@1@@, one positive, and affirmative, §. 34, 10, which if it is true, is reality (*realitas*), the other negative, §. 34, 10, which if it is true, is negation (*negatio*)@@2@@. An apparent negation is a cryptic reality, an apparent reality is vanity (*vanitas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:37":["The determinations (*determinationes*) of a possible are either representable in it, even if it is not yet considered in a nexus, absolute (*absolutae*)@@1@@, or only when it is considered in a nexus, §. 10, respective (*respectivae*)@@2@@ (assumptive). The respective determinations of possibles are respects (*respectus*)@@3@@ (habitudes, τα προσ τι, relations more broadly said, either to the outside, or to the inside). Respects of possibles not representable in the same when considered in themselves are relations (*relationes*)@@4@@ (more strictly said, to the outside). The relations of possibles are their external determinations (*determinationes externae*)@@5@@ (relative, to the outside, extrinsic), all the remaining ones, internal (*internae*)@@6@@.",[]],"baum:38":["If in A there are the things that are in B, A and B are the same (*eadem*)@@1@@. Things not the same are diverse (*diversa*)@@2@@ (other).",[]],"baum:39":["The internal determinations (*determinationes internae*) of a possible are either grounds of the remaining internal ones in internal ones simply such, or not, §. 10. Those are the first (principal), or essentials (*essentialia*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:40":["The complex of essentials in a possible, or its internal possibility, is essence (*essentia*)@@1@@ (the being of a thing, formal ground, nature, cf. §. 430, quiddity, form, the formal of the whole, ουσια τινοτισ, substance, cf. §. 191, the first concept of a being).",[]],"baum:41":["The internal determinations (*determinationes internae*) of a possible, groundeds (*rationata*) of the essence, are affections (*affectiones*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:42":["An internal determination (*determinatio interna*), which is not essential (*essentiale*), is a consequent (*rationatum*) of the essence (*essentiae*), §. 39, 40, hence an affection (*affectio*), §. 41.",[]],"baum:43":["With the essence (*essentia*) posited in a possible (*possibili*), the affections (*affectiones*) are posited, §. 41, 30.",[]],"baum:44":["With the affections (*affectionibus*) posited in a possible (*possibili*), some essence (*essentia*) is posited, §. 41, 29.",[]],"baum:45":["With the essence (*essentia*) removed, some affections (*affectiones*) are removed, §. 41, 31.",[]],"baum:46":["With the affections (*affectionibus*) removed, the essence (*essentia*) is removed, §. 41, 32.",[]],"baum:47":["All internal determinations (*determinationes internae*) of a possible (*possibilis*) are connected (*connexae*) among themselves, each with each. For the affections (*affectiones*) are connected each with the essentials (*essentialibus*), §. 39, these with the essence (*essentia*), §. 40, 14. Hence the individual determinations each with each, §. 33.",[]],"baum:48":["The universal@@1@@ nexus (*nexus*) (harmony, *harmonia*) is that which is in individuals (*singulis*).",[]],"baum:49":["In the internal determinations (*determinationibus internis*) of a possible (*possibilis*) there is a universal nexus (*nexus universalis*), §. 47, 48.",[]],"baum:50":["Affections (*affectiones*) have a ground (*rationem*) in the essence (*essentia*), §. 41, hence either a sufficient one or less so, §. 21, 10. The former are attributes@@1@@ (*attributa*), the latter modes@@2@@ (*modi*) (predicable accidents, or logical ones, cf. §. 191, adjuncts, secondary predicates).",[]],"baum:51":["Attributes (*attributa*) have a sufficient ground (*rationem sufficientem*) either in all the essentials (*essentialibus*), or only in some, §. 50, 10. The former are proper@@1@@ (*propria*), the latter common@@2@@ (*communia*).",[]],"baum:52":["Every determination (*determinatio*) of possibles (*possibilium*) is either an essential (*essentiale*), §. 39, or an attribute (*attributum*), or a mode (*modus*), §. 42, or a relation (*relatio*), §. 37.",[]],"baum:53":["Every possible (*possibile*) is determined (*determinatum*), as regards possibility, §. 34, 8, hence in itself possible, as regards internal possibility, §. 15, which, since it is the essence (*essentia*), §. 40, every possible has an essence, determined as regards essence. Therefore what is in every way undetermined is nothing (*nihil*), §. 7.",[]],"baum:54":["A possible (*possibile*) besides the essence (*essentiam*), §. 53, is either determined as regards all the affections (*affectiones*) also compossible in it itself, or less so, §. 34, 10. The former is called actual@@1@@ (*actuale*), the latter a privative (*privativum*) non-being (*non ens*) (nothing, cf. §. 7) (merely possible)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:55":["Existence@@1@@ (*exsistentia*) (act, *actus*, cf. §. 210, actuality, *actualitas*) is the complex (*complexus*) of affections (*affectionum*) in something of the compossibles (*compossibilium*), i.e. the complement (*complementum*) of the essence or of internal possibility, insofar as this alone is regarded as a complex of determinations, §. 40.",[]],"baum:56":["Every internal possible determination (*determinatio*) pertains either to its essence (*essentiam*) or to its existence (*exsistentiam*), §. 55, 42.",[]],"baum:57":["Everything actual (*actuale*) is internally possible (*interne possibile*), §. 54, or, with existence posited, internal possibility is posited, §. 55, 40, from being to being able, valid consequence (V. C.).",[]],"baum:58":["Nothing internally impossible (*interne impossibile*) is actual, §. 57; with internal possibility removed, actuality (*actualitas*) is removed, §. 55, 40, or from not being able to not being, V. C.",[]],"baum:59":["Something possible (*possibile*) is not actual, §. 54, or, with possibility posited in a certain case, actuality can nevertheless be removed, or from a certain being able to being, not a valid consequence (N. V. C.).",[]],"baum:60":["Something not actual (*non actuale*) is possible, §. 59; with actuality removed, not all possibility is removed, or from not being to wholly not being able, N. V. C.",[]],"baum:61":["A possible (*possibile*), as regards existence (*exsistentiam*), determinable (*determinabile*), is a being@@1@@ (*ens*).",[]],"baum:62":["A non-being@@1@@ (*non ens*) (negative, *negativum*, cf. §. 54) would be a possible, as regards existence, not determinable, §. 61. But this is impossible, §. 10, and, if it seems to be a being, is a fictitious being@@2@@ (*ens fictum*) (of the ratiocinating reason, *rationis ratiocinantis*, cf. §. 647).",[]],"baum:63":["Every being (*ens*) is possible, §. 61, has a sufficient ground (*rationem sufficientem*), §. 20, 22, and a consequent (*rationatum*), §. 23, hence is connected in two ways, §. 24, has an essence (*essentiam*), §. 53, and essentials (*essentialia*), §. 40, hence affections (*affectiones*), §. 43, in a universal nexus (*nexu universali*), §. 49. All determinations of a being are either essentials, or attributes (*attributa*), or modes (*modi*), or relations (*relationes*), §. 52. With a being posited, its essence is posited, hence all the essentials. With the essence removed, the being is removed. With an essential removed, the essence is removed, hence the being itself, §. 53, 40. Finally, every internal determination of a being pertains either to its essence or to its existence (*exsistentiam*), §. 56.",[]],"baum:64":["Through the essence (*essentiam*) the attributes (*attributa*) of a being (*entis*) are sufficiently determined, §. 35, 50, hence, with the essence posited, the attributes are posited, §. 35. Therefore, with a being posited, its attributes are posited, §. 63; with an attribute removed, the essence and the being are removed, §. 35, 63.",[]],"baum:65":["The modes (*modi*) of a being (*entis*) are not sufficiently determined through the essence (*essentiam*), §. 50, 35, hence are not determined as regards existence (*exsistentiam*), § 55. A being therefore, as regards actual modes, is undetermined through the essence, §. 34, 54, i.e. modes can be absent and present with the essence of the thing remaining intact.",[]],"baum:66":["Existence (*exsistentia*) is not repugnant to the essence (*essentiae*), but is a reality (*realitas*), §. 36, compossible with it, §. 50, 55.",[]],"baum:67":["Cognition (*cognitio*) of diversity is distinction@@1@@ (*distinctio*), and the ground (*ratio*) of distinction in distinguishing is the distinguishing feature (*discrimen*) (difference, *differentia*, character, distinctive character taken more broadly, *character distinctivus latius dictus*, cf. §. 350, note, characteristic mark, *nota characteristica*)@@2@@. Now every determination (*determinatio*) of a being (*entis*) is that from which it can be cognized that that being is neither undetermined nor determined in another way, §. 36, 34. Therefore every determination is a distinguishing feature of a being, §. 38, 14.",[]],"baum:68":["The distinguishing features (*discrimina*) of a being (*entis*) are either external or relative, or internal, §. 67, 37, and these either essential, which pertain to the essence, or accidental, which pertain to existence, §. 56, or absolute, or respective, §. 37.",[]],"baum:69":["Internal distinguishing features (*discrimina interna*) can be represented in a being (*ente*) regarded in itself, §. 68, 37, hence cognized in whatever way, or given@@1@@. Of the given we either can also, without another assumed (without compresence), without relation to another, conceive@@2@@ and understand, i.e. distinctly cognize, or we cannot. The former are qualities@@3@@ (*qualitates*), the latter quantities@@4@@ (*quantitates*).",[]],"baum:70":["As regards quality the same are similar @@1@@ (*similia*), as regards quantity, equal = @@2@@ (*aequalia*), as regards both, congruent _ @@3@@ (*congruentia*). Diverse as regards quality, dissimilar @@4@@ (*dissimilia*), as regards quantity, unequal @@5@@ (*inaequalia*), diverse as regards both, incongruent @@6@@ (*discongruentia*).",[]],"baum:71":["Merely similar things (*mere similia*) are not congruent (*congruentia*), hence differ in quantity (*quantitate*), §. 70, hence quantity is the internal distinguishing feature (*discrimen internum*) of merely similar things.",[]],"baum:72":["The determinations (*determinationes*) of a being (*entis*) are separated@@1@@ (*separantur*) if, from those posited together, some are removed. Hence inseparable@@2@@ (*inseparabiles*) are those of which, posited together, none can be removed.",[]],"baum:73":["With a being (*ente*) posited, the essence (*essentia*) is posited, §. 63, therefore the complex (*complexus*) of the essentials (*essentialium*), §. 40, hence with a being posited all the essentials are posited at the same time, and indeed in such a way that none can be removed, §. 63, 40. Therefore the essentials of a being are inseparable through themselves (*per se*), §. 72, 15. One@@1@@ (*unum*) is that whose determinations are inseparable, and transcendentally@@2@@ (*transcendentaliter*) so, that whose determinations are inseparable through themselves. Therefore every being is transcendentally one (*unum transcendentale*).",[]],"baum:74":["A one and B one, e.g., partly the same, partly diverse, are many@@1@@ (*multa*). Whatever we think, either are many or not many, §. 10. The former determination is multitude (*multitudo*) (plurality, *pluralitas*)@@2@@, the latter categorical unity@@3@@ (*unitas categorica*).",[]],"baum:75":["The individual determinations (*determinationes*) of a being (*entis*) are one, §. 73, partly the same, while they are determinations of the same being, partly diverse, §. 38, hence many, §. 74. Therefore multitude can be given in a being regarded in itself, §. 69, and is its internal distinguishing feature (*discrimen internum*), §. 37, which we nevertheless cannot understand unless one other is assumed, §. 74, 38, hence multitude is quantity, §. 69.",[]],"baum:76":["Since the inseparability (*inseparabilitas*) of determinations (*determinationum*) is the impossibility of separation, §. 72, it is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. Hence unity (*unitas*) is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 73.",[]],"baum:77":["Whatever is, which many are not, is unique (*unicum*) (one exclusively so)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:78":["If many are posited beside or after one another, they are conjoined@@1@@ (*coniunguntur*). The conjunction (*coniunctio*) of several is either the same or diverse, §. 10, 38. If the former, it is coordination@@2@@ (*coordinatio*), and its identity is order@@3@@ (*ordo*). The science of order was formerly music taken more broadly.",[]],"baum:79":["Diversity in the conjunction of several is confusion (*confusio*) (disorder, *inordinatio*)@@1@@. The conjunction of inseparables is union@@2@@ (*unitio*).",[]],"baum:80":["Every determination (*determinatio*) has a ground (*rationem*), §. 20, 36, and whatever can be cognized from some determinate ground is said to be conformable to the ground (*rationi conformis*) (suitable, agreeing)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:81":["If, A being posited, B is removed, A and B are opposites@@1@@ (*opposita*).",[]],"baum:82":["A determination (*determinatio*) opposite to that which is conformable to the ground (*rationi conformis*) is contrary to the ground (*rationi contraria*) (nonconformable, unsuitable) or a defect@@1@@ (*defectus*).",[]],"baum:83":["A proposition enunciating a determination (*determinationem*) conformable to the ground is a norm (*norma*) (rule@@1@@, *regula*, law, *lex*), indeed, more broadly, a representation (*repraesentatio*) of a determination conformable to the ground.",[]],"baum:84":["Wherever there are determinations (*determinationes*), there are laws (*leges*), §. 83, 80.",[]],"baum:85":["The respect of a being (*entis*) determined from its conjunction with others is position@@1@@ (*positus*). Where therefore there is position, there are laws, §. 84, 37.",[]],"baum:86":["By conjunction (*coniunctione*) position is determined, hence in conjunction there are laws, §. 85, in the same the same, §. 38. Therefore in order (*ordine*) several are conjoined conformably to the same ground, §. 83, 78. What is in A and B is common@@1@@ (*commune*) to them. What is in A, not in B, is, with respect to B, proper@@2@@ (*proprium*) to A (τῳ A), therefore in order there are common rules (*regulae communes*).",[]],"baum:87":["In a diverse conjunction (*coniunctione*) there are diverse laws (*leges*), §. 86, 38. Therefore in confusion (*confusione*) there are no common laws, §. 79, 86.",[]],"baum:88":["If the rule of an order (*ordinis*) is single, it is called simple@@1@@ (*simplex*), if several, compound order@@2@@ (*ordo compositus*).",[]],"baum:89":["Metaphysical truth@@1@@ (*veritas metaphysica*) (real, objective, material) is the order (*ordo*) of several in one, truth in the essentials (*essentialibus*) and attributes (*attributis*) of a being (*entis*), transcendental@@2@@ (*transcendentalis*).",[]],"baum:90":["Since the determinations (*determinationes*) of every being (*entis*) are conjoined, the essential ones according to the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*), §. 40, 7, and the accidental ones, the attributes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 64, 7, and of reason (*rationis*), §. 20, and of sufficient reason, §. 22, 50, the modes according to the principle of contradiction, §. 65, 7, and of reason, §. 42, 20, the essentials and affections (*affectiones*) according to the principle of the consequent (*principium rationati*), §. 23, 41, hence common rules, §. 83, 86, every being is transcendentally true (*verum transcendentaliter*), § 89.",[]],"baum:91":["Confusion (*confusio*) opposed to transcendental truth would be a dream taken objectively@@1@@ (*somnium obiective sumptum*) (cf. §. 593). An aggregate of dreams would be a fabulous world@@2@@ (*mundus fabulosus*) (cf. §. 354).",[]],"baum:92":["Catholic@@1@@ (*catholica*) (universal) principles (*principia*) (cf. §. 307, 311) are common to individual beings. Things metaphysically true are determined suitably by catholic principles, §. 7, 20, 22, 23, §. 90, 80, and those which are determined conformably to these principles are metaphysically true, §. 89. Hence metaphysical truth (*veritas methaphysica*) can be defined through the agreement (*convenientiam*) of a being with catholic principles.",[]],"baum:93":["Objective certainty@@1@@ (*certitudo obiectiva*) (cf. §. 531) is the apperceptibility (*apperceptibilitas*) of truth in a being (*ente*). Now the truth of every being is clearly cognizable, §. 90, 8. Therefore every being is objectively certain.",[]],"baum:94":["If several taken together constitute the sufficient ground (*rationem sufficientem*) of one, they agree@@1@@ (*consentiunt*). The agreement (*consensus*) itself is perfection@@2@@ (*perfectio*), and the one, in which they agree, the determining ground of perfection (*ratio perfectionis determinans*) (focus of perfection)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:95":["In perfection (*perfectione*) several are determined conformably to the same ground, §. 94, 80, therefore there is in perfection order (*ordo*), §. 78, and common rules (*regulae*) of perfection, §. 86.",[]],"baum:96":["If the determining ground (*ratio determinans*) of perfection is only one, it is simple (*simplex*)@@1@@, if several, the perfection is then composite (*composita*)@@2@@, §. 88, 95.",[]],"baum:97":["Opposite rules are said to collide (*collidi*)@@1@@, and a defect from colliding rules of perfection an exception (*exceptio*)@@2@@, according as norms collide by an opposition either true or merely apparent, §. 81, or true, or apparent, §. 12.",[]],"baum:98":["The agreement (*consensus*) of the essentials is (essential) transcendental perfection (*perfectio transcendentalis*)@@1@@, of accidental affections accidental@@2@@, each internal@@3@@. The agreement of relations is external perfection@@4@@.",[]],"baum:99":["The essentials (*essentialia*) of every being (*ens*) agree toward its essence (*essentia*), §. 63, 40, and attributes (*attributa*), §. 50, 94. Therefore every being is transcendentally perfect.",[]],"baum:100":["The good (*bonum*)@@1@@ is that by the positing of which perfection is posited. Therefore every being is transcendentally good, §. 99.",[]],"baum:101":["The necessary (*necessarium*)@@1@@ is that whose opposite is impossible, the not necessary is the contingent (*contingens*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:102":["That whose opposite is in itself impossible is the necessary in itself@@1@@ (metaphysically, intrinsically, absolutely, geometrically, logically). That whose opposite is only extrinsically impossible is necessary hypothetically@@2@@ (*secundum quid*). The determination (*determinatio*) of a being by which it is necessary is its necessity@@3@@. Therefore necessity is either absolute@@4@@ (of the consequent), or hypothetical@@5@@ (of the consequence), the former that by which something is in and through itself, the latter that by which something is only hypothetically necessary.",[]],"baum:103":["Possibility (*possibilitas*) involves the opposite, §. 81, 7, hence the possibility of a being is its necessary determination, §. 101, the intrinsic intrinsically, the extrinsic extrinsically, §. 102.",[]],"baum:104":["That whose opposite is absolutely possible is contingent in itself@@1@@ (through itself, intrinsically), that whose opposite is also hypothetically possible is also extrinsically contingent@@2@@ (hypothetically). The determination of a being by which it is contingent is its contingency@@3@@. Therefore contingency is either absolute@@4@@, by which something is at least in and through itself, or hypothetical@@5@@, by which something is hypothetically contingent.",[]],"baum:105":["Nothing absolutely necessary is in any way contingent, §. 102, 104. Therefore whatever is in any way contingent is not absolutely necessary. Everything hypothetically necessary is contingent in and through itself, §. 18. Therefore something contingent in and through itself is hypothetically necessary. Everything hypothetically contingent is also in itself, §. 104.",[]],"baum:106":["The essences of things are absolutely necessary in them, §. 40, 103.",[]],"baum:107":["With the essentials and attributes removed the essence is removed, §. 63, 64, hence the internal impossibility of the being is posited, §. 81, 40. Therefore both the essentials and the attributes are absolutely necessary determinations of the being, §. 103.",[]],"baum:108":["The opposite of the modes (*modi*) is in the being absolutely possible, §. 65, 81, hence the modes are determinations of the being contingent in themselves, §. 104. Therefore not absolutely necessary, §. 105. The opposite of the absolutely necessary determinations in a being is in the same absolutely impossible, §. 102. Therefore they themselves are absolutely possible in the being, §. 81, hence representable in the same considered in itself, §. 15. But relations (*relationes*) are not representable in a being considered in itself, §. 37. Therefore no relations of a being are absolutely necessary, all are contingent, §. 101.",[]],"baum:109":["Since the opposite of existence (*exsistentia*) in something is possible, §. 54, 55, existence will either be absolutely necessary, or contingent in itself, §. 102, 10. A being whose existence is absolutely necessary is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*)@@1@@, one whose existence is intrinsically contingent is a contingent being (*ens contingens*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:110":["All internal determinations of a necessary being are absolutely necessary. For they pertain either to the essence, or to the existence, §. 56. Both the latter and the former are absolutely necessary in a necessary being, §. 109, 106.",[]],"baum:111":["In a necessary being there are no modes, §. 110, 108. Therefore that in which modes inhere is a contingent being, §. 109.",[]],"baum:112":["Every contingent being has modes. For posit that no modes inhere in some contingent being, all its affections (*affectiones*) are absolutely necessary, §. 52, 107, hence its existence too is absolutely necessary, §. 55, which was to be shown, §. 109.",[]],"baum:113":["The determinations of one are inseparable, §. 73, hence the opposite of any of them is impossible, §. 72, 81, therefore the determinations of one are necessary, §. 101, and indeed absolutely those of what is absolutely one, hypothetically those of what is hypothetically one, §. 102, 76. Things united are necessarily conjoined, §. 79, either in themselves, or hypothetically, §. 102.",[]],"baum:114":["The necessary are determinable in only a single mode and way. For they are either A, or non-A. A third mode, a third way of determinability is impossible, §. 10. Now posit that the necessary is determinable through A; non-A is the opposite of this determination, §. 81, hence impossible, §. 101. Therefore there are not several modes and ways of determinability, besides the single A, §. 77. Let them be determinable in only a single mode and way A, their opposite would be non-A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. Let them be non-A, their opposite would be A, §. 81, but this is impossible, §. 77. A third is not given, §. 10. Therefore the opposite of what is determinable in only a single mode and way is impossible, and so they themselves are necessary, §. 102. The contingent are not necessary, hence they are neither determinable in only a single mode and way, nor in more than two, §. 10. Therefore they are determinable in a twofold mode. What is determinable in a twofold mode and way is not necessary, hence contingent, §. 102. Therefore the following can be defined: 1) the necessary, through the determinable in only a single [mode], 2) the contingent, through the determinable in a twofold mode, 3) necessity through the singleness, 4) contingency through the twofoldness of determinability.",[]],"baum:115":["A one that is such hypothetically has determinations separable in themselves, §. 76, 18, hence its unity (*unitas*) is intrinsically contingent, §. 104.",[]],"baum:116":["Transcendental unity is absolutely necessary, §. 73, 102, hence there is no opposite of it, §. 102, 15.",[]],"baum:117":["Since the opposites of order (*ordo*) are confusion, §. 79, 78, and a conjunction not made, §. 78, 81, absolutely necessary order is in things necessarily conjoined through themselves and indeed in the same mode, §. 102. Therefore wherever both the conjunction and the identity of the conjunction are not absolutely necessary, there is order contingent in itself, §. 104.",[]],"baum:118":["Transcendental truth (*veritas*) is order in the essentials and attributes, §. 89, hence in things necessarily conjoined through themselves, §. 78, 107, and indeed in the same mode, §. 7, 22, hence transcendental truth is absolutely necessary, §. 117, and has no opposite, §. 102, 15.",[]],"baum:119":["The order of several in one, whose non-conjunction or confusion is internally possible in the being, is truth contingent in itself, §. 117, 89.",[]],"baum:120":["A dream taken objectively and a fabulous world are non-beings (*nonentia*), §. 118, 91, and if they seem beings, they are fictitious beings, §. 62.",[]],"baum:121":["The opposite of perfection is imperfection (*imperfectio*)@@1@@, and indeed 1) simple non-agreement, if among several taken together some are not grounds of one: this is imperfection said privatively, 2) disagreement (*dissensus*), if among several taken together some agree toward one, some toward its opposite: this is imperfection said contrarily, §. 81, 94.",[]],"baum:122":["The perfection of those whose non-agreement, hence also disagreement, is impossible in itself, is absolutely necessary, §. 121, 102. But perfection in those that not to agree or even to disagree is possible in itself, is contingent in itself, §. 104, 121.",[]],"baum:123":["Transcendental perfection is absolutely necessary, §. 122, 94, hence has no opposite imperfection, §. 121, 102.",[]],"baum:124":["Those succeed (*succedunt*)@@1@@ one another (are successive to one another) of which one exists after another. The determination of beings by which they are successive to one another is their succession (*successio*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:125":["That whose determinations succeed one another is changed (*mutatur*)@@1@@: hence the changeable (*mutabile*)@@2@@ (variable) is that whose determinations can succeed one another; that whose determinations cannot succeed one another is unchangeable (*immutabile*)@@3@@ (fixed, invariable, constant). But the very succession of determinations in a being is its, and at the same time its determinations', change (*mutatio*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:126":["The change of a being is either internal@@1@@ of the internal, or external@@2@@ or relative of relations, §. 125, 37.",[]],"baum:127":["The changeability (*mutabilitas*)@@1@@ of a being, or the possibility of changes in the same, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 16. The unchangeability (*immutabilitas*)@@2@@ of a being, or the impossibility of changes in the same, is either absolute, §. 125, 15, or hypothetical, §. 125, 17.",[]],"baum:128":["Nothing unchangeable through itself is hypothetically changeable. Therefore nothing hypothetically changeable is absolutely unchangeable. Everything hypothetically unchangeable is changeable in itself. Everything hypothetically changeable is changeable in itself. Therefore the absolutely unchangeable are neither hypothetically changeable, nor unchangeable. Something absolutely changeable is hypothetically unchangeable, §. 127, 18.",[]],"baum:129":["The determinations of the changeable can succeed one another, §. 125, hence the changeable are determinable in several modes, §. 74, 34. The necessary are not determinable in several modes, §. 114. Therefore the necessary are unchangeable, §. 125.",[]],"baum:130":["The absolutely necessary are absolutely unchangeable, the hypothetically necessary are hypothetically unchangeable, §. 129, 127.",[]],"baum:131":["Nothing changeable is necessary, §. 129, hence everything changeable is contingent, §. 101, and what is changeable in itself is contingent in itself, what is hypothetically changeable is hypothetically contingent, §. 104, 127.",[]],"baum:132":["The essences of things, §. 106, the essentials and attributes, §. 107, the existence of a necessary being, §. 109, all its internal determinations, §. 110, unity, §. 116, truth, §. 118, and perfection, transcendental, §. 123, are absolutely and internally unchangeable, §. 130, 126.",[]],"baum:133":["The modes of a being can succeed one another in it considered in itself, §. 124, 65, hence both they themselves, and the being in which they inhere, are absolutely changeable, §. 125, 127. Now every contingent being has modes, §. 112. Therefore every contingent being is absolutely and internally changeable, §. 126. The relations of a being are intrinsically contingent, §. 108. Therefore they can succeed one another in it considered in itself, §. 124, 104. Hence they are absolutely changeable in every being, §. 127. Every being, in respect of all its relations, is changeable, §. 125.",[]],"baum:134":["Essence (*essentia*) is not mutable (*mutabilis*), §. 132, hence every contingent (*contingens*) being (*ens*) is mutable, as regards existence (*exsistentia*), §. 133, 56. Hence the existence of a contingent being is mutable, §. 125, and so is neither essential nor an attribute (*attributum*), §. 132, yet an internal determination (*determinatio*), §. 55, therefore a mode (*modus*), §. 52. Whose existence is a mode, its existence is absolutely mutable, §. 133, hence also intrinsically contingent, §. 131. Therefore whose existence is a mode is a contingent being, §. 109. A contingent being can hence be defined through a being whose existence is a mode.",[]],"baum:135":["With negation (*negatio*) posited, reality (*realitas*) is removed, §. 36, 10. Hence negations and realities are mutually opposed to one another, §. 81. Both the realities themselves and the beings in which they inhere are called real beings (*entia realia*)@@1@@ or positive. But negations are called negative beings (*entia negativa*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:136":["A merely negative being (*ens mere negativum*) would be one in which no reality inhered, hence neither possibility (*possibilitas*), §. 8, nor rationality (*rationalitas*), §. 19, nor actuality (*actualitas*), §. 55, nor unity (*unitas*), §. 73, nor truth (*veritas*), §. 89, nor perfection (*perfectio*), §. 94, which are realities, §. 36. Therefore a merely negative being is a non-being (*non ens*), and, if it seems a being, it is a fictitious being (*ens fictum*), §. 62. Since therefore some reality inheres in every being, every being is real, §. 135.",[]],"baum:137":["Since every being is real, §. 136, either no negation will inhere in it, or some will inhere together with its realities, §. 10, and this indeed either absolutely necessary, or in itself contingent, §. 102, 104. The former is negation strictly speaking (*negatio stricte dicta*)@@1@@ (negative being strictly speaking). The latter is privation (*privatio*)@@2@@ or privative being (*ens privativum*). Negative essentials (*essentialia*) and attributes are negations strictly speaking, §. 106, 107, negative modes are privations, §. 108.",[]],"baum:138":["No privations in a being are absolutely necessary, §. 137, 105, hence internal ones are not essentials, nor attributes, §. 107. Therefore modes, §. 52. A necessary being (*ens necessarium*) accordingly does not have internal privations, §. 111, and that in which they inhere is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"baum:139":["A negative being, as such, is not positive, §. 135, but, if it agreed (*consentiret*), as a negation, to one reality, it would be a reality, §. 94, 36, hence a negative being, as such, does not agree in the real being in which it inheres, §. 137, to one reality.",[]],"baum:140":["Realities, as such, do not agree, except to realities. For to be a grounded (*rationatum*) of mere realities is reality, §. 36, 14.",[]],"baum:141":["Every being is perfect, §. 99, and real, §. 137, hence its perfection, as such, is the agreement (*consensus*) of realities to one, §. 94, 140.",[]],"baum:142":["With negations posited in a being, non-agreement is posited, §. 141, 139, hence imperfection (*imperfectio*), §. 121, hence with negations strictly speaking posited in a being, absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 137.",[]],"baum:143":["The absolutely necessary imperfection of a being is either its attribute, or essential, §. 107, 108, but, because in realities there is no sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of negations, §. 139, there is no imperfection in the attributes of a being which is not also in its essentials, §. 50, nor is there imperfection in the essentials of a being which would not also be in its affections (*affectiones*), §. 23.",[]],"baum:144":["With privations posited in a being, imperfection is posited, §. 142, but in itself contingent, §. 138.",[]],"baum:145":["There is no essential perfection without accidental, §. 98, 140, nor accidental without essential, §. 98, there is no essential imperfection without accidental, nor accidental without essential, §. 143, 98, nor are essential perfection and imperfection opposed, §. 81, 142.",[]],"baum:146":["That, with which posited imperfection is posited, is evil (*malum*)@@1@@, hence negations are evil, §. 142, and these either strictly speaking, metaphysical evil (*malum metaphysicum*)@@2@@, with which posited absolutely necessary imperfection is posited, §. 142, or privations, contingent evil (*malum contingens*)@@3@@ (physical broadly speaking, cf. §. 788), with which posited imperfection in itself contingent is posited, §. 144.",[]],"baum:147":["With the realities of a being posited, its perfection is posited, §. 141, hence realities are goods (*bona*), §. 100, and absolutely necessary ones indeed, metaphysical good (*bonum metaphysicum*)@@1@@, in themselves contingent ones, contingent good (*bonum contingens*)@@2@@ (physical broadly speaking, cf. §. 787).",[]],"baum:148":["The complex of all determinations compossible in a being is its omnimodal determination (*omnimoda determinatio*)@@1@@. Hence a being is either omnimodally determined, or less so, §. 10. The former is singular (*singulare*)@@2@@ (individual, *individuum*), the latter universal (*universale*)@@3@@. Each, with respect to all the less determined which it contains in itself, is called inferior (*inferius*)@@4@@, those, with respect to it, superior (*superiora*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:149":["A universal being regarded in its inferior, and a singular regarded in so far as other predicates of it too, besides a certain universal, are regarded, is regarded in the concrete (*in concreto*)@@1@@ and then is called concrete (*concretum*)@@2@@. A universal being which is indeed attended to, yet not in its inferior, and a singular, in which yet only a certain superior of it is attended to, is regarded in the abstract (*in abstracto*)@@3@@, and then is called abstract (*abstractum*)@@4@@. The universal in the concrete is the physical universal (*universale physicum*)@@5@@ (in many, in the thing), the universal in the abstract is the logical (*logicum*)@@6@@ (after many, after the thing).",[]],"baum:150":["A universal representable in the concrete in individuals alone, or, which contains individuals alone under itself, is a species (*species*)@@1@@, that which is also representable in universals in the concrete, or, which also contains universals under itself, is a genus (*genus*)@@2@@, and the lowest (*infimum*)@@3@@ of these, that which is in no genus, or which contains no genus under itself, the highest (*summum*)@@4@@ that in which no genus, or which is contained under no genus, subaltern (*subalterna*)@@5@@ finally are called those which are not highest.",[]],"baum:151":["The determinations of an inferior being, undetermined in its superior, are the difference (*differentia*)@@1@@ of that. Hence generic difference (*differentia generica*)@@2@@ is the complex of determinations determined in the genus, undetermined in its superior. Specific difference (*differentia specifica*)@@3@@ is the complex of determinations of the species undetermined in its lowest genus. Numerical difference (*differentia numerica*)@@4@@ (*haecceitas*, principle of individuation) is the complex of determinations of the individual undetermined in the species, §. 148.",[]],"baum:152":["Singulars are internally thoroughly determined, §. 148, hence actual, §. 54.",[]],"baum:153":["Since in the inferior there is the superior, §. 149, 148, in the individual there are species, lowest genus, subalterns, highest, in the species genera, in the lowest genus and subaltern superiors, in all inferiors the highest, §. 150.",[]],"baum:154":["The determinations of the highest genus are in its inferiors, §. 148, i.e. in subaltern genera, the lowest, species and individuals; the determinations of a subaltern genus are in its inferior genera, species, and individuals; the determinations of the lowest genus are in species and individuals; the determinations of a species are in individuals, §. 153, 35, i.e. the determinations of the superior are in its inferior, whether they were positive or negative, §. 36. This proposition is called the *dictum de O. et N.*",[]],"baum:155":["One altogether the same with many taken together is a whole (*totum*)@@1@@, and many taken together altogether the same with the whole are its parts (*partes*)@@2@@. Those which are to be taken together with a given part, so that it be altogether the same with the whole, are its co-parts, or complements to the whole (*complementa ad totum*)@@3@@ (supplements).",[]],"baum:156":["Any part taken together with its complements to the whole is an actual part (*pars actualis*)@@1@@, taken together with others a potential part (*pars potentialis*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:157":["The whole is altogether the same with its actual parts, §. 155, 156, hence also equal, and similar and congruent, §. 70.",[]],"baum:158":["A being whose essence is a part of another is an incomplete being (*ens incompletum*)@@1@@, one whose essence is not a part of another is a complete being (*ens completum*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:159":["The multitude (*multitudo*) of parts is continuous magnitude (*magnitudo*)@@1@@ (absolute, cf. §. 161) or quantity (*quantitas*), §. 75. The multitude of wholes is discrete number (*numerus*)@@2@@ (absolute, cf. 161) or quantity, §. 75. If the wholes, of which there is a number, are regarded again as parts, the number is a fraction (*fractus*)@@3@@ (*fractio*, *minutia*), if not, the number is an integer (*integer*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:160":["That whose part is equal to the whole is greater (*maius*) >@@1@@, a whole equal to a part is less (*minus*) <@@2@@.",[]],"baum:161":["The minimum (*minimum*)@@1@@ is greater than nothing alone, or, than which a lesser is impossible, the maximum (*maximum*)@@2@@ is less than nothing alone, or, than which a greater is impossible. A greater multitude is a comparative multitude (*multitudo comparativa*)@@3@@, cf. §. 74, a lesser, paucity (*paucitas*)@@4@@. A greater magnitude is a comparative magnitude (*magnitudo comparativa*)@@5@@, cf. §. 159, a lesser, smallness (*parvitas*)@@6@@. A greater number is comparative, cf. §. 159, a lesser, rarity (*raritas*)°°). °°) Seltenheit.",[]],"baum:162":["To be changed into less is to be diminished (*minui*)@@1@@, to be changed into greater to be increased (*augeri*)@@2@@. Whatever therefore can be increased or diminished is mutable, §. 125, and indeed as regards quantity, §. 160, 70, hence internally, §. 126, 69.",[]],"baum:163":["The essences of things, essentials and attributes, all internal determinations of a necessary being, unity in beings, truth, and perfection, the transcendentals (*transcendentales*), cannot be increased or diminished intrinsically, §. 162, 132.",[]],"baum:164":["An internal determination of a being which can be increased or diminished is a mode, §. 163, 52, and that in which such an internal determination occurs, which in it itself can be increased or diminished, is a contingent being, §. 111.",[]],"baum:165":["Minimal possibility is the non-repugnance of the fewest minima, §. 161, 8. Therefore the more things, the greater things, are compossible, the greater is the possibility, §. 160, until it is maximal, where the most maxima are compossible, §. 161. Hence every hypothetical possibility of a being is greater than its intrinsic possibility, §. 16, 15.",[]],"baum:166":["A minimal ground (*ratio*) is one which has a single minimal grounded, §. 161. Therefore the more, the greater grounded things it has, the greater it is, §. 160, until the maximal one comes about, having the most maximal grounded things, §. 161. The magnitude of a ground from the number of the grounded is fecundity (*fecunditas*)@@1@@, from their magnitude weight (*pondus*)@@2@@ (gravity, dignity, nobility).",[]],"baum:167":["A minimal nexus (*nexus*) is that of a single minimal ground, §. 166, 14. Therefore the more, the greater the grounds, the greater the nexus, §. 160, until it comes about maximal, of the most maximal grounds, §. 161, i.e. the most fecund and weightiest, §. 166. A greater nexus is harmony (*harmonia*).",[]],"baum:168":["Minimal hypothetical possibility (*possibilitas hypothetica*) is that in which a single minimal being enjoys a minimal nexus, §. 16, 161. Therefore the more things, the greater things, and in the greater nexus they are possible, the greater is the hypothetical possibility, §. 160, until it is maximal, where the most maxima are possible in the maximal nexus, §. 161, i.e. the most fecund, weightiest grounded of the most fecund and weightiest grounds, §. 167.",[]],"baum:169":["The sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) is the most fecund of grounds, §. 166, 21, yet among sufficient ones the smallest is that which suffices for a single smallest grounded (*rationatum*), §. 166, 161. The more, therefore, and the greater the grounded things for which it suffices, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is greatest, sufficient for the most and greatest grounded things, §. 161, and the same most fecund and most noble, §. 166.",[]],"baum:170":["A farther ground (*ratio ulterior*) is greater than a nearer one, §. 166, 27, hence what is sufficient simply (*simpliciter*) as such is greater than all that are sufficient in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), §. 169, 28.",[]],"baum:171":["The essence (*essentia*) is smallest when it is the sum total of the fewest smallest essentials, §. 40, 161. The more, and the greater, the essentials are, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is greatest, the sum total of the most and greatest essentials, §. 161.",[]],"baum:172":["The universal nexus (*nexus universalis*) is smallest where the singulars are either smallest grounds or their grounded things, §. 48, 167. The more, therefore, the singulars become either greater grounds, or grounded things of greater grounds, or both, the greater the universal nexus is, §. 160, until it is greatest, where the singulars are greatest grounds, §. 161, i.e. most fecund and most weighty, §. 166.",[]],"baum:173":["Unity (*unitas*) is smallest if the fewest smallest determinations (*determinationes*) of a single smallest thing are inseparable, §. 73, 161. The more, therefore, and the greater the determinations, and the more and the greater the things of which they are inseparable, the greater the unity is, §. 160, until it is greatest, where the most and greatest determinations are inseparable from the most and greatest things, §. 161. Uniqueness (*unicitas*) is smallest where one smallest thing differs from the fewest smallest things by a single smallest difference, §. 77, 161. The greater, therefore, the thing, and the more and the greater the things from which, and the more and the greater the differences by which, it is distinguished, the greater the uniqueness is, §. 160, until it is greatest, where the greatest thing is distinguished from all, even the greatest in its own genus, by the most and greatest differences, §. 161.",[]],"baum:174":["Identity (*identitas*) is smallest if a single smallest determination is common to the fewest smallest things, §. 38, 161. Hence the more, and the greater the determinations, and the more and the greater the things to which they are common, the greater the identity is, §. 160, until it is greatest, the community of the most and greatest determinations in the most and greatest things, §. 161.",[]],"baum:175":["Order (*ordo*) is smallest where the identity in the combination is smallest, §. 78, 161. Therefore the greater the identity of the combination is, the greater the order becomes, §. 160, 174, until it is greatest, where the identity of the combination is greatest, §. 161, i.e. where the most and greatest things are combined in the same manner as often and as far as they can be, §. 174. Unification (*unitio*) is the least necessary combination of the fewest smallest things, §. 79, 113, 161. The more things, therefore, are combined, the greater the unification is extensively, and the greater the things, and the greater the necessity with which they are combined, the greater it is intensively, until the combination of the most things is necessary extensively, or of only two or three, but of the greatest and with the greatest necessity that can be among them, the unification is greatest intensively.",[]],"baum:176":["Since the cognizability (*cognoscibilitas*) of a determination from a ground is its conformity (*conformitas*) with the ground@@1@@, §. 80, this conformity will be smallest if the determination is cognizable only from the smallest ground, §. 161, 166, in the smallest possibility of cognition, §. 165. The greater the ground from which the determination is to be cognized, and the more possible this cognition, the greater the conformity of the determination with the ground, §. 160, until it is greatest, most cognizable from the greatest ground, §. 161.",[]],"baum:177":["A determination conformable to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier ground, §. 166, 169, hence to a farther one and one simply such, §. 170, has a greater conformity than one conformable only to an insufficient, less fecund and less weighty ground, §. 166, 169, a nearer one or one sufficient in a certain respect, §. 170, §. 176.",[]],"baum:178":["The smallest defect (*defectus*) is that opposed to a determination of smallest conformity with the ground, §. 82, 161, hence it is the greater, the greater the conformity of its opposite, §. 160, 176, until it is greatest, opposed to a determination in which there is greatest conformity, §. 176, 161.",[]],"baum:179":["A defect contrary to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier, farther ground and one simply such is greater than one contrary to an insufficient, less fecund, less weighty, nearer ground and one sufficient only in a certain respect, §. 178, 177.",[]],"baum:180":["The magnitude of the conformity with the ground in the determination which a law (*lex*) enunciates is the strength (*robur*) of the law@@1@@. A law enunciating a determination of comparatively great conformity with the ground is strong@@2@@, one enunciating a determination of small conformity is weak@@3@@. Hence a law is most weak, or of smallest strength, which enunciates a determination of smallest conformity with the ground, §. 161, 176. The greater the conformity with the ground of the determination enunciated by the law, the stronger the law is, §. 160, until it is strongest when enunciating a determination in which there is greatest conformity, §. 161.",[]],"baum:181":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a sufficient, more fecund, weightier, farther ground, of which the nearer one to be compared is the grounded thing, or to which it is subordinated, and to one simply such, is stronger than a weaker law enunciating a determination conformable only to an insufficient, less fecund, less weighty, nearer ground, which is subordinated to that with which it is compared, a farther one, and one sufficient in a certain respect, §. 180, 177.",[]],"baum:182":["A law enunciating a determination conformable to a sufficient, farther ground is called higher@@1@@, one enunciating a determination conformable to a nearer ground is called lower@@2@@. Therefore higher laws are stronger than lower ones, and the highest law is strongest, §. 181.",[]],"baum:183":["The greatest order has the most common rules, §. 175, 86, hence it is most composite, §. 88, and thus a simple order, however great it may be, is nevertheless not greatest, §. 175, 88.",[]],"baum:184":["Metaphysical truth (*veritas metaphysica*) is smallest when it is the smallest order of many things in one, §. 175, 89, or the smallest conformity with universal principles, §. 176, 92. The more things, therefore, in a being, and the greater, and according to the more and stronger rules they are combined, the greater the truth in it is, §. 175, 180, until it is greatest, where the most and greatest things are found most agreeable with the strongest rules (of the universal principles, §. 182), §. 160, 161.",[]],"baum:185":["Perfection (*perfectio*) is smallest when it is a single smallest agreement (*consensus*) of the fewest smallest things toward one smallest thing, §. 94, 161, hence the more, and the greater the things, and the more and the greater the things in which, and the more often and the more they agree, the greater the perfection is, §. 160, until it is greatest, the greatest agreement of the most and greatest things toward one, §. 161, 169. But since the highest perfection is thus most composite, §. 183, 96, a simple perfection, however great it may be, is nevertheless not greatest, §. 96.",[]],"baum:186":["The exception (*exceptio*) is smallest when made from a most weak rule of perfection on account of a strongest one, §. 178, 97. Therefore the stronger the rule of perfection from which, and the weaker the one on account of which, an exception is made, the greater the exception is, §. 160, until it is greatest when made from the strongest, hence the highest rule of perfection, §. 182, on account of a most weak law, §. 161. If a sufficient and an insufficient ground of perfection collide, the exception from the sufficient one is greater, from the insufficient one smaller. If a more fecund and a less fecund collide, the exception from the more fecund is greater, from the less fecund smaller. If a weightier and a less weighty ground of perfection collide, the exception from the weightier is greater, from the less weighty smaller. If a farther one and a nearer one to be subordinated to it collide, the exception from the farther is greater, from the nearer smaller. If a ground sufficient simply and one such in a certain respect collide, the exception from the one simply such is greater, from the one such in a certain respect smaller, §. 181. If a higher and a lower rule of perfection collide, the exception from the higher is greater, from the lower smaller, §. 182. Hence an exception contrary to the law of the highest perfection, i.e. one that is made from the highest rule of perfection on account of the smallest, i.e. one conformable to the smallest, is greatest, §. 187, 182.",[]],"baum:187":["The good (*bonum*) is smallest when, once it is posited, the smallest perfection is posited, §. 100, 161. The greater the perfection to be posited once the good is posited, the greater the good is, §. 160, until it is the best@@1@@ (*optimum*), when, once it is posited, the highest perfection is posited, §. 161.",[]],"baum:188":["That is least contingent (*contingens*) whose opposite is least possible, §. 104, 161. The greater, therefore, the possibility of one of two opposites, the greater the contingency of the other, §. 160. The greatest contingency is of that whose opposite has the greatest possibility, §. 161.",[]],"baum:189":["The greater the separability of the determinations of one thing hypothetically, the greater the contingency of this unity, §. 115, 188. The more both non-combination and confusion are possible, the more contingent the opposite order is, §. 117, 188. The more non-combination or confusion of certain various things of a being is possible, the more contingent its opposite truth is, §. 119, 188. The more imperfection is possible, the more contingent the opposite perfection is, §. 122, 188.",[]],"baum:190":["The smallest change (*mutatio*) is a succession of a single smallest thing in a single smallest thing, §. 161, 125. Therefore the more, and the greater the things, in the more and the greater things in which they succeed, the greater the change is, §. 160, until it is greatest, a succession of the most and greatest things in the more and greatest things. The smallest possibility of the smallest change in a being is its smallest mutability, §. 161, 127. Therefore the more possible, and the greater the change in a being, the greater its mutability is, until it is greatest, the greatest possibility of the greatest change, §. 161. Reality (*realitas*) in a being is smallest when the determinations that are truly positive in it are extremely few and small, §. 135, 161. The more of these, and the greater ones, it has, the more real it is. The most real (*realissimum*), therefore, is that in which there are the greatest and most realities, §. 161, 36. These, absolutely necessary, are the highest good, or the metaphysical best@@1@@, §. 137, 147, and, contingent in themselves, the highest contingent good@@2@@ (the physical, broadly speaking).",[]],"baum:191":["A being either cannot exist except as a determination of another (in another), or it can, §. 10. The former is an accident (*accidens*)@@1@@ (predicamental or physical, cf. §. 50, whose being is inhering, συμβεβηκοσ), the latter is a substance (*substantia*)@@2@@ (a being subsisting through itself, form, ἐντελεχεια οὐσια ὑποστασισ ἐνεργεια), which can exist even if it is not in another, even if it is not a determination of another.",[]],"baum:192":["The existence of an accident, as such, is inherence (*inhaerentia*)@@1@@, the existence of a substance, as such, is subsistence (*subsistentia*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:193":["If accidents appear to be subsisting through themselves, they are substantiated phenomena (*phaenomena substantiata*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:194":["Accidents (*accidentia*) cannot exist except in others. Now there are no things different from accidents except substances, §. 191. Therefore accidents cannot exist except in substances, or do not exist outside their substances, §. 58.",[]],"baum:195":["The essentials (*essentialia*), attributes (*attributa*), modes (*modi*), relations (*relationes*), as accidents, §. 191, 52, cannot exist except in substances, §. 194.",[]],"baum:196":["That in a substance in which accidents can inhere, or the substance insofar as it is the subject (cf. §. 344), that in which accidents can inhere, is called the substantial (*substantiale*), nor do accidents exist outside the substantial, §. 194.",[]],"baum:197":["If accidents inhere in a substance, there is something that is the ground (*ratio*) of the inherence, §. 20, or force (*vis*) broadly speaking@@1@@ (efficacy, energy, activity, cf. §. 216), and sufficient, §. 22. This is force (more strictly speaking@@2@@, and for the sake of brevity sometimes simply).",[]],"baum:198":["Force more strictly speaking is either a substance or an accident, §. 191. Now it is not an accident, since it is the sufficient ground of all of these, §. 197. Therefore it is a substance, and insofar as accidents can inhere in it, as in a subject, the substantial, §. 196.",[]],"baum:199":["Every substance is by the substantial, §. 191, 196, hence endowed with force, §. 198, is the substantial, §. 196, 191, hence force both broadly and more strictly speaking, §. 198, 197.",[]],"baum:200":["If substances seem accidents, let them be predicated substances; singular ones are supposita (*supposita*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:201":["Force is attributed to substantiated phenomena (*phaenomena substantiata*), §. 199, 193, and they are also forces broadly speaking, or endowed with forces broadly speaking, §. 197, 23. But if force more strictly speaking is attributed to accidents, they are substantiated phenomena, §. 198, 193.",[]],"baum:202":["Every substance has essentials and attributes absolutely necessarily, §. 107, hence every substance has accidents, §. 191, 195. But it either has modes or does not have them, §. 10. What has modes is a contingent being (*ens contingens*), what does not have them, a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 111. Therefore a substance is either necessary or contingent. The subsistence of a contingent substance is a mode, §. 134, 192.",[]],"baum:203":["The minimal force (*vis minima*) is the ground (*ratio*) of only a single minimal inhering accident, §. 197, 166. Therefore the more numerous, the greater the inhering accidents of which it is the ground, the greater it is, until it is the maximum, of the most numerous greatest inhering accidents, §. 197, 166.",[]],"baum:204":["The science (*scientia*) of forces is dynamics (*dynamica*), both philosophical and mathematical, dynameometry (*dynameometria*).",[]],"baum:205":["A contingent suppositum (*suppositum contingens*) is determined, as to modes and relations, §. 184, 200. Hence fixed things coexist in it, or intrinsically immutable things, §. 107, 132, with mutable ones, §. 133. Coexistence of this kind is a state@@1@@ (*status*). Therefore a contingent suppositum has a state. The state of things united is union@@2@@ (*unio*).",[]],"baum:206":["The coexistence of modes with the fixed is an internal state@@1@@ (*status internus*), hence a contingent suppositum has an internal state, §. 205.",[]],"baum:207":["The relations of a substance are intrinsically mutable in it, §. 133. Therefore, coexisting with the fixed, they give a state, §. 205, which is called external@@1@@ (*externus*).",[]],"baum:208":["With a mode changed, the internal state is changed, §. 125, 200; with a relation changed, the external state is changed, §. 125, 207. Now modes and relations are mutable, §. 133. 207. Therefore in a contingent substance changes of state are possible. §. 206, 207.",[]],"baum:209":["The change of a mode is modification@@1@@ (*modificatio*). Therefore modification is a change of the internal state, §. 208, and a contingent substance is modifiable, §. 128. The change of a relation is variation@@2@@ (*variatio*).",[]],"baum:210":["Changes of state are accidents, §. 191, hence cannot exist except in substances, §. 194, and indeed with force posited, even more strictly speaking, §. 197, 22. That force, the sufficient ground, §. 197, of a change, or in general of an inhering accident, is either the substantial that is changed, or in general that in which the accident inheres, or a force different from it, §. 10, 38. If the former is the case, the substance whose state is changed, or in general in which the accident inheres, acts@@1@@ (*agit*); if the latter is the case, the substance whose state is changed, or in general in which the accident inheres, suffers (*patitur*). Hence action@@2@@ (*actio*) (act, operation) is a change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance, through its own force: passion@@3@@ (*passio*) a change of state, and in general the actuation of an accident in a substance, through an alien force.",[]],"baum:211":["A substance acting on a substance outside itself flows into it, so that influx@@1@@ (*influxus*) (transient action) is the action of a substance on a substance outside itself. Action that is not influent is immanent (*immanens*).",[]],"baum:212":["If the passion of that substance into which another flows is at the same time the action of the one suffering itself, the passion and influx are called ideal (*ideales*). But if the passion is not the action of the one suffering, the passion and influx are called real (*reales*).",[]],"baum:213":["The action of the one suffering on the agent is reaction@@1@@ (*reactio*) and the mutual action and reaction of substances is conflict@@2@@ (*conflictus*).",[]],"baum:214":["Action and passion and reaction are minimal through which no more than a single minimal accident is actuated, §. 161, 210. Therefore the more numerous, the greater the accidents that are actuated, the greater the action, passion, reaction, §. 160, 213, until they are maximal, where the most numerous greatest accidents are actuated, §. 161, 210.",[]],"baum:215":["Action and passion is called simple@@1@@ (*simplex*) which is not a whole of others, composite@@2@@ (*composita*) which is; and the more partial ones it consists of, the more composite it is, §. 160. Hence the maximal action and passion is maximally composite, §. 214, 161.",[]],"baum:216":["Every existing substance acts, §. 210, 199, hence has the possibility of acting or faculty@@1@@ (*facultas*) (active potency, force, cf. §. 197), §. 57; if it suffers, it has the possibility of suffering, i.e. (passive potency, capacity) receptivity@@2@@ (*receptivitas*). §. 57.",[]],"baum:217":["The faculty and receptivity of real influxes are real, of ideal ones ideal, of simple actions simple@@1@@, of composite ones composite@@2@@.",[]],"baum:218":["Faculties and receptivities are either absolute, §. 216, 15, or hypothetical, §. 16. These indeed are always greater than those, §. 105, 216.",[]],"baum:219":["The minimal hypothetical faculty would be that by which only a single action in a minimal nexus of substance would be possible, §. 216, 161. The more numerous, the greater the actions, the greater the nexus in which the substances are possible, the greater the hypothetical faculty, §. 168, 160. A greater hypothetical faculty is a habit@@1@@ (*habitus*) (readiness, dexterity).",[]],"baum:220":["With faculty and receptivity posited, since action or passion is not posited, §. 216, 259, yet is posited with force more strictly speaking posited, §. 210, 30, this will be the complement (*complementum*) of the faculty to the act, i.e. what is added to the faculty so that the action exists. Hence a given certain force more strictly speaking either suffices for a given certain action, or less, §. 21, 210; the former is called living@@1@@ (*viva*), the latter dead@@2@@ (*mortua*) solicitation (*sollicitatio*).",[]],"baum:221":["An impediment@@1@@ (*impedimentum*) (obstacle) is the opposite of the inherence of an accident, hence also the opposite of changes is an impediment, §. 210.",[]],"baum:222":["Resistance@@1@@ (*resistentia*) is an impediment of action. And since the opposite of the inherence of accidents and of changes is an accident, §. 191, 81, impediments and resistances have a sufficient ground in force, §. 197, 27. With an impediment posited, an impeding force is posited: with resistance posited, a resisting force is posited, §. 22.",[]],"baum:223":["A substance flowing more closely into a substance is present@@1@@ (*praesens*) to it, and substances most closely present to one another touch each other, are contiguous@@2@@ (*contigua*), so that presence@@3@@ (*praesentia*) is a closer influx, and immediate mutual presence, or immediate conflict, is contact@@4@@ (*contactus*). Insofar as something does not flow into another, nor suffers from it, more closely, it is said to be absent@@5@@ (*absens*) from it.",[]],"baum:224":["A composite being (*ens compositum*) (strictly and simply speaking)@@1@@ is a whole of parts outside parts, a non-composite being is simple (*simplex*) (simply and rigorously speaking)@@2@@. A composite being broadly speaking@@3@@ is whatever has parts, and the simple comparatively@@4@@ the less composite.",[]],"baum:225":["The parts of composites either singly and taken together are accidents, or some parts of composites are substances, §. 10, 191. If the former is the case, the composite being is an accident, §. 224, 155. The latter is a composite being more strictly speaking (real)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:226":["Posit that things placed outside one another can in no way constitute a whole, or be composed@@1@@: the composite will be intrinsically impossible, §. 224, 15. The mode of composition (*compositio*) therefore is the opposite of internal impossibility in composites, §. 81, i.e. the internal possibility and the essence of composites, §. 40.",[]],"baum:227":["Origin@@1@@ (*ortus*) is a change from non-existent into existent. A change from existent into non-existent is perishing@@2@@ (*interitus*). Hence the origin and perishing of a necessary being and a necessary substance is absolutely impossible, §. 132, 202.",[]],"baum:228":["Origin from nothing@@1@@ (*ortus ex nihilo*) is the origin of that none of whose part preexists it, and annihilation@@2@@ (*annihilatio*) the perishing of that none of whose part exists surviving. The origin from nothing and annihilation of a necessary being and substance is absolutely impossible, §. 227.",[]],"baum:229":["Origin and origin from nothing, perishing and annihilation cannot exist except in substances, §. 227, 228, because they are accidents, §. 210, but not in a necessary one, §. 227, 228. Therefore in contingent ones, §. 202.",[]],"baum:230":["A substance is either simple or composite, §. 224. The former is called a monad (*monas*) (atom, perfect unity).",[]],"baum:231":["Every part of a composite substance is either a substantial or an accident. Accidents are not outside the substantial, §. 196. Therefore the substantials of a composite substance are posited outside themselves, §. 124.",[]],"baum:232":["A composite substance has forces, hence substances posited outside themselves for parts, §. 231, 198, hence is a composite being more strictly speaking, §. 225.",[]],"baum:233":["A composite substance cannot exist except as a complex of other substances posited outside one another, §. 232, 155, and composed in a certain mode, §. 226. Therefore it cannot exist except as a determination (*determinatio*) of others, §. 36, 38. Therefore it is an accident, §. 191, and, if it seems to subsist through itself, and force is attributed to it, it is a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 193, 201.",[]],"baum:234":["Every substance is a monad, §. 233, 230, a composite being more strictly speaking is not a monad, §. 225. Therefore a substantiated phenomenon, §. 193, 201.",[]],"baum:235":["A composite more strictly speaking consists of monads, §. 225, 234.",[]],"baum:236":["A monad cannot originate except from nothing. For its parts are the substantial and the accidents inhering in it, §. 196. The substantial does not preexist the substance; for with it existing, force exists, hence substance, §. 198. None of the accidents preexists its substance, §. 194. Therefore no part of the monad about to originate preexists, hence it cannot originate except from nothing, §. 228.",[]],"baum:237":["With the substantial (*substantiale*) surviving, the substance (*substantia*) has not yet perished, §. 198, but no accident (*accidens*) survives its substance, §. 194. Therefore a monad (*monas*) cannot perish except through annihilation (*annihilatio*), §. 228, 196.",[]],"baum:238":["Conjoined things posited beside one another are simultaneous (*simultanea*)@@1@@, those posited after one another successive (*successiva*)@@2@@. A whole of simultaneous things is a simultaneous being (*ens simultaneum*), a whole of successive things a successive being (*ens successivum*).",[]],"baum:239":["The order of simultaneous things posited outside one another is space (*spatium*)@@1@@, that of successive things time (*tempus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:240":["With simultaneous things posited outside one another, space is posited. With space posited, simultaneous things are posited outside one another. With successive things posited, time is posited, and with time posited, diverse things are posited, §. 74, succeeding one another, §. 239, 78.",[]],"baum:241":["That in which there is space is extended (*extensum*)@@1@@, and is said to fill space or place (to be in space, or place, namely repletively). Now in every composite (*compositum*) strictly so called there is space, §. 240, 224. Therefore every composite strictly so called is extended and fills space. Everything extended has parts outside simultaneous parts, §. 240, hence it is a composite, §. 224.",[]],"baum:242":["A monad (*monas*) is not extended, nor does it fill space, §. 241, 230. But the whole of monads, §. 235, is extended, §. 241.",[]],"baum:243":["Every composite (*compositum*) has magnitude (*magnitudo*), §. 159, 224. The magnitude of a composite strictly and more strictly so called is quantitative (*quantitativa*)@@1@@. Hence a monad does not have quantitative magnitude, §. 230.",[]],"baum:244":["Physical division (*divisio physica*)@@1@@ is a diminution of quantitative magnitude. Just as the logical division of an individual is impossible, §. 148, so indivisible (*indivisibile*)@@2@@ is that whose physical division is impossible. Therefore indivisibility is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 17. Nothing absolutely indivisible is hypothetically divisible. Something absolutely divisible is hypothetically indivisible, §. 18. Hence a monad is indivisible, §. 243, and indeed through itself (*per se*), §. 15.",[]],"baum:245":["Composites (*composita*) cannot exist except as determinations (*determinationes*) of others, §. 225, 233. Now there are no others besides composites than simples, §. 224, 38. Therefore, with a composite existing, monads exist, §. 230, 233.",[]],"baum:246":["The quantity (*quantitas*) of a quality is a degree (*gradus*)@@1@@ (quantity of power, *quantitas virtutis*). Hence we can understand a degree only with another assumed, §. 69.",[]],"baum:247":["The lowest or least degree is that than which a smaller is impossible, the greatest is that than which a greater is impossible, §. 246, 161. Greater degrees are wholes of several least ones, §. 155, 160. Hence from a greater degree affirmed to a smaller one to be affirmed, and from a smaller degree denied to a greater degree to be denied, the consequence (*consequentia*) holds, §. 157. In any greater degree there is a multitude of degrees, which is called intension (*intensio*)@@1@@, §. 159. If this is increased, the quality whose degree it is is intensified (*intenditur*)@@2@@; if it is diminished, the quality whose degree it is is remitted (*remittitur*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:248":["To be a real being (*ens reale*) is a quality, §. 69, belonging to every being, §. 136. And since in every being there is a certain number of realities, §. 136, 159, every being has a certain degree of reality, §. 246, 159. Hence it will be either greatest or not greatest, §. 10, 247. And since the degree of reality than which a greater is possible, that is, the not greatest, §. 247, is called a bound (*limes*)@@1@@ (*terminus*, cf. §. 350; *finis*, cf. §. 341), but what has a bound is called finite (*finitum*)@@2@@ (cf. §. 341, limited), what does not have a bound will be infinite (*infinitum*)@@3@@ (real, unlimited). Therefore a being having the greatest degree of reality, or the most real (*realissimum*), §. 190, is infinite, all the rest finite. A finite whose bounds we either cannot determine or do not wish to is indefinite (*indefinitum*)@@4@@ (the imaginary infinite, mathematically such).",[]],"baum:249":["Finites have a bound, hence a degree, §. 248, therefore a quantity, §. 246, hence a philosophical and mathematical cognition of all finites is possible, §. 93, 22. The bound of a given finite, short of which more beyond in the same is impossible through its essence, is essential (*essentialis*)@@1@@. There is a mathesis of non-extended, of intensive things (*intensorum*)@@2@@, §. 247.",[]],"baum:250":["In every finite some reality must be removed, §. 248, 247. Therefore some negation must be posited, §. 135, 81, and thus imperfection (*imperfectio*), both essential and accidental, §. 143, hence absolutely necessary, §. 107, a negation, §. 36, strictly so called, §. 137, metaphysical evil (*malum metaphysicum*), §. 146.",[]],"baum:251":["The greatest degree of reality of the infinite is absolutely necessary, §. 248, 102, hence absolutely immutable, §. 130.",[]],"baum:252":["If an infinite being were internally mutable, the determinations that would succeed others would change the degree of reality, §. 246, 125. But this is internally immutable, §. 251. Therefore an infinite being is internally immutable.",[]],"baum:253":["What is internally immutable is, as regards internal determinations, actually whatever it can be, §. 125. Therefore an infinite being is, as regards internal determinations, actually whatever it can be, §. 252.",[]],"baum:254":["What is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be is a finite being, §. 253, 248.",[]],"baum:255":["What is internally mutable is a finite being, §. 252, 126. Therefore every contingent being is finite, §. 133, although it can be in many respects indefinite and mathematically infinite, §. 248.",[]],"baum:256":["An infinite being is a necessary being, §. 255, 109.",[]],"baum:257":["Of that which has a degree of reality than which a greater is possible, some quality of it, §. 248, hence an internal determination, can be intensified, §. 247, 69, hence changed, §. 247, 162. Therefore a finite being is internally mutable, §. 248, 126, hence is not a necessary being, §. 132, its existence is a mode, §. 134, and it itself is a contingent being, §. 109.",[]],"baum:258":["A necessary being is infinite, §. 257, 248.",[]],"baum:259":["That which is actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, is a necessary being, §. 132, therefore infinite, §. 258. Hence an infinite being can be defined through the being that actually is, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 253.",[]],"baum:260":["A contingent being is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 133. A finite being is a contingent being, §. 257. Therefore a finite being can be defined through the being that is not actually, as regards internal determinations, whatever it can be, §. 254. This is also clear from the fact that, of things contradictorily opposed, with one defined affirmatively, the other can be defined by the same marks, but negative ones, §. 81, 248.",[]],"baum:261":["Infinity (*infinitudo*) is a reality, §. 36, whose ground (*ratio*) is the greatest degree of reality, §. 248, 14; finitude or limitation is a negation, §. 36, whose ground is the bound, §. 248, 14.",[]],"baum:262":["The modes and relations of a being are either realities or negations, §. 36, hence with these changed in a finite being its bound is changed, §. 248. Now every change of a finite being is a change of mode or relation, §. 52, 132. Therefore every change of a finite being is a change of its bounds, hence also of limitation, §. 261, 30.",[]],"baum:263":["A finite being has modes, §. 257, 112. These are either realities or negations, §. 36. The opposite of each is absolutely possible in a finite being, §. 108. Hence privations (*privationes*) are absolutely possible in every finite being, §. 137. Hence also contingent imperfection and evil, §. 144, 146 (physical, broadly so called).",[]],"baum:264":["Every finite being is partly evil, partly good, §. 147, 137, and in every finite a contingent good and evil is intrinsically possible, §. 147, 263.",[]],"baum:265":["Least similarity (*similitudo*) is in two in which a single least quality is common, §. 174, 70. Now in all beings some qualities are common, §. 8-100. Therefore all beings are similar to one another in some degree, §. 246. Hence in beings there is some similarity and thus a universal identity, §. 70. The more, the greater the qualities, the more things they are common to, the greater the similarity, §. 174, 70. Least equality (*aequalitas*) is in two in which a single least quantity is common; therefore the more, in the more things, the greater the quantities that are common, the greater the equality, §. 174, 70.",[]],"baum:266":["Least congruence (*congruentia*) is least similarity and least equality; and the greater these are at once, the greater the congruence, §. 265, 70. The identity, similarity, equality, congruence of essentials are called essential (*essentiales*)@@1@@, of essentials and attributes necessary (*necessariae*)@@2@@, of modes contingent, of affections accidental (*accidentales*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:267":["Identity and diversity, as regards single predicates, is total (*totalis*)@@1@@, as regards some, partial (*partialis*)@@2@@. Therefore similarity, equality, congruence are either total or partial, §. 70.",[]],"baum:268":["Since there is a partial similarity of all beings, §. 265, beings are not totally diverse, §. 267. Let us call this proposition the principle of denied total dissimilarity and diversity.",[]],"baum:269":["Total identity of singulars is numerical (*numerica*). Two singulars outside one another entirely or totally the same are impossible. For when two are posited, many are posited, hence partly the same, partly diverse, §. 74. Therefore they are not totally the same, §. 267. Singulars that are totally the same are the same in number, and not partly the same, partly diverse, §. 267. Hence they are not many, nor two, §. 74. This proposition is called the principle of (identity of) indiscernibles (*principium indiscernibilium*) taken broadly, or of denied total identity.",[]],"baum:270":["Several actual things outside one another totally congruent are impossible. For if they existed outside one another, a proper existence of its own would belong to each, §. 86, diverse from the existence of the other, §. 38, hence either an attribute or a mode, at least a single one, §. 77, would be in the one that would not be in the other, §. 38, 55, and thus either a quality or a quantity, §. 69, nor would both hence be totally congruent, §. 70, 267. Things totally congruent, as regards all internal differences, are the same, §. 267, 70, hence the existence of one is not diverse from the existence of the other, §. 70, therefore they do not exist outside one another. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total congruence.",[]],"baum:271":["Several actual things (*actualia*) outside one another (*extra se invicem*) that are totally similar (*totaliter similia*) are impossible (*impossibilia*). For they would be either also totally equal (*totaliter aequalia*), or less so. If the former, they would be totally congruent (*totaliter congruentia*), §. 70, 267, q.a., §. 270. If they were not totally equal, there would be in one a magnitude (*quantitas*) that would not be in the other, §. 70, 38. This would have a sufficient ground, §. 22. Hence there would be in one of the totally similar things a quality (*qualitas*) that would not be in the other, §. 69, 14, q.a., §. 70, 267. This proposition is the principle (of identity) of indiscernibles (*indiscernibilium*) taken strictly, or of denied total similarity.",[]],"baum:272":["Several actual things outside one another that are totally equal are impossible. For they will be either at the same time totally similar, or only partially (*partialiter*), §. 265. If the former, they would be totally congruent, §. 70, 267, q.a., §. 270. If they were only partially similar, there would be a quality in one that would not be in the other, §. 267, 70, hence in both there would not be totally the same degree of reality, §. 248, and so a certain magnitude of the one would not be the magnitude of the other, §. 246, therefore neither would they be totally equal, §. 267, 70. Let this proposition be the principle of denied total equality.",[]],"baum:273":["All actual things outside one another are partially diverse (*diversa*), §. 268, incongruent (*discongruentia*), §. 270, 70, dissimilar (*dissimilia*), §. 271, unequal (*inaequalia*), §. 272.",[]],"baum:274":["The same things A and C with the same third thing B are the same among themselves. For what is in B is in A, §. 38. What is in C is in B, §. 38. Therefore what is in C is in A. Hence A and C are the same, §. 38.",[]],"baum:275":["Congruent, similar, equal things with the same third thing are congruent, similar, equal among themselves, §. 70, 274.",[]],"baum:276":["With the same, congruent, similar, equal sufficient ground posited, the same, congruent, similar, equal grounded thing (*rationatum*) is posited, and vice versa, §. 38, 70.",[]],"baum:277":["With the same essence posited, the same attributes are posited, and vice versa, §. 276, 50.",[]],"baum:278":["With a diverse, incongruent, dissimilar, unequal sufficient ground posited, a diverse, dissimilar, incongruent, unequal grounded thing is posited, and vice versa, hence with a diverse essence posited, diverse attributes are posited, and vice versa, §. 277, 276.",[]],"baum:279":["Identity (*identitas*), diversity, §. 38, congruence, incongruence, similarity, dissimilarity, equality, inequality, §. 70, of actual things outside one another are representable in none of them unless it is considered in nexus (*nexus*) with those that are posited outside it, §. 14, hence they are relations (*relationes*), §. 37. Those, therefore, between which it intervenes are connected, §. 14, 19. Now it intervenes between single actual things outside one another, §. 265-273. Therefore single actual things are connected, §. 47. Hence in all actual things nexus, indeed harmony (*harmonia*), is universal, §. 48, 167.",[]],"baum:280":["Since extension (*extensio*) is a quality of a composite being by which it fills space, §. 69, 241, and space can be greater and less, §. 239, 175, in the extended a degree of extension is possible, §. 246, i.e. figure (*figura*). Hence a monad (*monas*) does not admit, but a whole of monads admits figure, §. 242. A composite of greater extension is spacious@@1@@, of less, narrow@@2@@.",[]],"baum:281":["The position of a simultaneous thing outside other actual things is its place (*locus*)@@1@@, the position of a successive thing is age (*aetas*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:282":["The place of any simultaneous thing is determined from its conjunction with diverse actual things outside it, §. 85, 281. Hence it is diverse from the place of things coexisting, §. 38. Therefore simultaneous things posited outside one another are not in the same place, §. 281. The age of any successive thing is determined from its conjunction with actual things posited before and after it, §. 85, 78, hence it is diverse from their age, §. 38. Hence successive things cannot be in the same age with one another.",[]],"baum:283":["Change of place is motion (*motus*)@@1@@, hence all motion is change of relation, §. 281, 85, and would be least if the single position of a single least thing toward a single least actual thing outside it were changed, §. 161, hence it is the greater, the more, the of more, the of greater, positions toward the more, the greater things outside the movables are changed. Things that are not moved are at rest (*quiescunt*)@@2@@, and the absence of motion is rest (*quies*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:284":["Coordinates touching one another are contiguous (*contigua*)@@1@@, not contiguous they are distant (*distant*)@@2@@. The place of things distant among themselves is situation (*situs*)@@3@@. Action or immediate and proximate influence on a distant thing is impossible in and through itself, §. 223, 15.",[]],"baum:285":["A being simultaneous and successive of contiguous parts is continuous (*continuum*)@@1@@, but a being simultaneous and successive of parts distant among themselves is interrupted (*interruptum*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:286":["The parts of an extended thing posited outside one another are either simple or composite, §. 224. The former, insofar as they are not extended, §. 242, are called points (*puncta*). A continuous series of points interposed between distant points is a line (*linea*).",[]],"baum:287":["The extension of a line is determined from the number of points of which it consists, §. 241, 286, and the line in which the fewest are interposed between given distant points is this line the shortest among those@@1@@. The line shortest between given points is straight (*recta*)@@2@@, not straight, it is curved (*curva*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:288":["A straight line between distant things is their distance (*distantia*)@@1@@, and the greater this is, the more the distant things are distant from one another, §. 287, 284. But if the distance is greater, the distant things are more remote (*remotiora*)@@2@@, if less, nearer (*propiora*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:289":["A continuous series of lines interposed between distant lines is a surface (*superficies*)@@1@@. The extension of a surface is determined from the number of lines of which it consists, §. 241, 175, and that which is shortest between given distant lines is a plane surface (*superficies plana*)@@2@@, not plane, it is curved (*gibba*)@@3@@. A continuous series of surfaces interposed between distant surfaces is the mathematically solid (*mathematice solidum*)@@4@@ (body, cf. §. 296, mathematical).",[]],"baum:290":["The extension possible in a line alone is length (*longitudo*)@@1@@, what is added to length in a surface is breadth (*latitudo*)@@2@@, what is added to the extension of surfaces in mathematically solid things is (height) or depth (*altitudo, profunditas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:291":["If from a magnitude assumed as one we understand another homogeneous, or similar, we measure (*metimur*)@@3@@ this, the measured thing (*mensuratam*)@@1@@, by that measure (*mensura*)@@2@@, and this very action is called dimension (*dimensio*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:292":["A line admits only a single dimension of extension, §. 291, 287, a surface a double, §. 291, 289, the mathematically solid a triple, §. 280, 246.",[]],"baum:293":["Since the parts of space are in simultaneous things posited outside one another, §. 239, 155, they are simultaneous, and space is therefore a simultaneous being, §. 238.",[]],"baum:294":["Since motion is an accident (*accidens*), §. 283, it cannot exist except in substances, and indeed with a force posited, §. 210, which is called motive force (*vis motrix*)@@1@@. And since rest is an impediment of motion, §. 221, 283, with rest posited a force resisting motion is posited, §. 222, which is called force of inertia (*vis inertiae*)@@2@@ (antitypy, sluggishness, innate force).",[]],"baum:295":["The extended thing to which a force of inertia is attributed is matter (*materia*), cf. §. 344, and a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 234, 201. Matter to which this force alone is attributed is first (*prima*), cf. §. 423 (merely passive).",[]],"baum:296":["Matter to which motive force is attributed is a physical body (*corpus physicum*) (cf. §. 289, second matter, cf. §. 295) and a substantiated phenomenon, §. 295, 201.",[]],"baum:297":["The time simultaneous with the thought about it is the present (*praesens*)@@1@@, to which the present succeeds, the past (*praeteritum*)@@2@@, the successive of the present the future (*futurum*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:298":["The actual things of present time are existents (*exsistentia*)@@1@@ (present in time, beings in act). The actual things of past time, if they are not at the same time beings in act, or if they no longer exist, are past things (*praeterita*)@@2@@ (things of fact). The actual things of future time are future things (*futura*)@@3@@, and these, if they are not at the same time beings in act, are beings in potency (*entia in potentia*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:299":["The continuation of existence is duration (*duratio*)@@1@@. That in which duration is possible is called perdurable (*perdurabile*)@@2@@. But the actual thing that is not perdurable is instantaneous (*instantaneum*)@@3@@ (momentary). The perdurable of greater duration is lasting (*diuturnum*)@@4@@ (stable, constant, perpetual, eternal), of less it is brief (*breve*)@@5@@ (fleeting, transitory, inconstant).",[]],"baum:300":["The time simultaneous with a momentary thing is called an instant (*instans*) (moment)@@1@@. The successive of others is later (*posterius*)@@2@@, that of which other things are successive is earlier (*prius*)@@3@@, earlier than all, it is first (*primum*)@@4@@, later than all, last (*postremum*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:301":["The change of a being into the present is beginning (*initium*)@@1@@, into the past end (*finis*)@@2@@. Unition is the beginning of union, §. 79, 205, the beginning of contact is impact (*ictus*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:302":["Eternity (*aeternitas*)@@1@@ (strictly speaking, cf. §. 299) is duration without beginning and end. Duration without end only is aeviternity (*aeviternitas*)@@2@@, and that simultaneous with all time is sempiternity (*sempiternitas*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:303":["What continues existence without beginning is simultaneous with all past and present time, §. 301, 297, what will continue the same without end will be simultaneous with all future time, §. 301, 297. Therefore everything eternal is sempiternal, §. 302.",[]],"baum:304":["A being in potency either has the proximate sufficient ground of existence in beings in act, or less so, §. 27, 10. The former is a being in proximate potency (*ens in potentia proxima*)@@1@@, the latter a being in remote potency (*ens in potentia remota*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:305":["A being in act, while it lasts, cannot not last, §. 9, 7, hence it exists necessarily, §. 299, 101, or whatever is, while it is, is necessarily.",[]],"baum:306":["Things that determine place and age for one another mutually are connected, §. 281, 85. Hence simultaneous things are connected, by space, successive things, by time, §. 238, 239. Now single actual things posited outside one another are either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 298. Therefore between single actual things universal nexus and harmony intervenes, §. 48, 167.",[]],"baum:307":["That which contains the ground of another is its principle (*principium*)@@1@@. What is dependent on a principle is the principiated (*principiatum*)@@2@@. The principle of existence is a cause (*causa*)@@3@@, the principiated of a cause the caused (*causatum*)@@4@@. What cannot exist except as the caused of another posited outside itself is a being from another (*ens ab alio*)@@5@@ (dependent), what can also exist although it is not the caused of another posited outside itself is a being from itself (*ens a se*)@@6@@ (independent).",[]],"baum:308":["The existence (*exsistentia*) of a contingent (*contingens*), hence also finite (*finitum*), being (*ens*) is a mode (*modus*), §. 134, 257. Hence it is not sufficiently determined by the essence (*essentia*) of a contingent and finite being, §. 65. Therefore not by its attributes (*attributa*) either, §. 64, 25. Therefore in the internal determinations (*determinationes*) of a contingent and finite being there is not a sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of its existence, §. 52. But yet it is necessary for a contingent and finite being in order to exist, §. 22, 101. Therefore outside the contingent and finite being a sufficient ground of its existence, in order that it be, is necessary, than which those that contain it, since they are causes (*causae*), §. 307, a finite and contingent being cannot exist except as the caused (*causatum*) of something posited outside itself, i.e. is a being from another (*ens ab alio*), §. 307.",[]],"baum:309":["A being from itself (*ens a se*) is not contingent, nor finite, §. 308, 307, hence necessary (*necessarium*), §. 109, and infinite (*infinitum*), §. 258.",[]],"baum:310":["A necessary and infinite being, §. 258, can exist (*exsistere*), although it is not the caused (*causatum*) of another posited outside itself, §. 109, 102, therefore it is a being from itself (*ens a se*) and independent (*independens*), §. 307.",[]],"baum:311":["The principle of possibility (*principium possibilitatis*) is called the principle of being (*principium essendi*)@@1@@ (of composition), the cause (*causa*) the principle of becoming (*principium fiendi*)@@2@@ (of generation), the principle of cognition (*principium cognitionis*) the principle of cognizing (*principium cognoscendi*)@@3@@, and this, if neither more nor fewer things can be cognized from it than the given principiate (*principiatum*), is called adequate (*adaequatum*)@@4@@ to that principiate. Essence (*essentia*) is the principle of being and of cognizing of the modes (*modi*), §. 65, 50.",[]],"baum:312":["Of relata (*relata*), the principle is called the term of the relation (*terminus relationis*)@@1@@, the dependent (*dependens*) however the subject of the relation (*subiectum relationis*)@@2@@. And those of which one can become the term of the relation of the other are correlates (*correlata*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:313":["Between cause (*causa*) and caused (*causatum*) there is a nexus (*nexus*), §. 307, 14, which is called causal (*causalis*)@@1@@, and, insofar as it is attributed to the cause, causality (*causalitas*)@@2@@, insofar as to the caused, dependence (*dependentia*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:314":["Several causes (*causae*) of one and the same caused (*causatum*) are co-causes (*concausae*)@@1@@, and are said to concur (*concurrere*)@@2@@ to the caused. A cause that does not have a co-cause is solitary (*solitaria*)@@3@@. Of the co-causes, that which among the rest contains the greatest ground (*ratio*) of the caused is the principal (*principalis*) (primary, *primaria*)@@4@@. Co-causes of the principal cause are (less principal) secondary (*secundariae*)@@5@@. All co-causes are connected with one another, §. 313, 33.",[]],"baum:315":["Of co-causes (*concausae*), one either is the cause of another, or not, §. 10. In the former case the co-causes are subordinate (*subordinatae*)@@1@@, in the latter coordinate (*coordinatae*)@@2@@. Of all the subordinates, the first cause (*causa prima*)@@3@@, the rest are second (*secundae*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:316":["In subordinate co-causes (*concausae*) either in the nearer cause the same will depend on the more remote cause on which the caused (*causatum*) of the nearer depends, or something else, §. 315, 27. If the former, the co-causes are subordinated essentially (*essentialiter*)@@1@@, if the latter, accidentally (*accidentaliter*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:317":["The cause of a cause essentially subordinated to itself is also the cause of the caused (*causatum*), §. 316, 25.",[]],"baum:318":["A cause (*causa*) is either sufficient (*sufficiens*) or insufficient (*insufficiens*), §. 307, 21, and the latter either such in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), or simply (*simpliciter*), §. 28. A cause is either mediate (*mediata*), on which the given caused (*causatum*) depends through another, or immediate (*immediata*), on which the given caused, but not through another, depends, §. 27.",[]],"baum:319":["The cause (*causa*) of reality (*realitas*) through action (*actio*) is efficient (*efficiens*)@@1@@, of negation (*negatio*) however deficient (*deficiens*)@@2@@. Both the efficient and the deficient cause act, hence are substances (*substantiae*), §. 210, and so accidents (*accidentia*), regarded as efficient causes, are substantiated phenomena (*phaenomena substantiata*), §. 201, 200. The caused (*causata*) of an efficient or deficient cause are effects (*effectus*)@@3@@.",[]],"baum:320":["An efficient and deficient cause (*causa*) is either mediate (*mediata*), which actuates the effect (*effectus*) through another efficient or deficient cause, or immediate (*immediata*), which actuates the effect but not through another efficient or deficient cause. An efficient and deficient cause coordinated with another efficient and deficient one is called its partner (*socia*)@@1@@, and if the one or the other, or partner, was insufficient for a given effect, the other partner is called auxiliary (*auxiliaris*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:321":["Aid (*auxilium*)@@1@@ is the complement to the effect of an insufficient partner cause. To be an auxiliary cause is to help (*iuvare*)@@2@@. Therefore the helping one is not a solitary cause (*causa solitaria*), §. 320, and a solitary cause does not help, §. 314.",[]],"baum:322":["An efficient or deficient cause, less principal, subordinated to an efficient or deficient one, is instrumental (*instrumentalis*) (*administra*, and *ministerialis*).",[]],"baum:323":["A singular action (*actio*) with its effect is called an event (*eventus*)@@1@@. The relation of an event is a circumstance (*circumstantia*)@@2@@. The complex of relations concurring to an event is an occasion (*occasio*)@@3@@, and its cause is an occasional cause (*causa occasionalis*)@@4@@. The smallest consensus of very few, smallest circumstances to the given event is the smallest occasion, §. 161. The more, the greater the circumstances, the more they consent to it, the greater the occasion, §. 160, until there is the greatest consensus of the most numerous, greatest circumstances to the given event, §. 161. A greater occasion with respect to place is opportunity (*opportunitas*), and its opposite inopportunity (*inopportunitas*), with respect to time timeliness (*tempestivitas*), and its opposite untimeliness (*intempestivitas*)@@5@@.",[]],"baum:324":["If there is in event (*eventus*) A a smallest circumstance (*circumstantia*) that is not in event B, the events A and B are not totally the same, §. 267, 323, hence partially diverse, §. 267, i.e. the smallest circumstance varies the thing externally.",[]],"baum:325":["Let events (*eventus*) A and B be internally the same in some degree, and be regarded in the most diverse circumstances (*circumstantiae*); the diversity depending on this is not except in relations, §. 323, hence external, §. 37. Therefore the internal identity of events is neither increased nor diminished by the diversity of circumstances, of place, of age, §. 281, 85, e.g., §. 162, i.e. place and time do not vary the thing internally.",[]],"baum:326":["With the principle (*principium*) posited, the principiate (*principiatum*) is posited, §. 307, 30. With the cause (*causa*) posited, the caused (*causatum*) is posited, §. 311. With the efficient and deficient cause posited, the effect (*effectus*) is posited, §. 319, with the occasional cause posited, the occasion is posited, §. 323, and vice versa, §. 29.",[]],"baum:327":["With the same, similar, equal, congruent principle (*principium*) posited, hence causes (*causae*), §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, occasional, §. 323, the same, similar, equal, congruent principiates (*principiata*) are posited, §. 276, 307, caused (*causata*), effects (*effectus*), occasions, and vice versa.",[]],"baum:328":["With the principle (*principium*) removed, hence the cause (*causa*), §. 307, efficient, deficient, §. 319, and occasional, §. 223, the principiate (*principiatum*), the caused (*causatum*), the effect (*effectus*), the occasion is removed, §. 307, 31, and vice versa, §. 32.",[]],"baum:329":["Every effect (*effectus*) is similar to the deficient or efficient cause (*causa*), §. 265, i.e. such as the cause, such the effect, §. 70. The effect of an efficient cause is either similar, up to the specific difference of each, or not, §. 10. The former effect is called univocal (*univocus*)@@1@@, the latter equivocal (*aequivocus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:330":["An effect (*effectus*) having in a given action (*actio*) the proximate ground of existence (*ratio exsistentiae*) is its immediate effect (*effectus immediatus*)@@1@@ (proximate, and continuous, cf. §. 285). Mediate (*mediati*)@@2@@ (remote) are those that have only a more remote ground of their existence in the given action. The effects of an action, all taken in every intensity, are the full effect (*effectus plenus*)@@3@@. Less full (*minus plenus*)@@4@@ is either only a certain one, or one regarded in a lesser degree.",[]],"baum:331":["The full effect (*effectus plenus*) is as great as the action (*actio*) through which it is actuated, §. 330, 214, hence is equal to the action, §. 70. Now the action is as great as the living force (*vis viva*) through which it is actuated, §. 220, 166, hence the action is equal to the living forces by which it is actuated, §. 70. Therefore the action of an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) is equal to its living forces, §. 319. Hence the full effect is equal (proportioned) to the living forces of the efficient cause, §. 275.",[]],"baum:332":["The nobility or dignity of some effect (*effectus*) to be essentially subordinated to some efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) pertains to its full effect, §. 330, 317, 166. Therefore an effect is not more noble than its efficient cause to which it is essentially subordinated, §. 331, 160.",[]],"baum:333":["From the effect (*effectus*) the qualities, §. 329, and quantities, §. 331, hence the determinations (*determinationes*) of the cause (*causa*), can be cognized, §. 70, 67. Therefore the effect is the principle of cognizing (*principium cognoscendi*) the cause, §. 311, i.e. the effect bears witness of the cause.",[]],"baum:334":["Every contingent (*contingens*) and finite (*finitum*) being (*ens*) is a being from another (*ens ab alio*), §. 308. Therefore to the existent, existence (*exsistentia*) does not inhere by a force (*vis*) proper to itself, §. 307, hence an alien force posited outside the finite and contingent real is the sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of the existence inhering in the finite and contingent real being, §. 210. Therefore a substance (*substantia*) posited outside it acts upon it existence by influencing, §. 211. Hence every contingent and finite real being is an effect (*effectus*), §. 319, and has an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*), §. 326.",[]],"baum:335":["The causal nexus (*nexus causalis*) between the efficient cause, the deficient cause and the effect is called effective (*effectivus*)@@1@@. Hence partner causes are connected by effective nexus, §. 320, 314.",[]],"baum:336":["The useful (*utile*)@@1@@ is a good for another, a good not good for another is useless (*inutile*)@@2@@, an evil for another is harmful (*noxium*)@@3@@. Hence usefulness (*utilitas*)@@4@@ is respective goodness (*bonitas respectiva*), §. 37, which, if it is attributed to the thing to which another can be beneficial, can be called passive (*passiva*)@@5@@, if to that which can be beneficial, active (*activa*)@@6@@.",[]],"baum:337":["The smallest usefulness (*utilitas*) is that by which a single smallest thing posits in another single smallest thing a single smallest perfection (*perfectio*), §. 336, 161, hence it is increased, the more numerous, the greater things, in the more numerous, the greater things, the more numerous, the greater perfections they posit, §. 187. The degree of usefulness is called value (*valor*)@@1@@, and the judgment about value price (*pretium*)@@2@@ (estimation, *aestimatio*). Hence estimation with price is either true, or apparent or imaginary, §. 12.",[]],"baum:338":["Use (*usus*)@@1@@ is the actuation of usefulness. Abuse (*abusus*) is either apparent use (*usus apparens*)@@2@@, or use by which the useful perishes (*interit*)@@3@@. And since the perfection of that to which it is beneficial can be cognized from the useful, §. 336, 100, the useful and that to which it can be beneficial are connected, and their nexus can be called the nexus of usefulness (*nexus utilitatis*)@@4@@.",[]],"baum:339":["If someone uses the useful, the perfection of another is actuated through it, §. 338, 100, and the useful through use becomes the cause of the perfection of that to which it is beneficial, §. 307. And this causal nexus can be called the nexus of uses (*nexus usuum*)@@1@@. What someone uses is that useful thing, §. 336, 57, no one uses the wholly useless, §. 338, 58, yet some useful thing lacks use, §. 59, hence something that no one uses is nonetheless useful, §. 60.",[]],"baum:340":["Useful things for one and the same thing are connected with it, §. 339. Therefore all useful things for one are connected among themselves, §. 314. The useful A, essentially subordinated to the useful B, is useful also to that C to which B is beneficial, §. 317.",[]],"baum:341":["If someone uses or abuses something in order to actuate a good seen by himself: the good itself seen by the agent is called the end (*finis*)@@1@@, cf. §. 248, the causes (*causae*) that he can use or abuse for the end, means (*media*)@@2@@ (*destinata*, *finita*, cf. §. 248, *remedia*), and the representation of the end intention (*intentio*)@@3@@. Now the end is the principle of use or abuse, §. 338, 307, hence the final cause (*causa finalis*), §. 338, 307.",[]],"baum:342":["The reasons of the intention in the one intending are called impulsive causes (*causae impulsivae*)@@1@@. The end (*finis*) is the effect of the action and of the means, which the agent uses or abuses, §. 341, 319. Hence the full end is equal to the action and the means. The means and the action are equal (proportioned) to the full end, §. 331. The seizing of an occasion, §. 323, and the removal of impediments, §. 221, are means, §. 341.",[]],"baum:343":["The causal nexus (*nexus causalis*) of means and ends is final@@1@@; all co-ends are connected among themselves, §. 314, 341. The end of ends essentially subordinated to one another is also the end of the means, §. 317, 341. The first end, or that to which all co-ends are subordinated, is called the ultimate or goal (*scopus*)@@2@@, and this is either simply ultimate, or such in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) in a given subordination of ends, §. 28, the rest intermediate@@3@@.",[]],"baum:344":["If a being (*ens*) is conceived as determinable, it is matter (*materia*), cf. §. 295, 296, out of which (*ex qua*)@@1@@, in the very act of determination matter about which (*circa quam*) (object, subject of occupation)@@2@@, once the determination is made matter in which (*in qua*), and this together with the matter out of which is called the subject.",[]],"baum:345":["Since matter and form (*forma*) contain the ground (*ratio*) of actual determination, §. 344, 40, they are causes, §. 307. The former material, the latter formal. Material and formal concauses are connected among themselves, §. 314, and their nexus with their *principiata*, §. 307, and among themselves, §. 314, the former can be called subjective, the latter formal (essential).",[]],"baum:346":["The exemplar (*exemplar*)@@1@@ is that to which something similar is intended, and since it is an impulsive cause, §. 342, it is a cause, §. 307, which is called exemplary, and its *causatum* is called the exemplate@@2@@ (ectype, copy). An exemplar which has no other is the archetype@@3@@ (original). Exemplar and exemplate and co-exemplars are connected by causal nexus, §. 313, which nexus is exemplary (typical).",[]],"baum:347":["A means of cognizing the existence of another is a sign (*signum*)@@1@@, the end of the sign the signified (*signatum*)@@2@@. Hence a sign is the principle of cognizing (*principium cognoscendi*) of the signified, §. 311, and the nexus between sign and signified is significative@@3@@, and the significance attributed to the sign is called the signification@@4@@ (force, power).",[]],"baum:348":["An actual signified, §. 347, is either present, then the sign is called demonstrative@@1@@; or past, then the sign is called mnemonic@@2@@ (rememorative, μνημοσυνον); or future, §. 298, then the sign is called prognostic (*prognosticon*).",[]],"baum:349":["The science (*scientia*) of signs (semiotic, philosophical semiology, symbolic) is characteristic (*characteristica*)@@1@@, and it is I. heuristic@@2@@, concerning the discovery of signs both primitive@@3@@, which do not have signs for parts, and derivative@@4@@, which are composed of signs. If the mode of composition of these is more similar to the essence of the signified, they are essential@@5@@. The heuristic of derivative signs is combinatorial@@6@@, and II. hermeneutic@@7@@, concerning the cognizing of the signifieds of signs. This hermeneutic is universal@@8@@. The characteristic of prognostics is mantic@@9@@.",[]],"baum:350":["A sign of representation (*repraesentatio*) is a term (*terminus*) (cf. §. 248, symbol). Terms more usual through the human voice are words (*vocabula*)@@1@@. A series of words signifying connected representations is speech (broadly speaking)@@2@@. The complex of words more usual in a certain larger region is a particular language@@3@@. A language more usual in a certain smaller region, differing from itself as it is more usual in other smaller regions, is an idiom@@4@@. A more perfect idiom is a dialect. Terms, strokes to be observed by the eyes, are characters, strictly speaking@@5@@, cf. §. 67. These are either of words, nominal@@6@@, or of things to be signed immediate to words, real@@7@@, which if they signify another thing than that to which they are most similar, are hieroglyphic, the insignia of societies@@8@@, if they can be read in every particular language, their complex is a universal language@@9@@.",[]],"baum:351":["General cosmology (*cosmologia generalis*) is the science of the general predicates of the world, and it is either from experience more closely, empirical, or from the notion of the world, rational.",[]],"baum:352":["Cosmology, containing the first principles of psychology, theologies, physics, teleology, practical philosophy, is referred, §. 2, with reason, to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:353":["Cosmology teaches of the world 1) the notion, 2) the parts, 3) the perfection.",[]],"baum:354":["The world (*mundus*)@@1@@ (cf. §. 91, 403, 434, universe, παν) is a series (multitude, whole) of actual finite things, which is not a part of another.",[]],"baum:355":["This world exists. Therefore the world is in itself possible, §. 57, 18.",[]],"baum:356":["In this world there are actual things posited outside themselves, hence an actual universal nexus, §. 279, 306.",[]],"baum:357":["In every world there are actual parts, §. 354, 155, these singly are connected with the whole, §. 14, 157, hence singly connected with singles, §. 33. Therefore in every world there is a nexus of parts and universal harmony, §. 48, i.e. in the world there is no island. Otherwise: the parts of the world either are actual things posited outside one another, and are connected single with single, §. 279, 306, or are its internal determinations not posited outside one another, §. 10, 37, and are connected single with single, §. 49. Ουκ εστιν επεισοδιωδησ εστιν ωσπερ στρατευμα.",[]],"baum:358":["In this world there exists an effective nexus (realm of power), §. 335, of utility, §. 338, of uses, §. 339, a final (realm of wisdom), §. 343, a subjective and formal, §. 345, an exemplary, §. 346, a significative, §. 347. Hence nexuses of this kind are possible in the world, §. 57.",[]],"baum:359":["Since every world is a being, §. 355, 62, it will be one, §. 73 (§. 354, 155), true, §. 90 (§. 357, 355, 354, 92). Hence in every world there is order, §. 89, and common rules, §. 86. A fabulous world is not a world, §. 120.",[]],"baum:360":["Every world is perfect, §. 99, 359, and good. §. 100.",[]],"baum:361":["The single parts of every world are contingent beings, §. 354, 257. Hence their existence is a mode, §. 134. Now the existence of the single parts of the world taken together is the existence of the world, §. 155. Therefore the existence of the world is a mode, and every world is a contingent being, §. 111. Otherwise: Suppose some world to be a necessary being, all its internal determinations will be absolutely necessary, nor hence will any part of that world have modes, §. 108, 157, nor will it be a contingent being, §. 134, but every part of that world will be a necessary being, §. 109, hence infinite, §. 258, q.a., §. 354.",[]],"baum:362":["Every world is one, §. 359, yet it has modes, §. 361, 112, hence determinations in themselves separable, §. 72, 65, hence hypothetical unity, §. 76, and intrinsically contingent, §. 115.",[]],"baum:363":["The conjunction of the parts of no world is absolutely necessary, §. 362, 102, yet it is a coordination, §. 78. Therefore in every world there is an order contingent in itself, §. 117, hence also a truth intrinsically contingent, §. 119.",[]],"baum:364":["All real parts of the world, §. 136, since they agree toward the degree of reality to be attributed to the world by them, §. 248, 140, yet in such a way that in themselves they could also not agree, or not agree so much, as being contingent beings, §. 354, 257: in every world there is a perfection contingent in itself, §. 122.",[]],"baum:365":["Every world is absolutely and internally mutable, §. 361, 133.",[]],"baum:366":["In every world there are absolutely necessary realities, §. 359, 360, hence the metaphysical good, §. 147, and ones contingent in themselves, §. 362-364, hence the physical good broadly speaking, §. 147.",[]],"baum:367":["This world is one, §. 359, in itself contingently, §. 362. There is in this world order, §. 359, and truth intrinsically contingent, §. 363, there is also perfection and goodness intrinsically contingent, §. 364, the metaphysical and the physical good broadly speaking, §. 366, and this world is absolutely, is internally mutable, §. 365, 154.",[]],"baum:368":["The smallest world would be the whole of the very fewest smallest actual things, §. 161, 354, hence the more, the greater finite things it has for parts, the greater it becomes, §. 160, until it is the greatest, an aggregate of the very many greatest among finite things, §. 161.",[]],"baum:369":["The state of the world (*status mundi*)@@1@@ is the whole of all states simultaneous in its parts. Now this world has parts, in which fixed things coexist with mutable ones, §. 367. Therefore this world has a state, §. 205.",[]],"baum:370":["The existence of every world, and of this one, is a mode, §. 361, 134, hence it can be absent and present, the essence of the world remaining safe, §. 65. And since the actuality of every world, and of this one, is absolutely mutable, §. 133, the beginning and end of every world is absolutely possible, §. 301, 65.",[]],"baum:371":["A world cannot arise, except from nothing. For suppose some world to arise in such a way that nevertheless some part of it already pre-existed it: since, and as long as, this part of it existed, it was partly existing, as it always will be, if it existed, and not non-existing, hence after the pre-existing part it seems to arise but does not arise: §. 227, since such a world had not yet arisen, yet it was no longer merely possible, as long as it was a whole of an existing part, and therefore already, as to existence, determined, §. 54. Therefore a world not arising from nothing does not arise, §. 228. So too a world not annihilated does not perish, as, a part surviving, it remains partly existing, as it always was, if it existed, hence it is not changed into non-existing, §. 227. Therefore a world cannot perish, except through annihilation, §. 228.",[]],"baum:372":["Every world, and this one, is a finite being, §. 255, 361, hence its philosophical and mathematical cognition is possible, §. 249. Every world and this one has metaphysical evil, §. 250, and contingent, or physical broadly speaking, imperfection contingent in itself, absolutely possible, §. 263, partly good, partly evil, §. 264.",[]],"baum:373":["All worlds are partially similar both to the infinite being, and to one another, and to any of their parts, §. 265, 267, but impossible are several actual outside one another totally the same, §. 269, diverse, §. 268, congruent, §. 270, similar, §. 271, equal, §. 272.",[]],"baum:374":["Since the parts of the world are either simultaneous or successive, §. 238, 354, if they are posited outside one another, they will be connected in the world either through time, or through space, or through both, §. 239, 306.",[]],"baum:375":["Every world is a being from another (*ens ab alio*), §. 361, or dependent (*dependens*), §. 308, and this world has an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) posited outside itself, and is its effect (*effectus*), §. 334, testifying about the cause, §. 333.",[]],"baum:376":["The parts of every world are actual things (*actualia*), §. 354, hence each in its own world, and so the parts of this world have in this world truth (*veritas*), §. 90, and certainty (*certitudo*), §. 93, as internal determinations (*determinationes internae*), §. 37, 93, determinate (*determinatas*), §. 54.",[]],"baum:377":["Finite things (*finita*), which are possible not only absolutely, nor only hypothetically in any nexus whatever, but also in the universal nexus of some world, are called the possibles (*possibilia*)@@1@@ of that world. Hence the possibles of this world are those which, regarded in its universal nexus, are nevertheless hypothetically possible, hence have a greater degree of possibility, §. 165, 246.",[]],"baum:378":["If even a single part of this world were other than it is, this world would not be totally the same as it is, §. 155, 267. Now all parts of this universe can be other than they are, § 354, 260. Therefore worlds are possible, partly different from this one, §. 38, partly the same as it, §. 265, i.e. several, §. 74.",[]],"baum:379":["This world is uniquely singular, §. 77. For suppose there were several: they would constitute with this one a multitude or series, §. 74. Hence this one would not be a world, §. 354, or all those worlds would be parts of this one, only one, §. 354, 77.",[]],"baum:380":["A progression (regression) into the infinite (*progressus in infinitum*)@@1@@ would be a series of contingent beings posited outside one another, of which one is the cause of another in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), without a cause simply such. In this progression into the infinite, if the caused is taken as cause of itself, it would be curvilinear@@2@@ (circular): in which the caused is not taken as cause of itself, it would be rectilinear@@3@@.",[]],"baum:381":["A progression into the infinite, however great it is posited, would be a contingent being, §. 380, 155, hence would have an efficient cause (*causa efficiens*) posited outside itself, §. 334. This could not be a contingent being, for since that would again be a being from another, §. 308, it would not be the cause of the progression into the infinite, except such in a certain respect, §. 28. Therefore not posited outside the progression itself, but a part of it, §. 155, 380. Therefore the efficient cause of the progression into the infinite would have to be a necessary being, §. 109, and independent, §. 310. In whatever way this can exist, in that way it exists, §. 259. But it can exist, even if it is not the caused of another posited outside itself, §. 310. Therefore it is not the caused of another posited outside itself, and so the cause of its effects simply such, §. 28. Hence a progression into the infinite, which would have to be without a cause simply such, §. 380, and yet have it, is impossible, §. 7, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:382":["Fate (*fatum*)@@1@@ is the necessity of events in the world. Fate from the absolute necessity of the world would be Spinozistic (*spinosisticum*), a non-being, §. 361, 105, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:383":["An event in the world, whose sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) is unknown, is chance (*casus*)@@1@@. Chance, of which there is also no sufficient ground, would be pure@@2@@, impossible, §. 22, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:384":["An ordinary event (*eventus ordinarius*)@@1@@ is one whose existence is determined according to the rules of some certain order: an extraordinary one@@2@@ is a non-ordinary. Absolutely extraordinary@@3@@ would be one whose existence would be determined according to no rules of any order; relatively@@4@@ such is one that does not occur through the rules of a given certain order. An extraordinary one on account of confusion is disordered@@5@@.",[]],"baum:385":["The absolutely extraordinary would not be true, §. 384, 89, hence impossible, §. 90, 62, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58.",[]],"baum:386":["An event without any proximate sufficient ground would be an absolute leap (*saltus absolutus*)@@1@@. An event without an ordinary proximate sufficient ground is a respective leap (*saltus respectivus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:387":["What would exist without any proximate sufficient ground, §. 27, would exist through pure chance, §. 22, 383, hence an absolute leap is impossible, §. 386, 284, and is to be posited neither in this nor in any world, §. 354, 58. Every respective one also, if it is not something disordered, is nevertheless something extraordinary, §. 384.",[]],"baum:388":["The world is neither an infinite substance, §. 372, 248, nor its internal determination, §. 365, 252, hence neither the essence (*essentia*) of an infinite being, §. 40, nor an attribute (*attributum*), nor a mode (*modus*), §. 50, nor a modification (*modificatio*), §. 209. Hence every world is to be posited outside the infinite substance, and so this world also exists outside the infinite being, which is therefore called the extramundane being (*ens extramundanum*)@@1@@, the being actual outside this world.",[]],"baum:389":["Every world, hence also this one, is either a substance or an accident (*accidens*), §. 191. If this world should have been a substance, it subsists outside the infinite substance, §. 388, so that the infinite substance is therefore not the sole substance, §. 77.",[]],"baum:390":["If this world should have been an accident, it is nevertheless not an accident of the infinite substance, otherwise it would not be going to exist outside its substantial, §. 196, 388, nor can it exist except in a substance, or substances, §. 194. Therefore if this world is an accident, the infinite substance is not then the sole substance either, §. 77, 389. Now one or the other case is necessary, §. 191, 10. Therefore the infinite substance is not the sole substance.",[]],"baum:391":["Infinite force (*vis*) is not the sole force, §. 390, 198; in every world, and in this one, there are finite forces, §. 390, 388.",[]],"baum:392":["Every world is either a simple being or a composite one, §. 224; this one is a composite being. He who thinks this world to be a simple being is an egoist (*egoista*).",[]],"baum:393":["The parts of a composite world, singly and taken together, cannot be accidents. For since such a world could not exist except in substances, §. 155, 191, and yet could not inhere in an infinite one, §. 388, nor in a single finite one, §. 224, 194, it would require several finite substances in which it would inhere, §. 77. But these would constitute a series of actual finite things, which, if it were not a part of the world composed of mere accidents, would nevertheless constitute with it a series of actual finite things greater than itself, §. 160, hence it itself would not be a world, §. 354. If that series which finite substances would constitute were a part of that world, it would not be composed of mere accidents, §. 155. Hence a world composed of mere accidents is impossible, §. 61, 62. Every composite world is a composite taken more strictly (*compositum strictius dictum*), §. 225. Every world is either a substance, §. 389, 392, or a composite taken more strictly.",[]],"baum:394":["The parts of a composite world are either substantial or accidental, §. 393, and those indeed monads (*monades*), §. 235. Hence every composite world, and this one, consists of monads, §. 392. Whence it is again evident that no world can arise except from nothing, §. 236, nor perish except through annihilation, §. 237, 393.",[]],"baum:395":["This world, a composite taken more strictly, §. 393, consists of monads, nor is there in it anything substantial besides monads, §. 394. He who denies the existence of monads is a universal materialist (*materialista universalis*). He who denies the existence of the monads of the parts of the universe, e.g. of this one, is a cosmological materialist (*materialista cosmologicus*) (cf. §. 757).",[]],"baum:396":["The monads of every composite world, hence also of this one, are possibles (*possibilia*), §. 8, rational (*rationalia*), §. 24, beings (*entia*), §. 63, one (*una*), §. 73, true (*vera*), §. 90, objectively certain (*obiective certa*), §. 93, perfect (*perfecta*), §. 99, good (*bona*), §. 100, contingent beings (*entia contingentia*), §. 257, mutable (*mutabilia*), §. 133, real (*realia*), §. 136, universally connected (*universaliter connexa*), §. 357, are endowed with force, indeed are forces, also taken more strictly, §. 199, have an internal state, §. 206, and an external one, §. 207, modifiable, §. 209, not extended, nor singly filling space, but aggregated, §. 242, nor do they have quantitative magnitude, §. 243, indivisible, §. 244, finite, §. 354, hence have a certain limit of forces, §. 249, metaphysical evil, §. 250, partially similar to themselves, §. 265, 268, but partially also as dissimilar as unequal, §. 273, singly not admitting figure, yet so that the whole of them admits figure, §. 280.",[]],"baum:397":["The monads of every composite world, hence also of this one, since they are actual things posited outside one another, §. 354, 224, will be either simultaneous beings, or successive, or both, §. 238, hence singly have a position (*situs*), §. 148, either in simultaneous things a place (*locus*), or in successive things an age (*aetas*), or both, §. 281, although singly they do not fill a place, §. 396.",[]],"baum:398":["Of the monads of every finite universe, hence of this one, §. 354, each, if it is posited as subsisting outside the rest, being about to subsist, §. 192, cannot be with any other totally in the same place, §. 282. A substance in whose place no other posited outside it can be is impenetrable (*impenetrabilis*)@@1@@ (solid). Therefore every substance, hence also monads, both of this and of every composite world, are impenetrable, §. 230.",[]],"baum:399":["The monads of this and of every composite, hence extended, world, §. 241, 393, are points, §. 286, but by no means mathematical ones, in which nothing besides non-extension is posited, §. 396-398, nor, posited beside one another, do they coincide or coincide, §. 70, 396, but with several of them posited as coexisting, because each is impenetrable, §. 398, simultaneous things are posited outside one another in a certain order, §. 396, 78, hence in their aggregate there is space, §. 239. Therefore every aggregate of monads of this and of every composite world is extended, §. 241. A mathematical point, an abstract possible, §. 149, if it is imagined to exist, is a Zenonic point, a fictitious being (*ens fictum*), §. 62. If you should call a physical point the actual and, besides simplicity, determinate in every way, certain monads of this universe are physical points, namely those of whose aggregate the extended.",[]],"baum:400":["All monads (*monades*) of a composite world (*mundus*), hence also of this one, are in a universal nexus (*nexus*), §. 357, hence singly they are either grounds (*rationes*) or grounded (*rationata*) of singles, or both, §. 14, 48. From the grounded the ground can be cognized, §. 29. Therefore from any monad of every composite, hence also of this world, the single parts of the world to which it belongs can be cognized, §. 14, i.e. the single monads of every composite world, hence also of this one, are forces (*vires*), §. 199, representative (*repraesentativae*) of their universe (they are its active mirrors, §. 210, indivisible, §. 244, microcosms, worlds in compendium, and concentrations of their world, or they have force, are endowed with a representative force of their universe).",[]],"baum:401":["Monads representing their world, §. 400, either represent it to themselves conscious of their perception (*perceptio*), at least in part, or less so, §. 10. Hence the monads of this universe also represent this world either only obscurely, or clearly, at least in part. The former are bare monads@@1@@ (dormant).",[]],"baum:402":["Monads clearly representing the world either represent it to themselves distinctly, at least in part, or less so, §. 10. The former understand (*intelligunt*), §. 69. Therefore they have the faculty (*facultas*) of cognizing distinctly, §. 216, i.e. the understanding (*intellectus*)@@1@@ (more strictly speaking, cf. §. 519). An intellectual substance (*substantia intellectualis*)@@2@@, i.e. one endowed with understanding, is a spirit (*spiritus*)@@3@@ (intelligence, person). Therefore the intellectual monads of this universe are spirits, §. 230. One admitting only spirits in this world is an idealist (*idealista*).",[]],"baum:403":["The nexus of the spirits of some world among themselves is pneumatic (*pneumaticus*)@@1@@. Now in every world, this one too, in which spirits are present, singles are connected with singles. Therefore in this and every world in which spirits are present there is a universal pneumatic nexus, §. 357 (world, cf. §. 354, pneumatic, intellectual, moral, cf. §. 723, kingdom of grace).",[]],"baum:404":["Every substance (*substantia*) is a monad (*monas*), §. 234. Every spirit is a substance, §. 402. Therefore a monad is also a simple being (*ens simplex*), §. 230.",[]],"baum:405":["All spirits of every world, this one too, in which spirits are present, are monads, §. 404, representative forces of their world at least in part distinctly, §. 402, not extended, nor singly filling space, nor do they have quantitative magnitude, indivisible, finite, endowed only with a certain degree of forces, subject to metaphysical evil, partially similar to one another, but also singly partially dissimilar and unequal, nor do singles admit figure, §. 396; only one of all is the most perfect, §. 77, 185.",[]],"baum:406":["The whole of monads is a monadate (*monadatum*). Therefore this world and every composite is a monadate, §. 394.",[]],"baum:407":["Monads in a composite world either have also its parts as their monadates or not, §. 10, 406. In either case a monadate of this sort will be extended (*extensum*), §. 396, of impenetrable parts, §. 398, partially dissimilar, §. 396, i.e. partially heterogeneous (nonuniform, dissimilar), hence it will not be an extended thing totally homogeneous (uniform, similar), i.e. of totally similar parts, §. 271.",[]],"baum:408":["The simultaneous monads of this world determine place (*locus*) to one another, the successive determine age (*aetas*), §. 281, 85, hence they flow into one another, §. 211, posited in conflict, §. 213. Therefore in this world there is universal influx (*influxus*) and conflict (*conflictus*), §. 48, 306 (war of all against all, discordant concord, concordant discord, §. 364).",[]],"baum:409":["Of the monads in this world some flow into one another more remotely, § 408, 27, some more closely, present to one another, §. 223, and because an absolute leap in the world is impossible, §. 387, some most closely, touching one another, §. 223.",[]],"baum:410":["Because all monads of this world flow into one another, §. 408, their action is not transeunt (*transiens*), or influx without reaction, §. 213.",[]],"baum:411":["The transeunt action or influx of parts of the world into others outside them is either simple or composite, §. 215. The simple one is also the least, §. 247, 214. To this corresponds a reaction (*reactio*), either least or greater, §. 160, 161. If it is least, it is equal to the influx, §. 70. If a greater reaction be posited, that by which the reaction exceeds the influx posits a reaction of the one influencing, §. 410, hence its influx will not be simple, §. 215, which is contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore to a simple influx an equal reaction corresponds, §. 70.",[]],"baum:412":["A composite influx of parts of the world into others outside them is a whole of several simple influxes, §. 215, 214. To each of these an equal reaction corresponds, §. 411. Now if you add equals to equals, the sums are equal, §. 70. Therefore to every composite influx of parts of the world an equal reaction corresponds. Hence every reaction of parts of the world upon others outside them is equal to the influx or transeunt action, §. 411.",[]],"baum:413":["The living forces (*vires vivae*) of monads in the world conflicting among themselves through mutual contact are equal, §. 412, 331, and because the same cannot be proximate grounds of contradictories, §. 140, 36, determining to one another proximately place, §. 408, 409, hence conjunction, §. 281, 85, they do not contain the proximate ground of mutual separation, §. 72. Hence, unless a third force comes in, they touch inseparably, §. 386, constituting one, §. 73, united, §. 79.",[]],"baum:414":["Things touching one another in such a way that they can be separated only through a third force cohere (*cohaerent*)@@1@@. Therefore the monads of the world touching one another cohere, §. 413. There is no contact without cohesion. Therefore some monads of this world cohere, §. 409, constituting one, §. 413, an extended thing, §. 407.",[]],"baum:415":["No change (*mutatio*) occurs in a composite world without motion (*motus*). For let A be to be changed from B into non-B. It had coexisted with its simultaneous ones posited outside it, as B, now it will coexist as non-B, hence it will obtain a different relation to them, §. 37, 38, position, §. 85, and place, §. 281, and motion will arise, §. 283, 125. As often as such a change, such a motion, occurs in a composite world, the state of the changed thing, and of the universe of which it is a part, is partly the same as the preceding ones, §. 265, partly different, §. 125. Hence just as there was a certain motion, insofar as the new state differs from the former; so, insofar as the state remained the same, this duration of the state is in the composite world at once duration of place, §. 299, absence of a certain motion, rest (*quies*), §. 183, and was the hindrance of a certain motion, §. 221, and resistance, §. 222.",[]],"baum:416":["Monads in the universe constituting an extended thing, §. 414, always act, §. 216, 285, by their force, §. 400, representing the single states of their universe, and their own, also future, §. 298, insofar as these are the same as the antecedent ones, enduring, hindering a certain motion, resisting a certain motion, §. 415, 210, but insofar as these are different from the antecedent ones, effecting or moving a certain other motion, §. 415, 210. The aggregate of predicates agreeing with the single parts is attributed to the whole, §. 155. Hence certain monads of this universe constitute an extended thing to which the force of inertia (*vis inertiae*) is attributed, §. 294, hence matter (*materia*), §. 295. Matter, neither of this universe nor of any, can be totally homogeneous, §. 407.",[]],"baum:417":["Monads of this universe constituting matter do not exhibit it as first from the signification of §. 295, and merely passive, but as second matter, to which a moving force (*vis motrix*) is attributed, §. 416, and as a physical body (*corpus physicum*), §. 296. If one part of this world is moved, its relation to each of the remaining simultaneous ones is changed, §. 283, 281, hence there is no particular@@1@@ motion in the world, that is, of some part of the world, without a universal one, §. 283. Hence in this world all matter is in motion, §. 415, and its rest is nothing but relative@@2@@, i.e. the absence of some determinate motion, §. 283, which, if it were so great that no motion of the resting thing is observed, will be a vanishing motion@@3@@. There is in the world no absolute rest, absence of all motion.",[]],"baum:418":["An extended thing, an aggregate of monads each of which is impenetrable, will now hence resist the motion of things posited outside it that are about to occupy its place, §. 398, 222, now hence will exert the force of inertia, §. 294. An extended thing, an aggregate of monads of which no force is totally equal to any other posited outside itself, of which no whole, as to forces, is totally equal to any other monadate posited outside the former, §. 272, 406, is never in total equilibrium (*aequilibrium*)@@1@@, equality of forces in the conflicting, and now hence will exert moving force, §. 294. But such an extended thing is every monadate in this world, §. 414, 396. Therefore monadates in this world, on account of one and the same representative force of their monads, §. 400, insofar as it contains in the impenetrable the ground of enduring place, rest, and absence of a certain motion, will display the force of inertia, insofar as the same in those never totally equilibrating contains the ground of change of place and of a certain motion, the moving force, matter, §. 416, and physical bodies, §. 417. Which two forces are so far from being so opposed to one another that, one being posited, the other cannot at once be posited in the same subject in a different respect, §. 87, that rather in respect to different motions they are at once in any extended thing of this world, §. 417, and the same force attributed to a given extended thing is moving force in respect of a given motion, and in respect of the motion opposed to it is the force of inertia, and vice versa, §. 415.",[]],"baum:419":["Since forces are attributed to bodies (*corpora*), §. 296, they are composites more strictly so called (*composita strictius dicta*), §. 225, 198, hence the mode of composition is their essence, §. 226, they consist of monads, §. 235, they have quantitative magnitude, §. 243.",[]],"baum:420":["Bodies have parts outside parts, §. 296, 224. The actual first parts of bodies outside one another are called elements (*elementa*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:421":["Elements are either absolutely first parts of bodies, i.e. which do not themselves again have parts outside parts, or relatively such, beyond which it does not please to progress in attending to composition, §. 420. The former are simple beings or monads, §. 419, 224.",[]],"baum:422":["What is not matter is called immaterial (*immateriale*), what is matter, material (*materiale*). The elements of bodies absolutely such are immaterial, §. 421, 295. Incorporeal (*incorporeum*) is what is not a body, what is, corporeal (*corporeum*). Now everything corporeal is material, §. 296. Therefore the elements of bodies absolutely such are incorporeal, hence the singular ones are not bodies, §. 296.",[]],"baum:423":["Philosophy that holds the respectively first for the absolutely first is called the philosophy of the lazy (*philosophia pigrorum*); since it is false, elements relatively such, §. 421, material and corporeal, §. 422, are not to be held for elements absolutely such, immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422. Hence the latter are more fittingly called elements simply, the former first matters (*materiae primae*)@@1@@, cf. §. 295.",[]],"baum:424":["An atom (*atomus*)@@1@@ is called a being indivisible through itself (*ens per se indivisibile*). Every monad is indivisible through itself, §. 244. Hence every monad is an atom. Therefore the elements are atoms, §. 423, hence they are called atoms of nature.",[]],"baum:425":["We call observables (*observabilia*)@@1@@ (phenomena) what we can cognize through the senses (more confusedly). Smaller bodies not observable to us are called corpuscles (*corpuscula*). Philosophy explaining the phenomena of bodies from corpuscles is corpuscular.",[]],"baum:426":["Corpuscles either have again other corpuscles for parts, or less so, §. 10, 155. The latter are called primitive (*primitiva*)@@1@@, the former derivative (*derivativa*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:427":["The elements constitute all matter, such as is in this world, §. 418, 419. Hence the quantitative magnitude to be attributed to it, §. 419, 423, is the multitude of elements united for constituting a certain matter, §. 159, 413. Hence the elements, parts of a certain matter, are with it, as their monadated, in the same place, §. 155, 406. Therefore if their place is changed in such a way that they are not in the same place with the matter that they had constituted before, they do not remain parts of that matter, §. 155. Now, however, every change of relation is possible in a being regarded in itself, §. 133. Therefore every change of place is possible in all elements regarded in themselves, §. 281, 85. Therefore also that motion by which they cease to be parts of the matter whose parts they had been, §. 283. If they are moved in this way, the matter is divided, §. 244, 162. Therefore all matter, such as is in the world, is divisible, at least in and through itself, §. 18.",[]],"baum:428":["If matter in the world is said to be divisible to infinity: that is understood either absolutely, that there are no indivisible parts in it, and it is false, §. 424, 419, or respectively, that in dividing it there are parts not observable to us, unless again divisible, §. 248, and it is true that matter is divisible indefinitely, §. 425.",[]],"baum:429":["A material atom would be a corpuscle indivisible through itself, which is nothing, §. 427, 425. Atomistic philosophy, therefore, explaining the phenomena of bodies from material atoms, is mistaken.",[]],"baum:430":["The nature (*natura*) (cf. §. 431, 466) of a being is the complex of those of its internal determinations that are the principles of its changes, or of the accidents inhering in it in general. Hence to the nature of a being belong 1) its essentials, §. 39, 2) the essence, §. 40, 3) faculties, 4) receptivities, §. 216, 5) all the forces with which it is equipped, §. 197. The beginning of nature is origin (*origo*)@@1@@, duration life (*vita*)@@2@@, the end death (*mors*)@@3@@, cf. §. 556.",[]],"baum:431":["The nature of bodies is the mode of their composition, §. 419, with every faculty, receptivity, force of inertia and motive force, by which they are strong, §. 296, 430. Sometimes also it is called nature simply, cf. §. 430, 466.",[]],"baum:432":["Since in the world there is universal truth, §. 363, 90, all its changes have common norms, §. 86, 89, according to which the nature of its parts is determined, §. 431, hence also the motions of bodies in the world, §. 283, of which norms the higher are called laws (*leges*)@@1@@, the lower rules of motion (*regulae motus*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:433":["A machine (*machina*) is a composite, strictly speaking, movable according to laws of motion. Therefore every body in the world is a machine, §. 419, 432. Nor is a machine given in the world, except as a contingent being, §. 361. The nature of a machine determined through laws of motion is mechanism (*mechanismus*). But whatever is not composite is not a machine, hence no monad is a machine, §. 230.",[]],"baum:434":["The nexus of machines is mechanical, hence the nexus of bodies in the world is mechanical (a world, cf. §. 354, corporeal, material, kingdom of nature), §. 433. But in whatever world there are spirits and bodies, in it bodies are also connected with spirits, §. 357, hence in a world in which there are spirits and bodies there is a nexus 1) of bodies among themselves, 2) of spirits among themselves, §. 403, 3) a mutual one of bodies and spirits, pneumatic-mechanical and mechanical-pneumatic (harmony of the kingdom of nature and of grace).",[]],"baum:435":["Mechanical philosophy is that which explains the phenomena of bodies from their mechanism. Fate from the mechanism of bodies is physico-mechanical (physical or mechanical simply), but if some event of the world is determined through it, it is in itself contingent , §. 361, 354, necessary only hypothetically, §. 382, 105.",[]],"baum:436":["The most perfect world (*mundus perfectissimus*) is that in which the greatest of the most parts, and the most of the greatest parts, compossible in the world, agree toward one to the extent that can happen in a world. Hence the most perfect world has a most composite perfection, and any world to which only a simple perfection belongs is not most perfect, §. 185.",[]],"baum:437":["With the most perfect world posited, the highest perfection that is possible in a world is posited, §. 436. Hence the most perfect world is also the best of all possible worlds, §. 187. Now the parts of the world are actuals, §. 354, these either simultaneous or successive, §. 306. Therefore the most perfect world embraces so many 1) simultaneous, so many 2) successive, and 3) so great, as many and as great as are compossible in the best world, i.e. 1) extensively, 2) protensively, 3) intensively, it is the best, greatest of worlds, §. 436, 368.",[]],"baum:438":["If either only two contingents posited outside each other are compossible in the world, either as simultaneous or as successive, of which the perfection of one detracts either nothing or not so much from the perfection of the other as it adds to the perfection of the whole, the egoistic world (*mundus egoisticus*), such as the egoist posits this one, is not most perfect. If there is either only a single non-intellectual monad possible in itself and compossible with spirits in the world, whose perfection detracts either nothing or not so much from the perfection of spirits as it adds to the perfection of the whole, the idealistic world (*mundus idealisticus*), such as the idealist posits this one, is not most perfect, §. 437.",[]],"baum:439":["The materialist denies that a monad is given in this world, §. 395, hence forms for himself an impossible world, §. 394, therefore the world, such as the materialist feigns this one to be, materialistic (*materialisticus*), is neither any world, nor the most perfect, §. 436.",[]],"baum:440":["The most perfect world is also finite, §. 372, and all its parts, even the most perfect, are finite, §. 354, 257. Therefore neither it itself nor anything in it is infinite really, §. 248. Yet not only can it itself and many of its parts, for many reasons, be mathematically infinite, but it itself is also, for all who either cannot or to whom it does not please to determine the limits of the greatest contingent, of an extension, protension, intension mathematically infinite, such, or, intensively, extensively, protensively indefinite, §. 248, 437.",[]],"baum:441":["In the most perfect world there is the greatest nexus that is possible in a world, universal, §. 437, 94, harmony and consensus, §. 436, 357.",[]],"baum:442":["Since every world is perfect, §. 360, the most imperfect (*imperfectissimus*) is that in which there is the least perfection that can be in a world, the consensus of the fewest least things toward one least, the least, §. 354, 185, hence simple, §. 96, without exceptions, §. 97. The most imperfect world is the smallest, §. 368.",[]],"baum:443":["Even if the most perfect world is posited as the best of worlds, §. 437, it is nevertheless not thereby posited as the highest good or the metaphysical best, even if there are posited in it the greatest, most numerous realities that are compossible in any world, yet not absolutely the most numerous, greatest, §. 190, much less absolutely necessary, since the existence of singulars is a mode, §. 361. Even if to the most perfect world the greatest degree of reality to be obtained in some universe is attributed, that nevertheless in a contingent being will be only a limit, §. 248, 250, quite diverse, §. 38, from the degree of reality of the really infinite, absolutely greatest, necessary, immutable, §. 251.",[]],"baum:444":["In all perfection there is order, §. 95. Hence in the most perfect world there is the greatest order that is possible in a world, §. 437, 175. Therefore very many common rules of perfection, e.g. The more, the greater, the more spacious, §. 280, the more lasting, §. 299, other things equal, the better, §. 437, 187, and a most composite order, §. 183, yet in such a way that the lower and higher rules can finally all be cognized as to one from one highest, and likewise strongest, rule of perfection, §. 182, 185.",[]],"baum:445":["In the most perfect world, since there are very many rules of perfection, §. 444, there can also be quite many exceptions, §. 97, 372, provided only that they do not remove the greatest consensus, §. 440, hence there are those that can be, other things equal, the fewest, §. 161, the smallest, §. 186.",[]],"baum:446":["If in the most perfect world a sufficient and an insufficient ground of perfection collide, the exception arises from the insufficient: if a more fecund and a less fecund collide, the exception arises from the less fecund; if a weightier and a less weighty, the exception arises from the less weighty; if a more remote and a nearer one subordinated to the more remote, the exception arises from the nearer; if one sufficient in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) and one such simply, the exception arises from the one sufficient in a certain respect; if a lower and a higher, the exception arises from the lower. Whatever rule of perfection finally collides in the most perfect world with the highest, is excepted by it, §. 186, 445.",[]],"baum:447":["Of the most perfect world not only §. 361-376, 354, 436 hold, but also those things are furthest absent from it that §. 380-388, 247, 354 teach to be absent from every world. e.g. So far is Spinozistic fate, §. 382, absent from it, that rather contingency and mutability, even hypothetical, §. 104, 127, are to be attributed to it as indefinite extensively, intensively and protensively, §. 440, 437.",[]],"baum:448":["Since in this world there is harmony (*harmonia*), §. 356, there is universal influx (*influxus universalis*), §. 408, this will be either everywhere real (*realis*), or everywhere ideal (*idealis*), or sometimes real, sometimes ideal, §. 212. The harmony of the monads of the world flowing into one another is the commerce of worldly substances (*commercium substantiarum mundanarum*)@@1@@. Therefore the substances of every world, and of this one, are in universal commerce, §. 357. Opinions that seem apt for explaining this are called systems of explaining the commerce among the substances of the world@@2@@, and if they are applied to explaining all the commerce of all, universal@@3@@, cf. §. 761 (general). Changes of the parts of the world posited in commerce, of which, occurring in one part of the commerce, the sufficient reason (*ratio sufficiens*) can be cognized from the force of the other part of the commerce, are harmonic@@4@@. The ideal influx of all the substances of the world into one another is universal preestablished harmony (*harmonia praestabilita universalis*)@@5@@, cf. §. 462, and one who posits it in this world is a universal harmonist (*harmonista universalis*), whose system is called the system of universal preestablished harmony@@6@@.",[]],"baum:449":["The system of universal preestablished harmony does not remove the influx of the substances of this world into one another, but posits it, §. 448; it does not remove that one substance of the world suffers from another, §. 448, 212, but nevertheless posits that any change of its own, which it suffers from another substance of this world, it produces by its own force, §. 210; it does not remove the mutual conflict of the parts of this world, §. 213, the faculty and receptivity of transeunt actions, §. 217, much less the influx of the infinite substance, and the receptivity of that influx in finite ones, §. 448; it not only does not remove that spirits can act on bodies, and bodies on spirits, but even posits that in this world bodies and spirits flow into one another mutually, §. 408, 434, and can touch one another mutually, §. 223, 409.",[]],"baum:450":["The real influx of a part of the world's substance into another part of the world is physical (*physicus*). Hence universal physical influx (*influxus physicus universalis*) is the universal harmony of substances in the world, by which one flows really into another, and one who posits it in this world is a universal influxionist (*influxionista universalis*). His system is the system of universal physical influx.",[]],"baum:451":["The system of universal physical influx does not remove the mutual harmony of the substances of the world, and that a universal one, §. 450, 48, but a preestablished one, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the substances of this world flow into one another mutually, conflict among themselves, suffer from one another, and that both can touch bodies with respect to spirits, spirits with respect to bodies, indeed can touch one another mutually, is a universal influxionist, §. 449, 450. The universal influxionist 1) denies that any substance of this world, when it suffers from another substance of this world, acts and produces its passions by its own force, §. 450, 212, 2) posits that really the passions of one substance of this world are produced by another substance of this world flowing into it, §. 450. Hence according to the system of universal physical influx no substance, part of the world, in any of its harmonic changes acts by its own force, §. 448. But now all changes of a worldly substance can be sufficiently cognized from the force of any other monad belonging to the same world, §. 354, 400. Therefore all are harmonic, §. 448, and hence in none of its changes does any worldly substance act, according to the system of universal physical influx, really suffering in all from other worldly substances, which nevertheless on account of the same reason would not act at all, §. 210, hence neither would they be forces, §. 197, q.c., §. 199.",[]],"baum:452":["One who posits that all the substances of this world seen to suffer from other substances of this world, and indeed really suffering, according to his own opinion, that the infinite substance alone really flows in, is a universal occasionalist (*occasionalista universalis*) (assistentian), and his system is the universal system of occasional causes (*systema causarum occasionalium*)@@1@@ (Cartesian, or rather the Malebranchian of assistance), contradicting §. 400, 408, hence false. The same is evident from this, that through occasionalism no harmonic change in any worldly substance is its own, but only an action of the infinite substance, §. 448. Hence this system also removes all force and energy in finite things, §. 451.",[]],"baum:453":["The system of occasional causes, if not harmony, nevertheless removes the true universal mutual influx of the substances of this world, §. 452, 448, this physical, §. 450, and ideal, §. 212 (unless you thus call it imaginary, apparent, §. 12), that preestablished, §. 448. Not everyone who posits that the infinite substance really flows into all the passions of finite substances, nor everyone who posits that the infinite substance alone really flows into them, is a universal occasionalist, but one who posits 1) that all the passions of finite substances are real, 2) that they depend on the influx of the infinite substance alone, §. 452.",[]],"baum:454":["The systems of universal preestablished harmony and of universal physical influx are similar in this, §. 70, that 1) the universal ones remove neither the mutual influx of the substances of this world, nor harmony, nor the real influx of the infinite substance into the same, §. 448, 450, 2) they posit that the passions of the substances of this world are actions of some finite substance; they are dissimilar in this, that the latter excludes the suffering substance from action, the former does not exclude it, §. 449, 451.",[]],"baum:455":["The universal systems of preestablished harmony and of occasional causes are similar in this, that they remove the real influx of a substance of this world into another, a part of the same, §. 70, they are dissimilar in this, §. 70, that the former posits as ideal certain passions of them, which the latter posits as real, §. 449, 453.",[]],"baum:456":["The universal systems of physical influx and of occasional causes are similar in this, that both posit as real the passions of one substance in this world from another part of the same world; they are dissimilar in this, §. 70, that the former removes that the infinite substance alone flows into them, the latter posits it, §. 451, 452.",[]],"baum:457":["Universal systems of explaining the commerce of worldly substances, if they explain the single commerces of the single substances of the world in a similar manner, are called simple (*simplicia*)@@1@@. Therefore the universal systems of preestablished harmony, of physical influx and of occasional causes are simple systems, §. 448, 456. Universal systems are composite (*composita*)@@2@@ which explain certain commerces of certain substances of the world in a different manner than others.",[]],"baum:458":["Besides the universal systems of preestablished harmony, of physical influx, and of occasional causes, some fourth simple universal one, §. 457, is not possible, §. 448, 452; infinitely many, or rather indefinitely many, composite ones can be, §. 457, 248.",[]],"baum:459":["Through preestablished harmony, the ideal influx of several substances of the world posited in commerce into one another, a greater nexus (*nexus*) is actuated than through their physical influx, §. 167. For in physical influx the passion of the really suffering substance has no sufficient reason in the forces of the suffering substance, §. 450, 212. In preestablished harmony the passion of the suffering substance has a sufficient reason 1) in its own forces, 2) in the ideally influencing substance, §. 449. Hence the influencing substance in preestablished harmony is equally fecund as in physical influx, but the suffering substance is in preestablished harmony more fecund than in physical influx, §. 166.",[]],"baum:460":["Through the influx of the infinite substance attributed to it alone into the really suffering substances posited in commerce, whatever they suffer, the suffering substance is not more fecund than through physical influx, §. 453, but the other, finite part of the commerce is still less fecund, §. 453, 166. Therefore through preestablished harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance a greater nexus is actuated in the substances posited in commerce than through an influx attributed to the infinite substance alone, §. 167.",[]],"baum:461":["In the most perfect world the nexus of the possible things in the world is greatest, §. 441. Therefore the substances, which and as many as can be in it, are connected through preestablished harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance, §. 460, 459.",[]],"baum:462":["If universal preestablished harmony joined with the concurrence of the infinite substance is possible in the most perfect world, it is such as the universal harmonist posits this one to be, §. 448, 461, and not such as one about to adopt some composite system might conceive, §. 451; on the other hand every particular preestablished harmony@@1@@, i.e. of certain parts of the world, is to be admitted in the same, §. 154.",[]],"baum:463":["Since the condition, without which not (*conditio sine qua non*), of universal preestablished harmony is its possibility in the most perfect world, this is to be demonstrated by one about to demonstrate preestablished harmony through §. 462. Otherwise it is evident thus: from any monad of any world the single parts of the world, to which it belongs, can be cognized, §. 400, therefore also the single changes of the world, §. 354, 155. But such are all the passions of any worldly monad, which it suffers from another monad of its world, §. 210. But a suffering monad is a force, §. 199. Therefore that very monad suffering from another worldly monad is the reason of this its passion, and of its single parts, §. 354, 155, hence the sufficient reason of this its passion, §. 14, 21, and thus a given passion of it is at the same time an action of the suffering one, §. 210. Therefore all the passions of the monads of any world, which they suffer from other worldly monads, are only ideal, §. 212. Therefore such also is always the influx of a worldly monad into another, never real, never physical, §. 450. Hence in this world too all its substances are in universal commerce only through preestablished harmony, §. 448, none ever through physical influx. The system of universal preestablished harmony is the true opinion, therefore every particular preestablished harmony is also true, §. 462, every physical influx, both universal and particular, and its system, §. 450, and the system of occasional causes, §. 452, are false.",[]],"baum:464":["In the most perfect world the nexus is the greatest that is possible in a world, §. 441. Therefore if there are bodies and spirits in the most perfect world, §. 438, there is in it a greatest mechanical, pneumatic, and pneumatico-mechanical nexus, §. 441 (the greatest harmony of the realm of nature and of grace, §. 434, 403, preestablished, §. 463).",[]],"baum:465":["A dualist (*dualista*) is one who posits that this world consists of spirits and bodies outside themselves. Hence the most perfect world, not egoistic, not idealistic, §. 438, is such as the dualist conceives this one, §. 439.",[]],"baum:466":["The complex (*complexus*) of the natures in the single parts of the world, and in the parts taken together, is universal nature (*natura universa*) (natured, cf. §. 859). Hence the nature of this and of the most perfect universe is the aggregate or complex of all the essentials, essences, faculties, receptivities, forces, with which all its parts, monads, elements, spirits, matters, bodies are furnished, §. 430, hence every mode of composition, force of inertia, motive force, and mechanism of all the bodies in it is a part of universal nature, §. 431, 433.",[]],"baum:467":["The essence of the world and its mode of composition of the composite, §. 226, with the essences of the single parts of it, is nothing but a rather small part, §. 161, of universal nature, §. 466, 155.",[]],"baum:468":["Universal nature has internal mutable parts, §. 466, and the universal nature of the most perfect world has very many of them, §. 437. Hence the universal nature of any world whatever is a contingent being (*ens contingens*), §. 131, and finite, §. 255, and the universal nature of the most perfect world indeed has a mathematically thrice infinite contingency, §. 447.",[]],"baum:469":["An event to be actualized by the nature of any contingent being is natural (*naturalis*), as distinguished from the supernatural (*supernaturali*), cf. §. 474. But an event to be actualized by the determinate nature of a determinate contingent being is natural, as distinguished from the preternatural (*praeternaturali*), cf. §. 474. Whatever can be actualized by the nature of something is physically possible (*physice possibile*) for it, whatever cannot be is physically impossible (*physice impossibile*) for it. Certain things possible in themselves nevertheless cannot be actualized by every nature, §. 15, 430, therefore can be physically impossible for many. Not everything physically impossible for some is also absolutely such. Things physically impossible for something contingent are such either in whatever state its nature is regarded, naturally impossible simply (merely, entirely, utterly) (*simpliciter*), or only then, if its nature is regarded in a certain state, naturally impossible in a certain respect (*secundum quid*) (for now and thus). The natural impossibility, such simply, of realities is merely natural impotence (*impotentia mere naturalis*). Not every natural impossibility is merely natural impotence or absolute impossibility, §. 467. The opposite of the physically impossible is the physically necessary (*physice necessarium*), of the physically possible the physically contingent (*physice contingens*). Not everything physically necessary is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Certain things contingent in themselves can be physically necessary for many, §. 104. Things physically necessary are such either simply, or in a certain respect.",[]],"baum:470":["The natural for a certain body is what is to be actualized by its determinate nature, for a certain corporeal world it is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the bodies of a certain world, §. 466. The natural for a certain contingent spirit is to be actualized through its determinate nature. The natural for a certain pneumatic world is what is to be actualized through the universal nature of the spirits of a certain world, §. 469.",[]],"baum:471":["The succession (*successio*) of natural things in the world is the course of nature (*cursus naturae*). Hence the course of nature is opposed to the succession either of supernatural or of preternatural things, §. 469, and is regarded either in some body, or in some whole corporeal world, or in some spirit, or in some whole pneumatic world, or in universal nature, §. 470, 466.",[]],"baum:472":["The order (*ordo*) of natural things in the world is the order of nature (*ordo naturae*), and it is opposed to the conjunction either of supernatural or of preternatural things, and is regarded either only in a certain body, or corporeal world, or spirit, or pneumatic world, §. 469, 470, or in universal nature, §. 466. Each of these orders has its common norms, §. 86. Therefore in this world there are norms of the natural order, common laws and rules, §. 432, diverse, §. 38, but similar, §. 70. The complex of similar laws is right (*ius*)@@1@@, cf. §. 971 (said more broadly). The complex of the laws of the order of nature is the broadest right of nature, its parts are the laws and rules of motion, §. 432, and the laws of nature of spirits.",[]],"baum:473":["If a natural thing follows a natural thing, it occurs according to the course of nature, and that according to the course of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain nexus in a certain material world, or of a certain spirit, or also of a certain nexus in a certain pneumatic world, §. 471, or at least according to the course of universal nature, §. 466; if it succeeds, or coexists with, a natural thing, it occurs according to the order of nature, and that according to the order of nature either of a certain body, or of a certain nexus in a certain material world, or of a certain spirit, or also of a certain nexus in a certain pneumatic world, §. 472, or at least according to the order of universal nature, §. 466. Therefore the natural thing succeeding and coexisting with natural things is actualized according to natural laws, §. 472. The natural for a certain body, or a certain nexus in the material world, is actualized according to the laws and rules of motion through the mechanism of bodies, §. 433, hence can be explained mechanically (*mechanice*), §. 435, 433.",[]],"baum:474":["An event of the world actualized by the nature of no contingent being is supernatural. An event not actualized by the determinate nature of a certain contingent being, in which it occurs, is, with respect to that being, preternatural. Hence supernatural things are preternatural with respect to universal nature; and preternatural things with respect to universal nature are supernatural, §. 466. A supernatural event, insofar as it is regarded as extraordinary, is a miracle (*miraculum*)@@1@@. Hence a miracle is preternatural with respect to universal nature. Every miracle is supernatural, but not everything supernatural is referred to miracles.",[]],"baum:475":["Supernatural things and miracles are opposites of natural things and of things naturally ordinary, §. 81, 474. Now natural things and things naturally ordinary in every world are contingent, §. 354, 257. Therefore supernatural things and miracles are possible, §. 101.",[]],"baum:476":["What is actualized through the nature of a contingent being in the world is not supernatural, nor miracles, §. 474. Hence things actualized through the nature of a spirit, which is a part of the world, are not supernatural, not miracles, §. 354, 257.",[]],"baum:477":["Actions of finite spirits upon other parts of the world, not actualized through the determinate nature of the things acted upon, hence preternatural with respect to the things acted upon, §. 474, and extraordinary, §. 472, 384, are nevertheless not supernatural, nor miracles, §. 474. Hence if they are called comparative miracles (*miracula comparativa*)@@1@@, comparative miracles are not miracles, which, to be distinguished from those, are called rigorous (*rigorosa*)@@2@@. If such actions are posited above nature, that can be conceded only of the certain nature of a certain body or corporeal world, §. 470.",[]],"baum:478":["An extraordinary event whose natural cause we do not know is a prodigy (*prodigium*)@@1@@ (sign, cf. §. 347, *ostentum*, *portentum*). Prodigies are either rigorous miracles, §. 474, 477, or natural events, miracles as far as we are concerned (*quoad nos*)@@2@@.",[]],"baum:479":["Since supernatural things and miracles are not actualized through universal nature, §. 474, 466, and nevertheless are contingent beings, §. 474, 354, hence effects, §. 334, when they exist they have as efficient cause an extramundane being, §. 388, to which they bear witness, §. 333.",[]],"baum:480":["A non-natural event succeeding natural things occurs against the course of nature, §. 471, 473. Now the supernatural and the miracle is not a natural event, §. 474, 469. Therefore the supernatural and the miracle occurs against the course of nature succeeding natural things, and indeed against the course of universal nature, §. 471. The preternatural for a certain body, or a certain corporeal world, a certain spirit, or a certain pneumatic world occurs against only some course of nature, §. 474, 471. Hence not everything occurring against any course of nature whatever is supernatural, or a miracle, §. 474.",[]],"baum:481":["Supernatural things and miracles are not absolutely extraordinary, §. 475, 385, nor against every order of the world, §. 359, 7, yet they are not naturally ordinary, §. 472, 474. And since a non-natural event coexisting with or succeeding natural things occurs against the order of nature, supernatural things and miracles are against every order of universal nature. Therefore they do not occur according to the common norms of a certain order of nature, §. 472. Therefore they are extraordinary relative to the order of nature, §. 384. Supernatural things and miracles are extraordinary with respect to the order of universal nature, §. 472. The preternatural is extraordinary with respect to a certain body, or corporeal world, a certain spirit, or pneumatic nexus, and the order of nature determined in them, §. 474, 472, so that it occurs against this, §. 384. Therefore not everything extraordinary with respect to any order of nature whatever, not everything that is against any order of nature whatever, is supernatural, much less a miracle, §. 474.",[]],"baum:482":["The law of order in the most perfect world, §. 84, and one among the higher ones, §. 182, is this: Let the best of the compossibles in the most perfect world be conjoined with the best, §. 444. This law is called the law of the best in the world (*lex optimi in mundo*), cf. §. 822, to which is subordinated the law of the best in nature (*lex optimi in natura*): let the best of the natural things in the most perfect world be conjoined with the best, §. 469, 444.",[]],"baum:483":["Posit in the most perfect world a natural event, hence one to occur according to the order of nature, §. 473, compossible with the rest. Posit under the same circumstances a supernatural event, hence one to occur against every order of nature, §. 481, compossible with the rest, but better than the natural. Since opposite events, the natural and the supernatural, e.g. the miraculous, cannot exist at the same time, §. 81, in this case the rules of the order of universal nature and the higher rule of perfection, §. 482, will collide, §. 97.",[]],"baum:484":["Since the rules of the order of nature, both the peculiar and the universal, are in the most perfect world lower and subordinated to the law of the best in the world, §. 182, 482, in the case posited through §. 483 an exception to the laws of the order of nature will occur, §. 446, and something supernatural, indeed a miracle, will occur, §. 483, most fitting to the highest order of the most perfect world, §. 444.",[]],"baum:485":["Supernatural things and miracles through an absolute leap (*saltus absolutus*) would be impossible, §. 387, hence no supernatural things, no miracles occur through an absolute leap, §. 475. Since nevertheless the proximate sufficient reason of naturally ordinary events in the world is the nature of some contingent being, §. 472, 469, when supernatural things and miracles occur, they occur through a relative leap (*saltus respectivus*), §. 474, 386.",[]],"baum:486":["Supernatural things and miracles occurring in the corporeal world cannot be explained mechanically, §. 435, 433, much less the pneumatic ones, occurring in the pneumatic world (the realm of grace), §. 433, 403.",[]],"baum:487":["If in place of a natural event in the world a supernatural and miraculous one is posited, the world is not totally the same as it would be if the natural event were a part of it, §. 267, 155. Therefore, with the supernatural and the miracle posited in the world, in place of the natural effect, a state of the world is posited, coexisting with the supernatural given, or the miracle, partially other than it would be if the supernatural or the miracle had not been performed, §. 378, 369.",[]],"baum:488":["Nothing at all is sterile, §. 23, hence no supernatural (*supernaturale*), no miracle (*miraculum*) is without a corollary (*corollarium*), or what is grounded (*rationatum*); this being posited, something is again posited, §. 14, hence its grounded thing in turn, §. 23, and so on. Therefore of every supernatural, of every miracle, consequences (*consectaria*) are posited indefinitely, §. 248, through the states of the world successive after the supernatural event, after the miracle, §. 30, 369.",[]],"baum:489":["If, in place of the supernatural event, in place of the miracle, a natural event had been posited in the world in the case mentioned in §. 487, 488, this too would have had its own grounded things (*rationata*) indefinitely through all the states of the world succeeding it, §. 23, 369, but the consequences of a natural effect and of a supernatural or miraculous one cannot be wholly the same, §. 267, 38. Therefore, the supernatural being posited, the miracle being posited in the world in place of the natural effect, all states of the world succeeding the supernatural event, or the miracle, are partially other than they would have been had the supernatural, or the miracle, been given and not performed, §. 369.",[]],"baum:490":["In the system of occasionalism (*systema occasionalismi*) every passion of a finite substance is supernatural, §. 474, 453, although some can be conceived that is not a miracle, insofar as it is regarded as ordinary, §. 384.",[]],"baum:491":["Since the supernatural and the miracle is an action, §. 474, 323, it will be either simple or composite, §. 215, and this latter either a simultaneous being (*ens simultaneum*), or a successive being (*ens successivum*), or both, §. 238. Both the simple and the composite, provided it is a simultaneous being, occurs in an instant, destitute of prior and posterior parts, §. 300.",[]],"baum:492":["The more fecund, the more noble the supernatural and the miracle, §. 166, the greater it is, §. 491, 214, hence the greatest will be the most fecund, the most noble, and the most composite, §. 214, 215.",[]],"baum:493":["A naturalist (*naturalista*) in the broader sense, cf. §. 999, is one who removes all supernatural events in this world. Therefore he removes the existence of miracles in this world, §. 474; if he does this on account of denying their possibility, he errs, §. 475.",[]],"baum:494":["The nature of this world being posited, as it is, of mathematically infinite extension, intension, protension, §. 440, 466, it is also of infinite contingency of this kind, §. 468, 361, nor can it exist, except as the effect of an extramundane cause, of which it testifies, §. 375, just as supernaturals and miracles, when they exist, §. 479. Hence one who, not confusing the limits of his theory about the whole nature of this world with the limits of the said nature itself, nor thence securely concluding from his mere ignorance of a natural cause to the denying of it altogether, is therefore not now injurious toward the cause common to universal nature and to supernaturals and miracles, §. 375, 479, nor is he a naturalist, §. 493.",[]],"baum:495":["Posit a natural and a supernatural event in the most perfect world, compossible under the same circumstances with the remaining parts of the world, but, as consequences, equally good. The natural will satisfy the law of the best in the world equally as the supernatural, §. 482, and at the same time the rules of perfection that the order of nature prescribes, §. 472, hence more of them than the supernatural, §. 481. In this case therefore nothing supernatural, or miracle, will come about in the most perfect world, §. 482, 445.",[]],"baum:496":["Since the whole nature even of the most perfect world is finite, §. 468, it is not a sufficient principle of all the accidents that can inhere in the best world, §. 430, 259. Posit therefore an event compossible with the rest in the best world, best of the best, but not to be actualized naturally and ordinarily, posit that the same can be actualized supernaturally and extraordinarily; in this case too, as in §. 484, something supernatural will come about, indeed a miracle, most conveniently to the order of the most perfect world, §. 482, 444.",[]],"baum:497":["As many and as great things as can happen best in the best world naturally and ordinarily come about in it naturally, §. 495. As many and as great things as either cannot at all in it, or cannot equally well, happen naturally and ordinarily, come about in it, as the best, supernaturally and miraculously, §. 484, 496. The former can be called supplementing miracles (*miracula supplentia*)@@1@@, the latter emending ones (*emendantia*)@@2@@. The reasoning is the same for supernaturals.",[]],"baum:498":["The number of supernaturals and miracles in the most perfect world is as great as the number of its possibles that cannot be actualized equally well naturally and according to the order of nature, §. 159, 497, neither greater nor less, §. 160.",[]],"baum:499":["If any supernatural event in the best world is better than a natural one, also as consequences, if any possible event of the best world is naturally impossible through universal nature: the most perfect world is not such as the naturalist in the more general signification posits this one to be, §. 498, 493.",[]],"baum:500":["Not everyone who denies that certain preternaturals, §. 474, extraordinary effects of finite spirits, §. 476, comparative miracles, §. 477, prodigies and miracles as to us, §. 478, events against the course of nature, §. 480, and some order, §. 481, are miracles in this world, §. 474, is such a naturalist in the more general signification, §. 493.",[]],"baum:501":["Psychology (*psychologia*) is the science of the general predicates of the soul.",[]],"baum:502":["Psychology, containing the first principles of theologies, aesthetics, logic, practical sciences, with reason, §. 501, is referred, §. 2, to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:503":["Psychology deduces its assertions 1) more proximately from experience, empirical (*empirica*), 2) from the notion of the soul by a longer series of ratiocinations, rational (*rationalis*).",[]],"baum:700":["I am changed, §. 505-699, internally, §. 126. Therefore I am a finite being, §. 254, and contingent, §. 257. Therefore my existence is a mode, §. 134, and so all my states are in themselves contingent, §. 205, 108. Therefore also the changes of them all, §. 124, 125. Therefore all my actions, all passions are in themselves contingent, §. 210. Hence also all my future actions, §. 298. Hence no actions of mine, no passions, are absolutely or intrinsically necessary, §. 105.",[]],"baum:701":["Necessitation (*necessitatio*)@@1@@ (coercion, *coactio*) is the change of something from contingent into necessary, hence it is either active@@2@@ of the necessitating thing, which is not properly, except a substance, §. 198, or passive@@3@@ of the necessitated, which is attributed both to accidents, either action or passion, that are changed from contingents into necessaries, and to the substance in which such necessitated accidents inhere.",[]],"baum:702":["Absolute@@1@@ necessitation (coercion) would be that which would change something contingent in itself into absolutely necessary. Now nothing can be changed into the absolutely necessary, §. 130. Therefore not any action, therefore not any action of mine. Therefore absolute necessitation of any action of mine is impossible, §. 7. All my actions are and always remain, in the act itself, and after it, contingent in themselves, hence also their opposite possible in itself, §. 700, 104.",[]],"baum:703":["Of the opposite of many of my actions there is a very great hypothetical possibility, §. 168. Hence of many of my actions there is a very great contingency, also hypothetical, §. 188, 700. Hence future ones are very contingent futures, §. 298.",[]],"baum:704":["An action dependent on a sufficient principle that is internal to the agent is spontaneous (*spontanea*)@@1@@. Whence spontaneity (*spontaneitas*) is attributed 1) to an action that depends on a sufficient principle that is internal to the agent, 2) to a substance performing such actions. Now every action properly such depends on a principle that is internal to the agent, §. 210, 37. Therefore every action properly such is spontaneous. Since nevertheless changes composed of actions and passions are sometimes denominated actions from the predominant part, insofar as they are passions, they are conceived as not spontaneous, §. 210.",[]],"baum:705":["Many of my actions, indeed all those properly so called, hence also actions of my soul, are spontaneous, and so spontaneity is truly attributed both to them and to my soul, §. 704. If an automaton (*automaton*) is called what changes itself, the soul will be an automaton.",[]],"baum:706":["Spontaneity is least if the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices only for one least action, §. 704, 161. The more numerous therefore, the greater the actions for which the principle intrinsic to the agent suffices, the greater is its spontaneity, until it is greatest in that agent in which it suffices for the most, the greatest actions. To my soul a very great spontaneity belongs, §. 705.",[]],"baum:707":["External necessitation (*necessitatio externa*)@@1@@ (coercion from outside, *coactio ab extra*) is dependent on a force existing outside the necessitated substance, and is either ideal or real, §. 701, 212. An action externally really necessitated would not be spontaneous, nor properly an action, §. 704, but a real passion, §. 210. Real external necessitation is external necessitation (coercion) simply such@@2@@. Therefore actions coerced by external necessitation simply such would be real passions. A substance and actions that are not absolutely necessitated are free (cf. §. 719) from absolute coercion@@3@@. A substance and actions that are not necessitated by external coercion simply such are free from external coercion simply such@@4@@. Therefore all my changes are free from absolute coercion, §. 702, all my actions, all spontaneous actions of my soul, i.e. all actions properly so called, §. 704, and my soul, insofar as it acts spontaneously, are free from external coercion simply such.",[]],"baum:708":["Actions physically possible for me are posited in my power@@1@@, realities physically impossible for me are posited outside my power@@2@@. Therefore there is some action posited in my power either only simply, or also in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), there is something either also simply, or only in a certain respect, outside my power, §. 469. Of actions posited in the power of some agent the opposite is either also posited in the power of the same agent, or outside it, §. 9, and each again either simply, or in a certain respect, §. 469. Those that themselves with their opposites, at least simply, are posited in the power of someone, are free (cf. §. 719) for him as to execution@@3@@, those whose opposite is posited simply above someone's power, are for him merely natural. A free action as to execution, to whose opposite there is an equal physical possibility with respect to a certain agent, is physically indifferent for him@@4@@ (indifferent, as exercise of the act).",[]],"baum:709":["Merely natural actions are naturally necessary. Whence actions free as to execution, contradistinguished from these, are physically contingent, §. 708, 469. Hence future ones are sometimes called simply contingent futures@@1@@.",[]],"baum:710":["Internal necessitation (*necessitatio*)@@1@@ (coercion, *coactio*) is that which depends on the internal determination of a necessitated substance (*substantia*), and is attributed to substances and their actions, 1) when the actions of substances are thought to be necessitated by their essence alone, absolute@@2@@ (essential). Such necessitation, since it would change actions into absolutely necessary ones, §. 702, 107, is altogether impossible, §. 702. 2) when through the nature of a substance certain actions are changed from physically otherwise contingent ones into physically necessary ones, such for that nature either simply or in some respect, §. 469, physical (natural). Hence merely natural actions can be said to be necessitated by internal physical coercion, §. 709, 708. Actions and substances that in neither of these senses are internally coerced are free (cf. §. 719) from internal necessitation, both absolute and physical@@3@@. Now however free actions with regard to execution, and substances, which and insofar as they perform actions of this kind, are in neither of these senses internally coerced, §. 709, 708, therefore they are free from internal coercion, both absolute and physical.",[]],"baum:711":["Many of my actions, many spontaneous actions of my soul are free with regard to execution, §. 708. Therefore both to them and to the soul, insofar as it performs them, belongs freedom (*libertas*) from external coercion simply such, §. 707, and from internal coercion, both absolute and physical, §. 710.",[]],"baum:712":["Liking (*lubitus*)@@1@@ is the cognition (*cognitio*) with which a substance is endowed, from which, according to the laws of appetition and aversion, it can be cognized why it determines itself thus and not otherwise concerning a free action with regard to execution. But this can be cognized from foresight, presentiment, pleasure or tedium, §. 665, stimuli and motives, §. 677, 690. Therefore foresight, presentiment, pleasure or tedium, stimuli and motives, which are cognized by a certain substance, constitute its liking. If a substance concerning a free action with regard to execution so determines its force as can be cognized from its liking, it desires or averts according to liking@@2@@. He therefore who would desire or avert either what was not foreseen, or what he had not at all presaged would come to be by any effort of his, or what neither pleases nor displeases, without any stimuli, any motives, would not desire, would not avert according to liking. I desire many things, I avert many things according to my liking. Therefore I have the faculty (*facultas*) of desiring and averting according to my liking, i.e. choice (*arbitrium*)@@3@@. Actions which to determine through choice is placed in the power of some substance are for it arbitrary@@4@@. Many of my actions are arbitrary.",[]],"baum:713":["I desire or avert willingly@@1@@ 1) whatever I desire or avert according to liking, and then I desire nothing, I avert nothing unwillingly, or against my will@@2@@, §. 712, 665, 2) if the liking contains either mere tedium, or pure pleasure, or an enormous preponderance: then I desire or avert unwillingly (against liking) where there is not at all a great preponderance in the liking: or, where many and great things also seem to impel toward the opposite of what I desire or avert. In the last sense, unwilling, nevertheless I desire or avert according to liking, and in that sense an unwilling action is nevertheless arbitrary, §. 712.",[]],"baum:714":["Since coercion (*coactio*) in the strict sense@@1@@, cf. §. 701, is the production of an unwilling action: a coerced or unwilling action through external coercion taken simply@@2@@ would be one that I would perform according to no liking, against all liking, through external coercion taken simply, but this would not be an action properly so called, §. 707. If I do something unwillingly according to liking from §. 713, the preponderance of that which I desire or avert is considered either as produced by me, and I am said to have coerced myself@@3@@, or as produced by another outside me, §. 22, and the action is called unwilling or coerced through external coercion in some respect@@4@@ (mixed, namely from the arbitrary and the unwilling through external coercion taken simply).",[]],"baum:715":["Unwilling actions, to which I am said to coerce myself, or to be externally coerced in some respect by others, come about according to my liking, §. 714, hence are arbitrary, §. 712, and are called necessitated, insofar as they are conceived as less necessary, if either I myself or other things had not produced the preponderance that produced them, §. 701, 188.",[]],"baum:716":["Actions unwilling through ignorance or error (*ignorantia, error*)@@1@@ are called those which I would not have performed according to liking, had I not been ignorant of something or erred; which, since they nevertheless come about according to liking, are also arbitrary, §. 712.",[]],"baum:717":["Choice would be minimal if it determined only a single action according to the minimal liking, §. 161. The greater, therefore, the liking according to which, the more, the greater actions it determines, the greater it is, §. 160, until it is maximal, determining the greatest and most numerous actions from the greatest liking, §. 161, 712.",[]],"baum:718":["Since choice is the faculty of desiring or averting according to its liking, §. 712, a substance endowed with choice will have either only the faculty of desiring or averting sensitively, or only of willing or not willing according to liking, or the faculty of willing or not willing and of sensitively desiring or averting according to its liking, §. 676, 689.",[]],"baum:719":["The faculty of desiring or averting sensitively according to its liking is sensitive choice (*arbitrium sensitivum*)@@1@@, the faculty of willing or not willing according to its liking is (free choice) freedom (*libertas*)@@2@@, cf. §. 707, 708, 710 (moral, so called simply). Freedom of purely willing or not willing is pure freedom@@3@@. Therefore a substance endowed with choice will have either only that sensitive choice, or only pure freedom, or the same mixed with sensitive choice, §. 718. Actions to which determining oneself through freedom is placed in the power of some substance are free@@4@@, and the substance itself, which and insofar as it can perform free actions, is free@@5@@.",[]],"baum:720":["I desire and avert many things sensitively according to my liking. Therefore I have sensitive choice, §. 216, 719. I will and not will many things according to my liking. Therefore I have freedom, §. 216, 719. Many of my actions, many actions of my soul, and the soul in many of its actions, are free. To all my volitions and nolitions something sensitive is admixed, §. 692. Hence pure freedom does not belong to me; in my freest actions my freedom is mixed with sensitive choice, §. 719. Both sensitive and free choice are actuated through the force of the soul representative of the universe according to the position of my body in it, §. 712, 667.",[]],"baum:721":["Voluntary actions (*actiones voluntariae*)@@1@@ are called 1) whichever are determined through the higher appetitive faculty; then involuntary@@2@@ are those which are not determined through the higher appetitive faculty. And in this sense all voluntary actions are free, not all free actions are voluntary, §. 719. For suppose that I determine myself through sensitive choice, where determining myself through freedom was placed in my power; an action of that kind will be involuntary, yet free, §. 719.",[]],"baum:722":["Voluntary actions@@1@@ are called 2) whichever are determined through the higher appetitive faculty in no way unwillingly. Then involuntary@@2@@ are those which I will unwillingly. In this sense all voluntary and involuntary actions are free actions, §. 719. But not all free actions are either voluntary or involuntary in this sense, §. 721.",[]],"baum:723":["With freedom the moral, broadly said@@1@@, cf. §. 787, is more closely connected. Hence free determinations are moral@@2@@, habits of free actions are moral@@3@@, laws of moral determinations are moral@@4@@, the philosophy and theology teaching them are moral, the state resulting from them is moral@@5@@. Hence morally possible (*moraliter possibile*) is 1) what can come about only through freedom, or in a free substance, as such, more broadly@@6@@, 2) what can come about only through freedom determined in conformity with moral laws, more strictly, or licit@@7@@. Morally impossible is 1) what on account of freedom alone cannot come about in a free substance, more broadly@@8@@, 2) what is impossible through freedom to be determined in conformity with moral laws, more strictly, or illicit@@9@@. Morally necessary is that whose opposite is morally impossible, therefore 1) that whose opposite is impossible only through freedom, or in a substance insofar as it is free, more broadly@@10@@, 2) that whose opposite is illicit, more strictly@@11@@. Moral necessitation is obligation@@12@@. Obligation to an unwilling action will be moral coercion@@13@@.",[]],"baum:724":["Moral necessity can exist in neither sense where there is no freedom, §. 723, therefore it does not remove freedom, nor is it its opposite, §. 81, but a grounded (*rationatum*) or consequent (*consectarium*), §. 14. Hence morally necessary, necessitated, and coerced actions can not only be free, but are such necessarily, §. 723. Indeed, a most universal moral law being posited@@1@@, i.e. one determining all free actions of all free substances, cf. §. 822, all free actions are morally necessary, or illicit, §. 723.",[]],"baum:725":["Minimal freedom is that actuating only a single volition or nolition according to minimal liking; therefore the more, the greater, and the greater the liking according to which it actuates, the greater it is, until it is maximal, actuating the greatest and most numerous volitions or nolitions according to the highest liking, §. 719. Therefore the more distinct the liking according to which I will or not will something, the more freely I will or not will it. Therefore the more I am conscious of my motives, the more freely I will, §. 712. That one willing or not willing most freely should perceive most distinctly the motives of willing or not willing, whenever he wills or does not will, is morally necessary, §. 723.",[]],"baum:726":["This is the law of choice: Of the things free with regard to execution, what pleases I desire, what pleases I avert. Hence the rule of freedom: Of the things free with regard to execution, what pleases I will, what pleases I do not will. My free actions, while they are determined according to liking, are not necessitated through their impulsive causes, stimuli or motives, by physical coercion, not external, §. 707, for stimuli and motives are my representations, §. 677, 690. Hence my internal determinations, §. 37. Nor are my free actions necessitated through their impulsive causes by internal physical coercion, while they are and remain, motives being posited, free with regard to execution, §. 711, 710. Indeed, motives and stimuli, speaking accurately, do not even act on the soul, hence do not coerce it, §. 714, nor necessitate it morally, §. 723, 701, while they are nothing but accidents of my soul, §. 505.",[]],"baum:727":["If I compel myself (*cogere*), it is placed in my power to determine myself (*determinare*) through freedom (*libertas*) to that to which I compel myself, whence I compel myself freely, §. 714, 719. If I am compelled by another externally in a certain respect (*externe secundum quid*), I am determined by a liking (*lubitus*) that is conceived as produced by another placed outside me, §. 714. But if it was then placed in my power to determine myself through freedom to that to which I am compelled externally in a certain respect, an action of this kind is nevertheless free, §. 719. Many of my actions, to which I compel myself or am compelled externally in a certain respect, are free.",[]],"baum:728":["Compulsions external in a certain respect (*coactiones externae secundum quid*) will be 1) productions of stimuli from the pleasing (*placentibus*), allurements (*illecebrae*)@@1@@, 2) from the displeasing, threats (*minae*)@@2@@, 3) of motives from the pleasing, persuasions (*suasiones*)@@3@@, 4) from the displeasing, dissuasions (*dissuasiones*)@@4@@, 5) the very actuation of the displeasing, to be continued until the one compelling can be certain of the outweighing (*superpondium*) that has ensued toward the unwilling action, extortion (*extorsio*)@@5@@, §. 714. Many of my actions, although I am compelled to them by threats and allurements, persuasions, dissuasions, and by extortions themselves, are nevertheless free, §. 727.",[]],"baum:729":["Through the system of preestablished harmony (*systema harmoniae praestabilitae*) all actions of the soul that are unwilling, to be produced by external compulsion simply taken (*coactionem externam simpliciter sumptam*) by any finite being, are removed, §. 714, 449, and in those compelled by external force in a certain respect, that very liking from which they arise, really produced by another finite being outside the soul, is removed, §. 727, 449, and insofar as through that system the soul, as never, so in none of its free actions, really suffers from any finite being, total independence (*independentia*) from the whole world and all of it is ascribed to the soul in this sense, §. 354, 307.",[]],"baum:730":["My free volitions and nolitions are called elicited acts (*actus eliciti*) of the soul, the free actions of the remaining faculties commanded acts (*actus imperati*), and insofar as they depend on the freedom of the soul, command (*imperium*) over them is ascribed to it. Hence the command of the soul over itself@@1@@ is the faculty (*facultas*) of producing, according to a distinct liking, now the actions of this, now of that faculty, now the opposite of producing them. Therefore the greater the freedom, the greater the command of the free being over itself, §. 725. A notable lack of command over oneself is moral servitude (*servitus moralis*) in the broad signification@@2@@. What makes toward increasing command over oneself is (free-born) liberal@@3@@, what promotes moral servitude is servile@@4@@.",[]],"baum:731":["If actions of the soul depend on freedom only through several intermediate acts, they are subject to it indirectly (mediately)@@1@@, but directly@@2@@ (immediately) are those that I do or omit through freedom without several observable other intermediate acts. Actions that are subject to freedom even most of all by way of the indirect are nevertheless free, §. 719.",[]],"baum:732":["The determinate proportion of the appetitive faculties (*facultates appetitivae*) among themselves in a certain subject is its disposition (*indoles*)@@1@@, either upright (*erecta*)@@2@@, the higher habitually dominating, or abject (*abiecta*)@@3@@, the lower habitually dominating, the greatest of which is called the dominant passion@@4@@. And since a certain proportion of them is borne more easily toward a certain genus of the appetible or aversible, various genera of temperaments of the soul@@5@@ are hence constituted. Therefore the temperament of the soul can be changed much and rather often by exercises and habit, §. 650, 577.",[]],"baum:733":["Many motions of my body depend on my choice (*arbitrium*), §. 14. Motions of the body depending on a choice of which I am conscious are called arbitrary (*arbitrarii*)@@1@@, §. 712, and arbitrary ones depending on a higher faculty are voluntary (*voluntarii*)@@2@@, §. 721. The government of the soul over the body@@3@@ (*regimen animae in corpus*) is the dependence of the latter's motions on the choice of the former. Hence my soul has government over my body.",[]],"baum:734":["In the arbitrary and voluntary motions of my body it can be sufficiently cognized (*cognosci*) from the soul why an event happens in the body, this and not at another time and place, §. 733. Therefore the soul acts on the body, §. 210, and flows into it (*influit*), §. 211.",[]],"baum:735":["In the affects of the soul a motion conforming to them coexists in the body, which, since it can be sufficiently cognized from the same, gives a new argument for the influx (*influxus*) of the soul into the body, §. 734.",[]],"baum:736":["In external sensations (*sensationes externae*) it can be sufficiently cognized from the force of the body why a certain change occurs in the soul. Therefore the body acts on the soul, §. 210, and flows into it, §. 211. Therefore there is between my soul and my body mutual influx, §. 734, 735, mutual harmony, §. 14, and commerce (*commercium*), §. 448.",[]],"baum:737":["I do not experience that the arbitrary motions of the body are sufficiently determined through a force proper to the body, and that sensations are sufficiently determined through a force proper to the soul. Whoever therefore assumes this major premise (*maior*): ›whatever I do not experience, does not exist‹, will conclude from it: ›Therefore arbitrary motions of the body are not sufficiently determined through a force proper to the body, and external sensations through a force proper to the soul. Hence the body really flows into the soul, the soul into the body, §. 212, by physical influx (*influxus physicus*), §. 450 (by experience).‹ If he holds this conclusion for a sensation, from it arise his own fallacies of the senses (*fallaciae sensuum*), the false major premise having been assumed, §. 548, through the fault of subreption (*vitium subreptionis*), §. 546.",[]],"baum:738":["Changes of the body depending on the soul, and of the soul depending on the body, are harmonic, §. 448. In the harmonic changes of the soul and the body, a change of the soul coexists with or succeeds a change of the body, and a change of the body coexists with or succeeds a change of the soul, §. 733-736. Whoever therefore assumes this major premise: ›Of those which coexist with or succeed one another, one really flows into the other‹, will conclude from this: ›therefore in harmonic changes the body really flows into the soul, the soul into the body (by experience)‹. If he holds this conclusion for a sensation, from this arise his own fallacies of the senses, §. 548, through the fault of subreption, §. 546.",[]],"baum:739":["My soul and my body constitute me one. Therefore they are united with each other, §. 73, 79. Their commerce, insofar as one human being endures through it, is union (*unio*), §. 205, which, insofar as it is exceedingly great, §. 734-736, and as great as it is not between my soul and any other body, §. 508, is the closest (*artissima*).",[]],"baum:740":["The human soul (*anima humana*)@@1@@ is the soul that is in the closest commerce with the human body. And since the soul with the body with which it is in the closest commerce constitutes an animal@@2@@: the human soul with the body with which it is in the closest commerce constitutes the animal that we call human being@@3@@.",[]],"baum:741":["The human soul represents (*repraesentat*) its body to itself at its choice, §. 740. Therefore it acts, §. 210, it also moves its body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it desires and averts, §. 712. Therefore it acts and is a representative force (*vis repraesentativa*) of its body, §. 210. The human body is matter (*materia*), §. 296, hence divisible, §. 427, and therefore internally changeable, §. 244, and therefore a finite being (*ens finitum*), §. 255, actual, a part of the world, §. 354. From the position (*positus*) of the human body in the universe it can be cognized why the human soul represents these things obscurely, clearly, distinctly, and not others, §. 740, 736. Therefore the human soul@@1@@ is a representative force of the universe according to the position of the human body in it, §. 513, 155.",[]],"baum:742":["For thinking (*cogitatio*) are required 1) perception of a thing, 2) of marks sufficient for distinguishing it, §. 524, 3) its very distinction, §. 67. But thinking is an accident, §. 191. Therefore it cannot exist except in a substance, or an aggregate of substances, §. 194. An aggregate of substances, from whose collected changes or accidents, of which none would yet be a thinking, a thinking would finally result in the whole, would be thinking matter (*materia cogitans*). This in the world would be an aggregate of finite substances, §. 354, of which one would contribute more to thinking than the rest, §. 272, from which it would suffer aids, §. 210, 321. But it will suffer these only ideally, §. 451, 463. Therefore in the world in every thinking matter there would be one substance in whose single force everything required for thinking, to be furnished by the finite ones, would have a sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*), §. 210, 212, and yet 1) this dominating substance of the given aggregate would never think through itself, which is against §. 30, 220, 2) everything required for thinking, to be furnished by the finite ones, would have a sufficient ground in the given dominating substance, by what has been demonstrated, and at the same time would not be actuated except finally through an aggregate of substances, i.e. would not have a sufficient ground except in the force of several finite substances, §. 210, q.a., §. 7. Thinking matter is impossible in the world. Whatever can think either is a substance, a monad (*monas*), §. 234, or a whole of which a substance that can think is a part. Therefore every soul is a substance, a monad, §. 504. Whatever can understand (*intelligere*) can think, §. 69. Therefore whatever can understand either is a substance, a monad, a spirit (*spiritus*), §. 402, or a whole of which a spirit is a part. A whole of spirits is a (moral person) mystical body (*corpus mysticum*). The intellectual soul is a spirit, §. 504, cf. §. 402, 296. The human soul is a substance, §. 740. Therefore a monad, a spirit, §. 741.",[]],"baum:743":["The human soul subsists through itself, §. 742, 192. Therefore it is not a substantiated phenomenon (*phaenomenon substantiatum*), §. 193. Since however it represents according to the position of the human body in the universe, which is in perpetual motion, §. 417, 296, and hence always changes its position, §. 283, 281, its representations are always changed, §. 512. Since these are internal determinations of the human soul, §. 37, 741, the human soul is an internally changeable substance, hence contingent, §. 202, and finite, §. 255. That every human soul is a finite and contingent substance is also evident thus: the human soul represents according to the position of the body, §. 741. Therefore it perceives some things distinctly, some less distinctly, §. 512. But now to conceive something distinctly is reality (*realitas*), §. 515, 531. Therefore the human soul has a degree of reality that is not the greatest, §. 161, hence a limit (*limes*), §. 248. Therefore it is a finite and contingent, §. 257, substance, §. 742.",[]],"baum:744":["The human soul (*anima humana*) cognizes (*cognoscit*), desires (*appetit*) and averts itself (*aversatur*), §. 741. These are partially diverse actions (*actiones*), §. 267. Therefore the human soul has partially diverse faculties (*facultates*), §. 216, which are not strictly called forces (*vires*), §. 197, 59, but rather are conceived through the single representative force (*vis repraesentativa*) of the soul, strictly so called, §. 521-720, nor are they posited outside one another, since accidents (*accidentia*), §. 191, are not outside their substance (*substantia*), §. 194, nor are they accurately said to act in one another, since action is not properly except of a substance, §. 210, much less to influence one another (*influere*), §. 211.",[]],"baum:745":["The human soul, a monad (*monas*), §. 742, contingent (*contingens*), §. 743, cannot arise except from nothing, §. 236, cannot perish except through annihilation (*annihilationem*), §. 237, is not extended, nor fills space (*spatium*), §. 241, i.e. is not in a place (*loco*) repletively, §. 241. Yet, coexisting with simultaneous things posited outside it, it is in space, §. 239, and in place, §. 281, such that other things posited outside it are nearer to it, others more remote, §. 288, 282. If the complex of simultaneous things nearer to the human soul is called its seat (*sedes*)@@1@@: the human body before other bodies, and certain parts of it before other members, are the seat of the soul, §. 409.",[]],"baum:746":["The human soul has no quantitative magnitude (*magnitudo quantitativa*), §. 744, 243, and is indivisible, §. 244. Perishing through division is physical corruption (*corruptio physica*)@@1@@. Therefore physical corruption is in itself impossible for the human soul, §. 15, 745, i.e. the human soul is absolutely incorruptible physically@@2@@.",[]],"baum:747":["The human soul does not indeed admit the threefold dimension of extended things, §. 290, 745, yet its philosophical and mathematical cognition (*cognitio*) is possible, as is that of the human body, §. 743, 249. Man consists of soul and body, finite, §. 741, 743, hence internally mutable, §. 740, is a finite and contingent being (*ens*), §. 202, 257. Therefore a philosophical and mathematical cognition of man is possible, §. 249, i.e. philosophical and mathematical anthropology (*anthropologia*), or anthropometry (*anthropometria*), as empirical anthropology is possible through experience. The complex of rules to be observed in cognizing man is anthropognosy (*anthropognosia*).",[]],"baum:748":["A finite being having been changed into an infinite one, an infinite would arise, §. 125, 227, nor would it be eternal, §. 302, which is absolutely impossible, §. 252. The change of man into an infinite being is apotheosis (*apotheosis*)@@1@@, therefore apotheosis is absolutely impossible.",[]],"baum:749":["The similarity (*similitudo*) and total equality (*aequalitas*) of human souls, even of only two, is impossible, §. 271, 272. Therefore total agreement (*convenientia*) or identity of the senses, imaginations, foresights, judgments, tastes, boredoms, pleasures, stimuli, motives, errors, desires, aversions, volitions, nolitions in many men, or in very few, is a chimera (*chimaera*), §. 590.",[]],"baum:750":["The human soul moves its body, §. 740, 734. Therefore it has the faculty (*facultas*) of moving something posited outside itself, i.e. the locomotive (*locomotiva*), §. 216, which, like the remaining faculties of the soul, §. 744, is actuated through its representative force (*vis repraesentativa*) of the universe according to the position of the body, §. 741, 417.",[]],"baum:751":["The human soul represents (*repraesentat*) to itself certain parts of this universe as singular, §. 740, 736, therefore as determined in every way (*omnimode determinatas*), §. 148. To the determination in every way of the parts of this world belongs their nexus with each of the remaining ones, §. 357. Therefore the human soul represents certain things to itself as connected with the single parts of the world. Now however one thing cannot be cognized as connected with another unless the other is cognized in some way or other, §. 14. Therefore the human soul perceives (*percipit*) the single parts of this universe, therefore it also perceives the single states (*status*) of this universe, §. 369.",[]],"baum:752":["The human soul, according to the position of its body, represents to itself the present state of the world, i.e. senses (*sentit*), §. 534, 751, the past, i.e. imagines (*imaginatur*), §. 557, the future, i.e. foresees (*praevidet*), §. 595. The sensations (*sensationes*) of the human soul are representations of single parts of the world simultaneous to it, less or more distinct, confused, or obscure, according as their objects are related to the human body, §. 751. The imaginations (*imaginationes*) of the human soul are representations of single past parts of the world, which preceded the sensing soul, or existed before that act of sensation; and these imaginations too are distinct, confused, obscure, more or less, according as their objects were related to the human body, §. 751. The foresights (*praevisiones*) of the human soul are representations of single future parts of the world, which will exist after the act of the sensing soul, less or more distinct, confused, obscure, according as their objects will be related to the human body, §. 751.",[]],"baum:753":["In the same way in which §. 752 proved of sensation, e.g., that it is in every human soul, this can be shown of the remaining acts of the soul, to be found in the human soul through experience, enumerated by empirical psychology (*psychologiam empiricam*), §. 576 and following.",[]],"baum:754":["The human soul, according to the position of its body, distinctly represents, §. 741. Therefore it understands (*intelligit*), §. 402, and is a spirit (*spiritus*), §. 742, 216. Hence it is again evident that all human souls are unequal and that one of them is the most perfect of all, §. 405. The human soul is a finite spirit, §. 743, a part of this world, §. 355.",[]],"baum:755":["Every spirit (*spiritus*) is a substance (*substantia*), §. 402. Therefore a force (*vis*), §. 199, hence the sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) of the inherence of certain accidents, §. 197, and thus an agent (*agens*), §. 210. And since it is an intellectual substance, §. 402, it has the faculty (*facultas*) of actuating certain distinct representations, §. 216. Therefore of determining its force toward them, §. 210, hence of desiring (*appetendi*), §. 663. Whatever it desires, it averts the opposite of it, hence a spirit has the faculty of averting (*aversandi*), §. 663. The desires and aversions of a spirit are its internal determinations, §. 37. Therefore they are in universal nexus with its remaining internal determinations, §. 49. Therefore also with the rest of its cognition, which is an internal determination, §. 37, and thus come about at the pleasure (*lubitus*) of the spirit, §. 712. Therefore a spirit has choice (*arbitrium*), §. 712, and, since its pleasure can be distinct, §. 402, free (*liberum*), or freedom (*libertatem*), §. 719.",[]],"baum:756":["The human soul is a spirit, §. 754. Therefore it has freedom, §. 755. And since spirituality (*spiritualitas*), intellectuality, personality, §. 641, 754, freedom, absolute simplicity, §. 744, and incorruptibility belong to it absolutely necessarily, §. 746, they are not of its modes (*modi*), §. 108, hence they are either essentials or attributes, because they are internal determinations, §. 37, 52. One of them being removed, the human soul would be removed, §. 63. Hence a human soul that could not at all distinctly conceive something, nor determine itself from the distinct by pleasure, losing all personality and freedom, consisting of several forces as parts to be posited outside one another, and to be physically corrupted, are chimeras (*chimaerae*), §. 590.",[]],"baum:757":["The human soul is immaterial and incorporeal, §. 744, 422. He who denies that the human soul is an immaterial substance is a materialist (*materialista*) in the psychological signification, cf. §. 395, and is mistaken, whether he holds it to be a mere accident of the body, §. 742, or a material atom, §. 429, or any most subtle corpuscle, §. 426. The universal materialist is also such in the psychological signification, §. 395, but not everyone who thinks the human soul a material substance is necessarily a universal materialist, §. 395.",[]],"baum:758":["The representative force (*vis repraesentativa*) of the universe according to the position of the human body in it, with its modes depending thereon, is the complex of the internal determinations of the soul, which are the principles of its mutations and of the accidents inhering in it, §. 751-755. Therefore the same force with its determinations depending on the position of the body is the nature (*natura*) of the human soul, §. 430. Hence whatever is actuated in the human soul through this force determined according to the laws and rules of the single faculties is natural (*naturale*) to the human soul, §. 470, insofar as it is contradistinguished from the supernatural (*supernaturali*), §. 469. In this signification the free and moral actions of the soul are also natural, §. 756, 755, although they are not merely natural, §. 709. Besides this proper signification of nature in the human soul, and hence of the natural in it, there are other improper and synecdochic ones, now arisen badly from a confusion of part with whole, now from the fact that a peculiar name has attached to what is conceivable from a peculiar mode, modification, or state of nature in the human soul, and then, the remaining natural things lacking a peculiar name, if they had to be contradistinguished from those that have obtained such a name, the name of the genus remained in the stricter signification. Thus e.g. connate things are sometimes called natural, contradistinguished from acquired things; natural things are contradistinguished from artificial, social, arbitrary, finally from those obtained through custom, e.g. cf. §. 710.",[]],"baum:759":["Those things in the human soul that succeed one another, natural to the human soul according to laws prescribed by its force, come about with respect to the human soul according to the course of nature (*cursum naturae*), §. 758, 471, and, together with those that coexist in the human soul according to the same laws, occur according to the order of nature (*ordinem naturae*) with respect to the human soul, §. 473. The preternatural things (*praeternaturalia*), which are not actuated through its representative force according to the position of its body, according to the laws and rules of its faculties, which if they are not actuated even through universal nature, are supernatural (*supernaturalia*), §. 474.",[]],"baum:760":["The human soul of the best world (*mundi optimi*) is in universal pneumatic nexus (*nexu universali pneumatico*), and in the greatest pneumatic-mechanical nexus, §. 464, 754. Therefore it is connected with the single spirits and bodies of the best world, §. 48, but unequally, §. 272. Therefore most with one body, with one spirit, §. 161.",[]],"baum:761":["Psychological systems (*systemata psychologica*) are opinions which seem apt for explaining the commerce (*commercium*) of soul and body in man. Therefore psychological systems are particular systems, §. 462, either simple or composite, §. 457. Of those none is possible, besides the system of pre-established harmony (*harmoniae praestabilitae*), of physical influx (*influxus physici*), and of occasional causes (*causarum occasionalium*), perhaps psychological, §. 458.",[]],"baum:762":["One of the simple general systems being posited, one of the simple psychological ones is also posited, §. 761, 457. Therefore universal pre-established harmony (*harmonia praestabilita*) having been demonstrated, §. 463, psychological pre-established harmony is demonstrated at the same time. Yet one of the simple psychological systems being posited, one, or a most similar one, of the general ones is not necessarily to be posited, §. 761, 457. If the human soul and body can be in the closest commerce (*commercio*) through pre-established harmony, they are also connected through pre-established harmony in the best world, §. 461, such that physical influx (*influxus physicus*) or the system of occasional causes (*systema causarum occasionalium*) are not to be admitted, except where commerce through pre-established harmony is impossible, §. 462.",[]],"baum:763":["The psychological system of physical influx (*systema influxus physici psychologicum*), because it is simple, posits that all harmonic changes of all human souls and bodies happen through physical influx, §. 457. Therefore if even a single harmonic change of one human soul or body can be proved possible through preestablished harmony (*harmonia praestabilita*) in the best world (*mundus optimus*): the system of physical influx can no longer be defended as having a place in the best world, §. 762. The psychological system of preestablished harmony, because it is simple, posits that all harmonic changes of all human souls and bodies happen through preestablished harmony; for the same reason the psychological system of occasional causes (*systema causarum occasionalium psychologicum*) posits that the same happen through infinite power alone, §. 457. Therefore if even a single harmonic change of one human soul or body were impossible in the best world through preestablished harmony: its psychological system could no longer be defended as having a place in the best world. If even a single harmonic change of one human soul or body is possible in the best world through preestablished harmony or physical influx: neither can the system of occasional causes any longer be defended as having a place in the best world, §. 762, 460. The psychological system of physical influx is no more evident through experience than the system of preestablished harmony and of occasional causes, §. 737, 738.",[]],"baum:764":["The psychological system of physical influx posits that the human soul influences its body, and the human body its soul, really in harmonic changes, §. 761, 450. Hence through the psychological system of physical influx 1) in no harmonic changes occurring in the human body does the body act by its own power, §. 212. Now all changes of the human body, as of a part of the world which the soul represents, can be sufficiently cognized from the power of the soul, §. 354, 751. Therefore all are harmonic, §. 448, and in none of its changes does the body act by its own power, according to the psychological system of physical influx, but in all it really suffers from the soul. If the body never acts, it does not react either, §. 213. Therefore through the system of physical influx there is in the world a transient action of the soul on the human body without reaction, whenever the soul acts on its body, which, were it to happen even once, would be contrary to §. 410.",[]],"baum:765":["Through the psychological system of physical influx it is posited 2) that the human body influences its soul really, whenever there occurs in the human soul a change which can be sufficiently cognized from the power of the body, §. 764, 448. Therefore in the harmonic changes met with in the soul, through the psychological system of physical influx, the body really influences, which nevertheless, through the same system, must be posited as never acting in any of its changes, §. 764. Through the system of physical influx the soul in sensing does not act at all, §. 212, although it represents to itself by its own power the present state of the world no less than the rest, §. 751.",[]],"baum:766":["The human body consists of elements, §. 420, monads, representing their world, from each of which its individual parts can be cognized, §. 400, hence also the individual changes of the soul, parts of the world, §. 354. Therefore every action of the soul is a harmonic change, §. 448, 22. Now every volition and nolition of the soul is an action of it, §. 210, 690. Therefore every volition and nolition of the human soul is a harmonic change of the same. Now through the psychological system of physical influx the human soul in none of its harmonic changes acts by its own power, but really suffers from the body. Therefore through the system of physical influx the soul in all its volitions and nolitions, doing nothing whatever, suffers from the body, which is contrary to freedom, §. 755.",[]],"baum:767":["Through the psychological system of occasional causes 1) the human body does not act in any of its harmonic changes, but an infinite being (*ens infinitum*) does, §. 761, 452, whence it is evident, in the same way as was shown in §. 764, that through the system of assistance the body acts in none of its changes, but only the infinite being. 2) Through the same system the human soul does not act in its harmonic changes, but the infinite being alone, §. 761, 452, whence it is evident, in the same way as was shown in §. 766, that through the psychological system of assistance the human soul does anything in none of its volitions and nolitions, and only really suffers in them from the infinite being, which likewise is contrary to freedom, §. 755.",[]],"baum:768":["Through the psychological system of preestablished harmony each part of the commerce (*commercium*) actualizes by its own power the harmonic changes occurring in itself, suffering ideally from the other part, §. 761, 448, hence through the psychological system of preestablished harmony it is posited that 1) the arbitrary motions of the body, no less than the merely natural and vital ones, are sufficiently determined through the mechanism of the body itself and of the bodies surrounding it, 2) that the sensations of the soul, no less than its freest thoughts whatever, are sufficiently determined through its representative power, §. 758, 433.",[]],"baum:769":["Suppose a psychological harmonist is about to give the ground of some harmonic change in man, and seeks it 1) from the power of that very part of the commerce in which the harmonic change is observed, 2) from the other part of the commerce, which he himself supposes to influence ideally, 3) from the infinite power, which he holds to concur; suppose, I say, that he errs, he has most certainly given the true ground, §. 448, 450, even if through the hypothesis he has added a false one. Suppose that in the same case the influxionist or occasionalist errs, he has not only passed over a great part of the true ground, but has also denied it, §. 450, 452.",[]],"baum:770":["When a man is conceived, the soul, which is to be in the closest commerce with his body, such as it is in men on this earth, either already preexisted, or begins to exist only in the very conception, or a little after it. He who posits the first is called a preexistentian@@1@@.",[]],"baum:771":["Those who posit that the existence of the human soul begins only in the very conception, or a little after it, either want it to originate from the parents and are called traducians@@1@@, or admit that it then first exists out of nothing, and are called inducians@@2@@ (infusians, coexistentians).",[]],"baum:772":["He who posits the human soul as actualized out of nothing is called a creatian@@1@@. Hence an inducian is a creatian, §. 771. A preexistentian can be a creatian, §. 770. A traducian is not a creatian, and vice versa, §. 771. Traducians, whether they derive the soul that is to originate from the soul of the parents, as a little flame from a flame, or from their body, are bound to admit that a new soul originates either from a simple part of the parents, or from a composite one, §. 224. If the former: the soul does not originate, but preexists, §. 227; if the latter: the new soul will not be a monad, but a composite strictly so called, §. 225, which is contrary to §. 756, 757. The soul cannot originate except out of nothing, §. 745. Therefore it does not originate from the parents, §. 228.",[]],"baum:773":["If the propagation of human souls by transmission (*per traducem*)@@1@@ is said to be the union of ever other and other human souls with the body, such as it is in men on this earth, through the continuous succession of parents and offspring in the world, by which the human race, such as it is on this earth, is propagated in such a way that the souls pass over with the bodies of the offspring and are transferred from the bodies of the parents into their own proper places, where they dwell more separately, it can be conceived, 1) as at the same time involving the origin of the soul, i.e. that the soul, which with the intervening act of conception is about to come into the closest commerce with the body, such as it is in man on this earth, is posited at the same time to originate, and this again either from the parents, on which §. 772, or by the parents, by assuming that God can implant a determinate power of the human soul, by which it can produce out of nothing only some individuals of its own species, his creative power concurring and furnishing very many realities in the soul that is to originate, only some quite limited realities proceeding from the soul of the parents. One positing this opinion can be called a concreatian@@2@@.",[]],"baum:774":["The propagation of human souls by transmission can be conceived, 2) as not involving the origin of the soul, but a preexisting soul is posited to have already existed before the intervening act of conception in the closest commerce with a part of the seed, or more accurately a spermatic animalcule, and so backwards through several generations, the representative power of the soul itself gradually increased and evolved, in proportion as the body of the chosen spermatic animalcule, which was to come forth onto a greater stage, was brought ever nearer and nearer to its great transformation and growth, §. 773.",[]],"baum:775":["The origin of the soul and the propagation of the soul by transmission can be distinguished, §. 773, 771. Those too who posit the origin of the soul by transmission from the parents can be distinguished from those who posit that the same is to be derived by the parents, §. 773, 772. Therefore not everyone who admits transmission, however understood, is guilty of psychological materialism, §. 772, 757.",[]],"baum:776":["As long as even a single smallest change naturally inheres in a natural being, or generally a single smallest accident, its nature lasts, §. 469, 29, and the being lives, §. 430. But if all changes natural to the being, or generally all accidents naturally inhering in it, are ended or cease, its nature is ended, §. 469, 23, and the being dies, §. 430. Therefore the life of the human body@@1@@, or the duration of its nature, by which it is strong as a human body, is continued while even a single smallest vital or arbitrary motion survives, natural to it, as it is a human body, §. 733, 740. The death of the human body@@2@@, or the end of its nature, by which it is strong as a human body, is the cessation of vital and arbitrary motions of every kind. Therefore death as defined in §. 556 is the death of the human body.",[]],"baum:777":["Since an animal is composed of soul and body, with which that soul is in the closest commerce, §. 740, its nature is composed of the nature of the body and of the soul placed in the closest commerce, §. 430. Hence while certain harmonic changes of the soul and body placed in the closest commerce exist, the animal lives@@1@@. The cessation of all harmonic actions of the soul and body placed in the closest commerce is the death of the animal@@2@@. Now through the death of the body all harmonic changes of the soul and of the dying body placed in the closest commerce cease, §. 776, 448. Therefore the death of the body is also the death of the animal, the death of the human body the death of the man, §. 740. Therefore death as defined in §. 556 is the death of the man, §. 776.",[]],"baum:778":["Animal death (*mors animalis*) is either absolute@@1@@, the cessation of all the alterations of the soul harmonic with the whole animal body, or only respective@@2@@ (animal transformation and metamorphosis), the cessation of the harmonic alterations of the soul with only some certain body, with which it had been in the closest commerce (*commercium*). The death of a human being is either absolute or only respective, i.e. when the body dies, such as we experience human beings to have in these lands, either all the closest commerce of the human soul with some body ceases, or it comes into a new commerce of this kind with another, §. 776, 777.",[]],"baum:779":["Since daily certain parts of the human body cease to be in the closest commerce with the soul, and certain ones come into it, it is evident in what sense a human being can be said to die daily, to revive daily, §. 777. The opinion by which the death of a human being is posited to be nothing but animal transformation is called the exile of (absolute) death (*exsilium mortis*)@@1@@.",[]],"baum:780":["The life of the human soul (*vita animae humanae*)@@1@@, or the duration of its nature, is continued as long as even a single smallest accident (*accidens*) survives that is natural to it as it is a human soul, §. 776. Now, however, as long as the human soul lasts, there is in it perception or representation of the state of the world and of its own past, present and future, §. 742, 400, which in accordance with the position of a certain body can be distinct, §. 741, an accident, §. 191, natural to it, §. 758. Therefore the human soul, as long as it exists, lives. The death of the human soul@@2@@, or the end of its nature, is at the same time that of its existence. Now all existence, nature, life of the human soul is in itself contingent, §. 743, 430. Therefore the death of the human soul is in itself possible, §. 81, 104.",[]],"baum:781":["The mortal (*mortale*)@@1@@ is what can die, and the possibility of dying is mortality (*mortalitas*)@@2@@. What cannot die is the immortal (*immortale*)@@3@@, and the impossibility of dying is immortality (*immortalitas*)@@4@@. Mortality and immortality are either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. Not only absolute but also a sufficiently great hypothetical mortality belongs to the human body and to the human being, §. 777, 168; to the soul none but absolute, and hypothetical of being annihilated, §. 745, 780. Immortality that is absolute cannot indeed be attributed to the soul, §. 780, yet because in the six hundred ways in which a body can die it, being indestructible, can not die, §. 746, a quite great hypothetical immortality belongs to it. No substance of this world, §. 354, 358, is annihilated, §. 227, 228. Therefore the human soul, when the body dies, such as human beings have in these lands, survives and lives immortally, §. 780, 742. The Thnetopsychites@@5@@, who posit that the human soul dies with the body, err.",[]],"baum:782":["The human soul after death (of the body and of the human being, such as we experience in these lands) preserves spirituality, freedom, personality, §. 781, 756. If you were to call the preserved intellectual memory of itself immortality, in this signification too the human soul is immortal, §. 781, 641. As to its use, either its total perceptions for a longer time are none but sensitive, or it soon obtains intellectual ones. Those who posit the former are the Hypnopsychites@@1@@, who, if they deny that the soul ever understands after the death of this body, are Psychopannychites@@2@@. The human soul before death had cognized something clearly or distinctly, §. 754. This reality, §. 520, never wholly sterile, §. 517, as it is reality, has indefinitely no consequences but realities, §. 140, and is indefinitely in universal nexus with the spirituality, understanding, reason of the soul, again realities, §. 531, 49, which, as such, have no consequences but realities, indefinitely, §. 23, 140. Hence the nexus between the clear or distinct cognition of this life and the spirituality, understanding, reason of the soul is again a reality having real consequences indefinitely, §. 140. But the natural real consequences of all these cannot be increased indefinitely, §. 23, 162, without distinct perceptions, §. 631, ratiocinations, §. 642, volitions or nolitions, §. 665, 690. Hence the human soul after death, preserving its nature, §. 780, 781, naturally, at least finally, also exerts an intellectual life by spiritual actions, §. 639, and the Psychopannychites err.",[]],"baum:783":["The human soul after death, understanding, either exerts personality too so far that it distinctly recollects its state in this life, or less so, §. 782. Those who posit the latter can be called advocates of the Lethean cup (*patroni lethaei poculi*)@@1@@. The former is more natural, §. 583, 561.",[]],"baum:784":["The state of the human soul after death can be considered, 1) with the absolute death of the human being posited, §. 778. Then the human soul after death is posited deprived of every body, or that there is no body with which it would again have come into the closest commerce, §. 742, 740. 2) With only the respective death of the human being posited, so that it is again sent into the closest commerce with a new body, §. 778. The beginning of a new closest commerce with a new body is called palingenesis (*palingenesia*)@@1@@ (regeneration, metensomatosis and metempsychosis taken more broadly). Those who posit this either at the same time patronize the Lethean cup and posit a new body, such as are known in these lands, or posit neither of these with it. The former defend metempsychosis more strictly so called and crude@@2@@ improbably, §. 783.",[]],"baum:785":["The human soul, lasting after the death of the body that it had in these lands, §. 781, still represents the individual parts of this universe, §. 752, 780, therefore also the bodies of the same, §. 155. The bodies simultaneous with it act on it, are acted on by it, it is acted on by the bodies simultaneous with it, flows into it, §. 408, but it does not flow equally into two of them, nor is it acted on equally by two of them, §. 272. Therefore there is one body with which the human soul, lasting after the death of the body, comes into the closest commerce, §. 448, 739. Hence if a soul of this kind is called separated, this is to be understood respectively to the body such as we experience human beings to have in these lands, and the death of a human being is nothing but animal transformation, §. 779, and palingenesis, which however would be wrongly confused with crude metempsychosis, §. 784.",[]],"baum:786":["The human soul, lasting after the death of this body, is in the closest commerce with another, §. 785. That new body in its different states will be now less, now more congruent with this body, §. 270, 265. Therefore there will be some state of it in which it will be most congruent with the body that had been in the closest commerce with the soul in this life, §. 161, hence the same, §. 70.",[]],"baum:787":["Just as the perfection of a finite spirit is 1) either absolutely necessary or contingent, §. 147, 2) either natural or supernatural, §. 496, 3) either internal or external, §. 98, 4) either moral broadly speaking or less so, §. 723: so also the goods for a spirit, with which posited that is posited, §. 100, are 1) either metaphysical or contingent, §. 147, 2) either natural or supernatural, §. 469, 3) either domestic or adventitious, §. 660, 4) either moral broadly speaking or less such, §. 723. The broadly moral goods are connected more closely with the freedom of a given spirit, either as grounds (*rationes*), §. 14, a priori, §. 24, and as antecedents of a certain state of the same, or as what is grounded (*rationata*), §. 14, a posteriori, §. 24, and as consequences of a certain state of the same, §. 596, or on both sides, §. 24. What depends more closely on a given freedom is called strictly moral@@1@@ and sometimes simply so. Hence the goods that depend more closely on a spirit's freedom are strictly moral@@2@@, and the perfection to be posited with these posited is blessedness (*beatitudo*)@@3@@. The complex of perfections belonging to a spirit is happiness (*felicitas*)@@4@@. The complement of blessedness to the happiness of a finite spirit is prosperity (*prosperitas*)@@5@@, and the goods with which posited it is posited are the prosperous (*prospera*)@@6@@ (physical, strictly so called). The happiness of a finite spirit is the complex of prosperity and blessedness.",[]],"baum:788":["The evils of a spirit depending more closely on its freedom are strictly moral@@1@@, §. 787 (evils of guilt, sins), the broadly moral evils@@2@@ are those connected more closely with the freedom of a spirit. Of these, the imperfection is moral corruption broadly speaking@@3@@, of those, strictly so called@@4@@. The complex of imperfections belonging to a spirit is unhappiness (*infelicitas*)@@5@@. The complement of strictly moral corruption to the unhappiness of a finite spirit is misery (*miseria*)@@6@@, and the evils with which posited it is posited are damages broadly so called@@7@@ (physical evils, strictly so called). Unhappiness is the complex of misery and moral corruption.",[]],"baum:789":["The human soul in this life@@1@@, i.e. while it lives in the closest commerce with the body, such as we experience human beings to have in these lands, is continually altered, §. 418, 754. None of these alterations is objectively indifferent absolutely, §. 654. Therefore all are either good or evil, or both. Those alterations of the soul that are good and evil at the same time, as all are, §. 264, are either good and evil at the same time equally, or unequally, §. 70. Those more good than evil posit more of happiness than of unhappiness, those more evil than good posit more of unhappiness than of happiness, and so, to be denominated from the predominant, the former are good, the latter evil, §. 787, 788.",[]],"baum:790":["Posit a thing totally evil as much as it is good: it would not have the highest degree of reality, §. 246, 248. Therefore it would be a finite being, §. 248, hence contingent, §. 257, and so its opposite would be possible, §. 101, and since its existence would be contingent, §. 109, the existence of its opposite would be possible, §. 101. But the opposite of a thing of this kind could not exist except outside it, §. 81, 7, and the good and evil would be totally equal to the posited thing itself, §. 81, 267. Therefore there would be two possibles, actual outside one another, totally equal, which is against §. 272. Therefore a thing that would be only totally good as much as evil cannot exist. Therefore there are no alterations of the human soul but either good or evil. Therefore every human soul in this life is either happy or unhappy, §. 789.",[]],"baum:791":["The human soul (*anima humana*) lasting after the death of the body continues to be changed continuously, §. 781, 782. Therefore at any moment of its duration its happiness (*felicitas*), or unhappiness (*infelicitas*), is increased, §. 790, 162. Therefore the human soul lasting after the death of the body will either enjoy a greater happiness than in this life, and is blessed@@1@@, or will labor under a greater unhappiness, and is damned@@2@@. Beatitude (*beatitudo*), which the soul, once entered upon after this life, is posited, will either coexist with the soul while it lasts, or damnation (*damnatio*) will at some time succeed it. Damnation, which the soul, once entered upon after this life, is posited, will either coexist with the soul while it lasts, or beatitude will at some time succeed it, §. 790, 789. The former in each case is more natural, §. 139, 140.",[]],"baum:792":["Since every soul is that in a being which can be conscious to itself of something, §. 504, it has a cognitive faculty (*facultas cognoscitiva*), §. 519, and this either only a lower one, or also a higher one, §. 520, 524. The former will be a merely sensitive soul@@1@@. An animal which has a merely sensitive soul is a brute@@2@@; one whose soul is a spirit is a rational animal@@3@@. Therefore man is a rational animal, §. 754, 740.",[]],"baum:793":["The souls of brutes are in the closest commerce (*commercium*) with the animal body, §. 740, hence they represent (*repraesentant*) their body clearly and obscurely, §. 792, 736. Therefore they are representative forces of the universe according to the position of the body of the brutes in it, §. 741, hence substances, §. 198, monads, §. 234, simple beings, §. 230, they do not have parts outside parts, §. 224, finite, §. 202, 792, indivisible, §. 244, hence physically incorruptible, §. 746, immaterial and incorporeal, §. 422, furnished with the faculties of sensation, imagination, foresight and the rest, to be actualized without distinct cognition through the representative force of the universe according to the position of the body, §. 792, hence to be impelled by sensitive appetite and aversion, §. 667, and by choice, §. 718, by instincts, flights, and stimuli, §. 677, even to affects, §. 678.",[]],"baum:794":["The easier questions, whether brutes have a soul, whether a rational one, §. 792, 793, are not to be confused with these: 1) whether all the bodies on this earth, the phenomena, besides the human, are destitute of a soul, §. 504, 2) whether some of them are the seat of a rational soul, §. 745, 3) whether all that are animal bodies are either already now enjoying some use of reason, or will at last enjoy it, §. 782, 639.",[]],"baum:795":["The souls of brutes are destitute of understanding (*intellectus*), §. 792. Therefore they are not spirits, §. 402, they lack personality, §. 641, reason, §. 640, will, nill, §. 690, and freedom, §. 719, nor are they immortal, like human souls, §. 781-784, nor capable of happiness or of unhappiness, whether now or at some time, §. 787, 788.",[]],"baum:796":["Spirits endowed with a greater essential degree of understanding than that which is man's are superior@@1@@, those with a lesser, inferior@@2@@. Both are finite, either happy or unhappy, §. 790, the former agathodaemons@@3@@ (calodaemons), the latter cacodaemons@@4@@.",[]],"baum:797":["Every finite spirit, whether it is superior to man or inferior, when actual has a body with which it is in the closest commerce, §. 785, 796, either constantly gravitating toward a certain center outside itself, or less so. The former is the total inhabitant of a certain body in the universe, and of one either fixed or wandering, e.g.",[]],"baum:798":["Whatever degree of understanding a finite superior spirit has had, it nevertheless does not have the greatest, §. 248, hence it does not represent all things most distinctly, §. 637, but in such a way that it can be cognized from its body why it represents these things, not others, more purely, more deeply, more distinctly, confusedly, obscurely, §. 797, 512. Therefore every finite spirit has a lower cognitive faculty, §. 520.",[]],"baum:799":["No finite spirit, whether it has been superior to man or inferior, can be physically corrupted, §. 746; hence even if one of its bodies, indeed the greatest, is dissolved, it will nevertheless survive, unless it is annihilated, §. 745, while it lasts it preserves personality, with the use of understanding, reason, freedom increasing rather than decreasing or ceasing altogether, §. 782, and it is more natural for it to remember distinctly its past state than the Lethaean cup, §. 783. Therefore every finite spirit is by its nature, i.e. through its representative force of the universe, §. 782, immortal, like the human soul, §. 781, 783, more naturally enduring, while it lasts, in the beatitude or damnation which it has once entered upon, §. 787, 791, than that one once remarkably blessed should be damned, or vice versa, §. 791.",[]],"baum:800":["Natural theology (*theologia naturalis*) is the science (*scientia*) of God, insofar as he can be cognized without faith.",[]],"baum:801":["Natural theology contains the first principles of practical philosophy, of teleology and of revealed theology. Therefore it is referred, §. 2, with reason to metaphysics, §. 1.",[]],"baum:802":["Natural theology considers 1) the concept of God, 2) his operations.",[]],"baum:803":["The most perfect being (*ens perfectissimum*)@@1@@ is that to which belongs the highest perfection among beings: i.e. that in which as many things, as great, to such a degree, agree in as many and as great things, as many, as great, to what degree can agree in the most and the greatest of the possibles in any being, §. 185. There is therefore in the most perfect being a certain plurality absolutely necessarily, §. 74.",[]],"baum:804":["The predicates of the most perfect being are called its perfections. In the most perfect being there are as many perfections agreeing in the highest degree as can be in a being at the same time, as many as are compossible, §. 803.",[]],"baum:805":["The perfection of every most perfect being is as great as can be in any being, §. 803, 804.",[]],"baum:806":["The most perfect being is a real being, §. 803, 135. Therefore to it belongs as great a reality (*realitas*) as can be in a being. The most perfect being is the most real, §. 805, 804, in which are the most and the greatest realities, the highest good and the best metaphysically, §. 190.",[]],"baum:807":["All realities are truly positive, nor is any negation a reality, §. 36. Therefore even if all were united in a being to the greatest degree, a contradiction would never arise from them, §. 13. Therefore all realities are compossible in a being. Now the most perfect being is the most real of beings, §. 806. Therefore to the most perfect being belongs the totality of realities, and of those, the greatest, that can be in any being, §. 805, 190.",[]],"baum:808":["With reality posited, negation is removed, §. 36. Now in the most perfect being all realities are to be posited, §. 807. Therefore all negations are to be removed.",[]],"baum:809":["In everything impossible something is to be posited and removed at the same time, §. 7. This is either a negation or a reality, §. 36, 9. In the most perfect being no reality is to be removed, §. 807. In the most perfect being no negation is to be posited, §. 808. Therefore nothing in the most perfect being is to be removed and posited at the same time. The most perfect being is possible, §. 8.",[]],"baum:810":["Existence (*exsistentia*) is a reality compossible with the essence and the remaining realities, §. 66, 807. Therefore the most perfect being has existence, §. 807.",[]],"baum:811":["God (*deus*)@@1@@ is the most perfect being. Therefore God is actual, §. 810, 55.",[]],"baum:812":["The predicates of God are perfections, §. 811, 804, all the greatest realities, §. 807. His very possibility is the greatest, 1) internal and absolute, §. 165, while in him the most and the greatest things are compossible, §. 805, 807, 2) external and hypothetical, while the most fruitful and weightiest grounds in him have, in every nexus of all possible worlds, things grounded, which can be outside God, the most fruitful, the weightiest, §. 168.",[]],"baum:813":["In God there are no negations, §. 808, therefore neither negations strictly so called, nor privations, §. 137, nor metaphysical evil, nor contingent, §. 146, nor physical, nor moral in whatever signification, §. 788.",[]],"baum:814":["In the concept of God marks (*notae*) that are wholly negative are impossible, §. 525, but also partially such truly, §. 813, 808, although to us many things in him that are most real seem to be negative, §. 12, because we partly express negations in affirmative terms, partly do not sufficiently remember to affirm a negation of a negation, §. 36, 81.",[]],"baum:815":["The perfections of God are either in him absolute, or respective, and among these some relative, §. 37: or they can be represented by us without the mark of action, quiescent@@1@@, or they cannot, operative@@2@@.",[]],"baum:816":["The internal perfection of God from which all the remaining internal ones can finally be so deduced that it itself cannot afterwards be deduced from another internal perfection of God by those who are to avoid a circle, is the first concept of God, §. 40, 39. Now from the infinite perfections of God such a deduction of the remainder is possible, §. 24, 49, while each is the greatest ground, §. 812, 166, and the most sufficient, §. 169, hence also ultimate simply, §. 170, the greatest essence, §. 171. Therefore there are infinitely many first concepts of God, of which nevertheless each one, taken for the essence, is the single essence of God, §. 40, 77.",[]],"baum:817":["Although in God there is the greatest nexus, §. 808, 167, and the most universal, §. 172, so that in him from all things all things most truly, §. 876: nevertheless from one perfection of God his remaining perfections are more easily cognized by us than from another, §. 527. Therefore it is preferable to take for the essence that one from which we hope to deduce the remainder most easily, §. 816. So far is it from being the case that every plurality in God is impossible, that rather in his very essence and through it a certain plurality is posited absolutely necessarily, §. 812, 816.",[]],"baum:818":["God, besides the essence, whichever you have taken, §. 817, is determined, as to the remaining internal perfections also, §. 816, as much as anything can be determined as internal, §. 812. Therefore God is actual, §. 54.",[]],"baum:819":["God, as to existence, is determined, §. 818, 811. Therefore God is a being, §. 61, 57, neither a negative nor a privative non-being, §. 7, 54.",[]],"baum:820":["From the being able of God to the being of the same the consequence holds, i.e. his existence is sufficiently determined through his essence, §. 809-811, and indeed the greatest, §. 805, the complement, §. 55, eternal, §. 302, of the complex of the most and the greatest affections compossible in any being, or of the greatest essence, §. 816, whichever you have taken, §. 817.",[]],"baum:821":["Unity (*unitas*), §. 819, 73, the greatest, §. 812, belongs to God (*deus*), the inseparability of very many greatest realities (*realitates*), §. 173; hence it is evident, 1) how it can be called eminently perfect unity, which can stand in place of an example, and how even those things seen as most sterile in the perfections of God are nevertheless his essence (*essentia*), §. 816, 2) that, with the highest unity of God posited, a certain plurality of inseparables in the same is not only not removed but also posited, §. 74.",[]],"baum:822":["Transcendental truth (*veritas transcendentalis*), §. 819, 90, the greatest, §. 812, the highest order (*ordo*) of his perfections, §. 175, 89, the highest possibility (*possibilitas*), §. 812, the highest nexus (*nexus*), §. 817, the highest agreement (*convenientia*) with the strongest rules, §. 179, 184, such as the law of the best in general: let the best be joined to the best, §. 482, 187, and of the best among entities: let the best of the compossibles in the most perfect entity be conjoined with the best, §. 803, belong to God (*deus*).",[]],"baum:823":["God, if not actual (*actualis*), would be an entity (*ens*) enjoying all realities, to which nevertheless something would be lacking, §. 66, 812, determined, as to all internal perfections, so far as an entity can be determined as to the internal, §. 818, and yet, as to some, not so determined, §. 54. Therefore the opposite of divine existence (*exsistentia*) is in itself impossible, §. 15. The existence of God is absolutely necessary, §. 102. God is a necessary entity, §. 109, whose highest existence, §. 820, is his essence (*essentia*), §. 819, with §. 817 nevertheless preserved.",[]],"baum:824":["If God were not actual (*actualis*), the principle of contradiction (*principium contradictionis*) would be false, §. 823, 7, the first principle both of form and of matter in all our demonstrations. Therefore although many sciences (*scientiae*) can be completely demonstrated without any theological premise, §. 1-800: yet either they themselves or their objects would not be, e.g. §. 61, 354, 504, indeed they would not even be possible, §. 8, unless God were actual.",[]],"baum:825":["God is a necessary entity (*ens necessarium*), §. 823, 824. In a necessary entity there are no modes (*modi*), §. 111. Therefore in God there are no modes, i.e. predicable accidents (*accidentia praedicabilia*).",[]],"baum:826":["If we discover in a necessary entity (*ens necessarium*) something which is partially similar to, partially different from, what is represented in a contingent entity, yet we do not sufficiently understand the differences, nor find a name peculiar to it: we call that of it which we noted as similar in the contingent entity its analogue (*analogon*), and it is attributed to God by analogy (*per analogiam*), if realities seem to reign in its concept, by eminence@@1@@ (*per eminentiam*, excellence), if negations, by reduction@@2@@ (*per reductionem*, the way of negation, *via negationis*).",[]],"baum:827":["If we discover in a necessary entity (*ens necessarium*) certain things which, to be hypothetically necessary is absolutely necessary, insofar as, as to hypothetical necessity, they are similar to modes (*modi*), §. 108, they are called analogues of modes@@1@@, §. 826, insofar as for them to exist in God, hypothetically necessary, is absolutely necessary through the essence, §. 823, 54, they are attributes (*attributa*), §. 107. Hence the divine attributes can be divided into those more similar to the attributes of finite things, and to the modes of finite things. The latter too are true attributes, since the sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) is in the essence of God; 1) of their existence, insofar as it is contradistinguished from indetermination, §. 54. A contingent entity can be, as to many things, indeterminate, §. 34, 134. A necessary entity, as to none, and indeed through the essence, §. 820. 2) of the duration, eternity, and immutability of those things which are once in God. In contingent entities the essential limit is the sufficient ground why they must be continuously changed, internally, while they exist. In a necessary entity essential infinity is the sufficient ground of the opposite: 3) why other things in place of these could have existed in God from eternity to eternity, and hence, 4) why the existence of these, not of others, is determined thus, not otherwise, only through hypothetical necessity, §. 102.",[]],"baum:828":["Holiness@@1@@ (*sanctitas*) is the reality (*realitas*) of an entity by which several of its imperfections truly such are removed, hence the most holy@@2@@ (*sanctissimum*) is that by whose reality all its imperfections are removed. Now through the allness (*omnitudo*) of the greatest realities in God all imperfections in the same are removed, §. 142, 808. Therefore God is most holy.",[]],"baum:829":["In God there is no imperfection (*imperfectio*), §. 828. Therefore neither essential nor accidental, neither internal nor external, §. 121, 808. The same is evident from §. 813.",[]],"baum:830":["In God there is a sufficient ground (*ratio sufficiens*) why his perfections inhere in him, §. 822, 823. Therefore force (*vis*) strictly so called, §. 197. Hence God is a substance (*substantia*), §. 199.",[]],"baum:831":["God has force (*vis*), §. 830, the greatest, §. 812. Therefore one sufficient for actualizing very many greatest accidents (*accidentia*), §. 203.",[]],"baum:832":["Accidents (*accidentia*) do not exist outside their substances (*substantiae*), §. 194. Therefore a force (*vis*) sufficient for actualizing very many greatest accidents suffices for actualizing very many substances. Therefore for all, §. 191, 247. A force sufficient for actualizing something is power@@1@@ (*potentia*). Hence omnipotence@@2@@ (*omnipotentia*) is a force sufficient for actualizing all things. God is omnipotent, §. 831.",[]],"baum:833":["Absolutely impossible things (*absolute impossibilia*) are nothing, §. 7. Therefore one who could do absolutely impossible things would be able to do nothing, §. 469. God can do all things, §. 832. Therefore the omnipotence of God is not extended to absolutely impossible things. Impossible to someone is that for the actualizing of which his forces do not suffice, §. 469. In this signification nothing is impossible to God. Therefore every thing impossible to us and to everything finite is possible to God, §. 832.",[]],"baum:834":["Miracles (*miracula*) are possible, §. 475. The force of God suffices for actualizing all possible things, §. 832. Therefore God can perform strict miracles (*miracula rigorosa*), §. 477, 833.",[]],"baum:835":["God can actualize all possible worlds (*mundi possibiles*), §. 832. Therefore both the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect without exceptions, §. 442.",[]],"baum:836":["God is a substance (*substantia*), §. 830, and does not have modes (*modi*), §. 825. Therefore he is a necessary substance, §. 202, and does not have an internal state, §. 206, nor is he modifiable, §. 209. Whence it is again evident that the world cannot be a modification of God, §. 388.",[]],"baum:837":["All origin and perishing, origin out of nothing and annihilation of a necessary substance (*substantia necessaria*), are absolutely impossible; God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore all origin and perishing in God is absolutely impossible, §. 227, 228.",[]],"baum:838":["Every substance (*substantia*) is a monad (*monas*), §. 234. God is a substance, §. 830. Therefore he is a monad, and a simple entity (*ens simplex*), §. 230. With the highest simplicity of God posited, it is indeed removed that he is in any way composed of parts outside parts, §. 224, but the most real, §. 807, diversity of several things in God, §. 817, is not removed, because even in finite things it is false that all really diverse things are posited outside one another, §. 755.",[]],"baum:839":["God is a necessary entity (*ens necessarium*), §. 823, 824. The determinations (*determinationes*) of every necessary entity are absolutely and internally immutable, §. 132. Therefore God is absolutely and internally immutable, §. 126, 127. The same is evident thus: if God were absolutely and internally mutable, at least some internal perfection of his could exist after another, §. 124. Hence the existence of that which could have existed before it could be removed. This is a reality internal to God, §. 37. Therefore some of the realities (*realitates*) of God could be separated from others, §. 72, which is against the highest unity of God, §. 821.",[]],"baum:840":["If God were composed (*compositus*), he would be extended, §. 241, and the force of inertia (*vis inertiae*) would have to be attributed to him, §. 832, 294. Hence he would be matter (*materia*), §. 295. Therefore divisible, §. 427, and thus internally mutable, §. 244, 126, which is against §. 839. Therefore God is a simple entity (*ens simplex*), §. 224. And since he is a substance, §. 830, he is a monad (*monas*), §. 230. It is again evident that the universal materialists err, §. 395.",[]],"baum:841":["In God there are not simultaneous things posited outside one another, his parts, §. 840, hence neither space (*spatium*), §. 239. Therefore God is not extended, nor does he fill space in the signification in which extended things are said to fill it, §. 241.",[]],"baum:842":["God does not have quantitative magnitude (*magnitudo quantitativa*), §. 838, 243, although he is the greatest entity, §. 161, of very many greatest realities, §. 807, 812. It is again evident that God is indivisible, and indeed absolutely, §. 244.",[]],"baum:843":["The allness (*omnitudo*) of the greatest realities which can be is the greatest degree of reality (*gradus realitatis*), §. 247, 248. This belongs to God, the most real entity (*ens realissimum*), §. 807, 812. Therefore God is an infinite real entity (*ens infinitum reale*), §. 248. The same is evident from the fact that God is a necessary entity, §. 823, 258.",[]],"baum:844":["All perfections (*perfectiones*) of God have the greatest degree of their reality which they can have in an entity, §. 812. Hence they themselves too are all infinite, §. 248. To the highest reality of each of these perfections pertains consensus in the most perfect entity, §. 139, 140. Therefore in whom is one infinite reality, in him are all, in whom are all, in him the highest, §. 843. Therefore in whom is one infinite reality, God is, §. 811. Hence it can be gathered by what reason several perfections of God can be taken, now these, now those, as first concepts (*conceptus primi*), §. 816, and why a author of evil equally powerful as himself cannot be admitted outside God. Manichaeism (*Manichaeismus*) is the opinion positing that there exists an author of evil equally powerful as God, and it is an error.",[]],"baum:845":["God is in act (*actu*) whatever he can be, as to internal perfections, §. 843, 259. It is again evident that God is internally immutable, §. 252, and a necessary entity, provided a circle is guarded against, §. 843, 256.",[]],"baum:846":["Several gods (*dii*) are impossible (*impossibiles*). For while there would be several, they would in part be diverse (*diversi*), §. 74, hence there would be in one what would not be in the other, §. 38. This would be either a reality (*realitas*) or a negation (*negatio*), §. 36. If it were a reality, he to whom it was lacking would not be god (*deus*), §. 807. If it were a negation, he in whom it inhered would not be god, §. 808. If one reality were posited to be in one, but another equipollent (*aequipollens*) one in another, in neither would there be the totality of realities (*omnitudo realitatum*), hence neither would be god, §. 807. Since therefore the god whom we have meditated on up to now, §. 811-845, is so supremely one of all the greatest absolutely inseparable realities, §. 821, that there is not, nor can be, any other one or second diverse from this, which, that god is a necessary being, we have seen up to now, §. 811-845, the god whom we have meditated on up to now is a unique god, §. 77. Polytheism@@1@@ is the opinion positing several gods, and is an error. We rather truly venerate in god the highest uniqueness (*unicitas*), by which, as the greatest being, §. 842, he is distinguished, §. 173, from all, even the greatest in their genus, e.g. the whole nature of the best world, and the greatest spirit of finite ones, through the most numerous greatest differences, §. 844. This greatest difference extends to the relations themselves, so that such and so great a relation cannot intervene between a thing which is not the god considered up to now and a third, as and as great as between this god and a given third thing, §. 812, 817. By this highest uniqueness of god the equality with him of any thing which is not god, even if otherwise greatest, is indeed removed, and likewise a similarity not only almost total but also removing the infinite dissimilarity to be posited between god and all other things, §. 844, but the plurality of the greatest within god, and the infinite respective difference at the same time of these supremely the same, is not removed, §. 37, 174, but rather is posited in infinite characters, by which something distinguishes god from all things, §. 67.",[]],"baum:847":["The simplicity (*simplicitas*) of god is evident again. For if god were composite (*compositus*), his parts would be substances (*substantiae*) posited outside one another, §. 225, 282. Of these only one would be an infinite substance, §. 846. Therefore the rest would be finite, §. 77, 248. Hence in god certain absolutely necessary imperfections would have to be posited, §. 250, 155. Which is contrary to §. 828.",[]],"baum:848":["God has no figure (*figura*), §. 280, 847. Cruder anthropomorphism (*anthropomorphismus*) is the error of those who attribute to god a figure, e.g. a human one; the subtler is the error which attributes to god the imperfections of finite things, e.g. of humans, §. 828.",[]],"baum:849":["In god there are no successive things (*successiva*), §. 839, 124. Therefore neither is there time (*tempus*) in god, §. 239. He is not in time in such a way that he would constitute a part of successive things, §. 124, 837. And since origin and perishing are absolutely impossible for him, §. 837, he has the greatest duration (*duratio*), §. 299, §. 812, the only really infinite one, §. 844, 846, hence he is eternal (*aeternus*), §. 302, and, coexisting with all time, everlasting (*sempiternus*), §. 303; he was, is, will be, §. 298. God exists (*exsistit*).",[]],"baum:850":["If a contingent eternal being (*ens contingens aeternum*) is posited, its eternity differs greatly from the eternity of god, §. 67. 1) Its duration is subject to continual successions of modifications, §. 209, 836, 2) its eternity indeed has no bounds of extension, but yet on that account cannot yet be called really infinite, §. 259, 849, 3) its eternity would be time without beginning and end, whence it could be called infinite, but mathematically, not on that account really, §. 248, 849, because a successive being, §. 238, is never in act whatever it can be, as to its internal determinations, §. 259.",[]],"baum:851":["God is a necessary being (*ens necessarium*), §. 823, 824, and infinite (*infinitum*), §. 843. Hence he is a being from itself and independent (*ens a se et independens*), §. 310. He exists, §. 849, in such a way that he is not the caused (*causatum*) of another posited outside him, being simply such a cause of his effects, §. 318. All perfections of god are really infinite, §. 844, hence no perfection of god is the caused of something posited outside god, nor can be, §. 381, 248, 310. But a passion (*passio*) would be a predicate of god which would be the caused of something posited outside god, §. 210. Therefore god is wholly impassible (*impassibilis*), he suffers from no thing posited outside himself, neither ideally nor really, §. 212, nor does anything posited outside him act on him, §. 210, therefore neither does he react, §. 213. Every action of god in general is without reaction, as much ideal as real, §. 212.",[]],"baum:852":["Images@@1@@ (*imagines*) are 1) signs of the figure of another. Now god has no figure, §. 848. Therefore images of god in this signification are impossible, §. 347. 2) things similar to another in a notable degree. Since every being is similar to god in some degree, §. 265, every more perfect being will be an image of god, §. 70, 811, and the more perfect it will be, the more similar to god it will be, therefore the greater an image of god, §. 265, 160.",[]],"baum:853":["The world (*mundus*) is the whole of actual finite things, §. 354. God is not the whole of actual finite things, §. 844. Therefore god is not the world: neither this nor any world is god. The same is evident from §. 361, 823, from §. 365, 839, from §. 370, 837, from §. 388, 843.",[]],"baum:854":["This world has an extramundane efficient cause, §. 375, 388, and that a necessary substance, §. 381, 319. Therefore a necessary substance is possible (*possibilis*), §. 333, 69. If a necessary substance is possible, it is actual, §. 109, and everlasting, §. 302. Therefore a necessary substance exists. God is a necessary substance, §. 836. Therefore god exists.",[]],"baum:855":["God is an extramundane being, §. 843, 388, and the world is neither an essential (*essentiale*), nor an essence (*essentia*), nor an attribute (*attributum*), nor a mode (*modus*), nor a modification (*modificatio*), nor an accident (*accidens*) of god, §. 843. God is not a unique substance, §. 391. Theological Spinozism (*Spinozismus theologicus*) is the opinion removing god as an extramundane being, and is an error.",[]],"baum:856":["As §. 811 proves the existence of god a priori, so §. 854 presents the same as proved a posteriori, each independently of the propositions which egoism, §. 392, idealism, §. 402, and materialism remove, §. 395. Therefore they can be employed for convincing egoists, idealists, materialists of the existence of god, even as such.",[]],"baum:857":["God is not matter (*materia*), §. 295, 841, therefore he is immaterial (*immaterialis*), §. 422. God is not a body (*corpus*), §. 296. Therefore he is incorporeal (*incorporeus*), §. 422. Therefore he is incorruptible physically (*incorruptibilis physice*), §. 746. The same is evident from §. 837.",[]],"baum:858":["The most perfect world is the greatest image of god among finite things, §. 436, 852, in the world the substances, §. 400, 857, among the substances the spirits (*spiritus*), §. 402, 531, among the spirits those enjoying the greatest understanding (*intellectus*), §. 637, and among those enjoying the greatest understanding the happiest, §. 787, the furthest removed from any evil, especially moral, §. 788, 813, the holiest, §. 828.",[]],"baum:859":["The nature of god (*natura dei*) (naturing, cf. §. 466) is the complex of his internal perfections, §. 430, by which he is the simply such cause of his effects and impassible, §. 851. Therefore whatever is absolutely possible is physically possible for god, §. 833, and nothing is physically impossible for god which would not be impossible in itself, §. 469, 833, whence nothing is physically necessary for god which would not at the same time be absolutely necessary, absolutely contingent things are also physically contingent for god, §. 469, 104, not only all the preternatural but also the supernatural events of the world, §. 474, 475.",[]],"baum:860":["If a naturalist (*naturalista*), in the more general signification, denies all supernatural events in this world, he errs on account of the denied hypothetical possibility of the same. For since miracles, even in positing a nexus with god outside the same, §. 855, 474, are possible, §. 834, they also have extrinsic possibility, §. 16, 859.",[]],"baum:861":["A homogeneous measure for the degree of reality in god, and for all his perfections, is impossible, §. 843, 844, since uniqueness belongs to god and all his perfections, §. 846, 844. From a homogeneous quantity assumed as one, therefore, the degree of reality found in god and all his perfections cannot be understood. Therefore we can measure neither god nor any perfection of his, §. 291. What we cannot measure is immeasurable (*immensum*)@@1@@. Therefore god and all his perfections are immeasurable things. Which immensity (*immensitas*) we understand also from this, that one who measures must have as many clear perceptions as there are parts, degrees, or varied things in that which he truly measures. Hence one who truly measured god, or anything internal to god, would have to have really infinite clear perceptions, which do not fall into any finite understanding, §. 844.",[]],"baum:862":["God is in himself conceivable (*conceptibilis*), §. 632, 809, and to man, §. 804-1000, and can be rightly, indeed really, defined in many ways, §. 816. Comprehensible (*comprehensibile*)@@1@@ is called that of which a full cognition (*cognitio*)@@2@@ is possible, i.e. one excluding all ignorance, hence a complete historical, philosophical, mathematical one. Incomprehensible (*incomprehensibile*) to someone@@3@@ is that for whose full cognition to be acquired his powers do not suffice, and the more it is necessary for him to be far from the full cognition of it, the more incomprehensible it is given to him. The greater something is, the more difficult or more impossible is its comprehension for a finite cognizer, §. 160, 527, hence the greatest is maximally incomprehensible to all finite things, §. 261. Deism (*deismus*) is the opinion positing that almost nothing about god is conceivable, except perhaps his existence, and is an error, although god and everything in him is maximally incomprehensible to us and to all finite things, §. 861, 806.",[]],"baum:863":["Distinct cognition (*distincta cognitio*) is a reality, §. 531. In god are all realities, §. 807. Therefore god cognizes distinctly. Therefore he has understanding, is an intellectual substance, §. 830, a spirit, §. 402.",[]],"baum:864":["In god are all the greatest realities, §. 812. The more things, and the more distinctly, they are cognized, the greater the distinct cognition is, §. 634. Therefore the most distinct will be the distinct cognition of all things, §. 161. Now the distinct cognition of all things is possible, §. 632. Therefore in god there is a distinct cognition of all things, and the same the most distinct.",[]],"baum:865":["The understanding (*intellectus*) of God is the highest, §. 863, 812, and immutable, §. 839. Therefore in it there are no preceding and following thoughts, §. 125. It is the highest, while it represents (*repraesentat*) the most, the clearest marks (*notas*) of the most and the greatest things, in the strongest and most diverse associates. Therefore the understanding of God is the most profound, the most extensive, the purest, §. 637.",[]],"baum:866":["God cognizes (*cognoscit*) himself as fully as can be, §. 865. Cognition of God is theology in the broader signification; the theology by which God cognizes himself is exemplary (αρχετυποσ), insofar as theologies of finite beings are to be aimed at being similar to it, §. 346.",[]],"baum:867":["God, representing all things, represents to himself all possible worlds (*mundos possibiles*), §. 864. This is the internal perfection (*perfectio*) of God, §. 37, his essence (*essentia*), §. 816.",[]],"baum:868":["God cognizes most distinctly all essences of all finite things, §. 864. Insofar, therefore, as the essences of things are represented in the understanding of God, they depend on it, §. 14, and are eternal in the same, §. 849.",[]],"baum:869":["God represents to himself most distinctly all possible worlds, §. 867. Therefore both the best, §. 436, and the most imperfect, §. 442, and this one. The world, insofar as it is represented sensitively (*sensitive*), is sensible@@1@@ (*sensibilis*, visible [*adspectabilis*]); insofar as it is cognized distinctly, it is intelligible@@2@@. God cognizes this intelligible world most distinctly. Therefore he cognizes most distinctly all monads of this world, all its souls, §. 864. One who is endowed with the most distinct cognition of the human soul is a searcher of hearts@@3@@. Therefore God is a searcher of hearts, §. 740, and sees through the representation of the sensible world of each single monad, each single soul, §. 400, 741, and indeed much more perfectly than a given monad, a given soul, knows itself and its own representation of the world, §. 864.",[]],"baum:870":["In God there is no sensitive cognition, §. 864, 521. Therefore he does not have lower cognitive faculties, §. 520, nothing is obscure to him, nothing confused, hence he cognizes no thing more clearly than another, §. 528, and he neither attends, nor abstracts, §. 529, nor reflects, nor compares, §. 626.",[]],"baum:871":["Since God cognizes most distinctly all things signified, he has an intuition (*intuitus*) of all things, §. 620, 864. He also cognizes all signs, and all symbolic cognition of souls in the world, §. 864, 869. Yet there is never in him a greater or lesser perception of the signs than of the things signified, §. 870; both are always the greatest, §. 864.",[]],"baum:872":["God represents to himself most distinctly every nexus (*nexum*), §. 864. Therefore he has the highest reason (*rationem*), §. 640. His highest reason is, while he is the highest understanding, hence immutable, without any succession of ratiocinations, §. 865, seeing through the greatest nexus of the most things, §. 645.",[]],"baum:873":["The cognition of God is of the highest extension and majesty, the most exact, §. 864, and the most ordered, §. 822, free of all ignorance and error, narrowness and levity, in which there is nothing hasty, §. 515, the clearest, the most distinct, of all truth, hence the most certain, in which there is nothing obscure, nothing confused, nothing inadequate, nothing incomplete, nothing impure, nothing superficial, probable, doubtful, or improbable, nothing dead, inert and speculative, §. 669, 671. He cognizes most distinctly all certain principles, all things derived from them (*principiata*), every nexus of principles and things derived from principles, §. 864, 872; therefore his cognition is the highest knowledge (*scientia*). God cognizes most distinctly all ignorances, errors, narrownesses of cognition, meagernesses of souls, whatever is crude and hasty, of night, confusion, inadequacy, incompleteness, impurity in cognition of this kind, all moral certainties of souls, probabilities, doubts, improbabilities, opinions, arguments, scruples, inert and dead speculations, §. 869.",[]],"baum:874":["God knows (*scit*), §. 873, I) all determinations of all things, insofar as they are regarded as merely possible. This is the knowledge of simple intelligence@@1@@ (*scientia simplicis intelligentiae*).",[]],"baum:875":["God knows, §. 873, II) all determinations of actual things, 1) of this world, by free knowledge (of vision)@@1@@ (*scientia libera [visionis]*), α) past things by divine recollection@@2@@, β) present things, by knowledge of vision@@3@@, γ) future things, by foreknowledge@@4@@. Philosophical Socinianism is the opinion that removes the divine foreknowledge of future contingents, and it is an error.",[]],"baum:876":["God knows all determinations of actual things 2) of another world than this, by middle knowledge@@1@@ (*scientia media*). For any event of this world another could exist, §. 363, 324. But each would have its own consequences in the indefinite, following partially different through all states of the world, §. 488. Therefore if even for a single event of this world another had existed, this world would be partially other than it is, through all the states successive to that event, and indeed also the antecedent ones, §. 357, 278. Therefore whatever could have existed in this world in place of any event with all its consequences, God knows, by middle knowledge, §. 378.",[]],"baum:877":["God is always most distinctly conscious to himself of the states of the present world, §. 875. Therefore he never sleeps, §. 556. The same is evident from §. 870. He does not sense, as we do, §. 870, 544, but eminently by the knowledge of vision, §. 875, is awake, §. 552, 826.",[]],"baum:878":["From eternity God represented to himself a future thing, as much as he can, §. 875, 843. Therefore when it becomes present in the world, nothing is added to the cognition of God, §. 161, although it itself is changed from an object of foreknowledge into an object of vision, §. 125. From eternity God will represent to himself a past thing, as much as he can, §. 875, 843. Therefore when the present will become past, it itself indeed will be changed from an object of vision into an object of recollection, but nothing will be subtracted from the cognition of God, §. 161. God intuits from eternity all successive states of this world, §. 875, whence the internal immutability of the divine cognition can be thought, §. 839.",[]],"baum:879":["One who cannot err is infallible@@1@@ (*infallibilis*). Therefore God is infallible, §. 873. The infallibility is least if for someone it is not at all impossible to confuse a single least truth with the false; the more, the greater truths, therefore, that someone does not know how to confuse with false ones, the greater the impossibility of such confusion, the more he is infallible. When therefore we attribute to God the greatest infallibility, §. 812, we venerate him as absolutely unable to confuse the most, the greatest truths with any falsehood.",[]],"baum:880":["Certainty (*certitudo*) is least when it is the least clear cognition of a single least truth, §. 161. The more clearly, the more, the greater truths are cognized, the greater the certainty will be, §. 160. When therefore we attribute to God the highest subjective certainty, §. 873, 812, we venerate him as cognizing most distinctly the most, the greatest truths. The same God is objectively most certain, §. 93, 812, while his truth is the highest, §. 822, and existence is demonstrable through his essence and the existence of any finite thing, §. 856.",[]],"baum:881":["The free knowledge of God is his respective perfection, §. 875, 815. And since it is absolutely necessary that it be most true, §. 879, it presents this world to God as existing contingently in and through itself, §. 361, hence that it be necessary only hypothetically is absolutely necessary, §. 102. Therefore the free knowledge of God is an analogue of the mode, §. 827.",[]],"baum:882":["Wisdom is, generally@@1@@, seeing through a final nexus (*nexus finalis*), and indeed, specifically@@2@@, wisdom is seeing through ends, prudence@@3@@ seeing through means. Hence God is most all-wise, §. 872; when we say he is most all-wise, we venerate him 1) as seeing through all ends, 2) all means, 3) all possible nexuses of them, §. 343, 4) with respect to all qualities, 5) all quantities, 6) in the greatest possible nexus, 7) most certainly and most ardently, §. 880, 873.",[]],"baum:883":["God knows all ends, hence both the best and the worst, which of them can be a means of another, hence every possible subordination of all ends, every coordination as well, §. 315, all aims in a certain respect, and the ultimate absolutely, §. 343, according to all their goodness, and degree of goodness, every possible nexus with means, so that none indeed ever appears to him other than it is, §. 879, yet so that he at the same time cognizes how each end appears to all and single souls cognizing it, §. 869. Therefore God knows best, concerning the best ends, that they are the best, §. 882.",[]],"baum:884":["God knows all means, hence also the best, §. 882, by the positing of which the highest perfection is posited, §. 187. Now with the means posited the end is posited, §. 326, 341. Therefore the best means are subordinated to the best end. God knows them, insofar as they are subordinated to the best end, how, and to what extent they are subordinated, §. 883.",[]],"baum:885":["A certain means@@1@@ is one that in truth is such and as great as it is cognized to be, hence it is opposed to the apparent, which only seems, and either is not at all, or is not as great as it seems, and to the uncertain, whose true goodness, or degrees of goodness, is not evident. The best means are the most certain, both insofar as they are not apparent, and insofar as God is most certain, both of the goodness and of the degree of goodness of the same, §. 882, none the less seeing through all possible doubts of souls about them, §. 869, 873.",[]],"baum:886":["The more fecund a means is, the greater the perfection posited with it posited, §. 166, 341. Therefore the most fecund means are the best, and vice versa, §. 187. God knows the most fecund means, §. 884, and how much fecundity any has, §. 882, and how much fecundity is ascribed to any by given souls, §. 873.",[]],"baum:887":["The best means are complete, §. 886, for the best end, §. 884. Therefore they are not incomplete, §. 81. If they were superfluous, some would have things contributing nothing to the end. Therefore not actualizing the good, §. 341, and yet not wholly sterile, §. 23. Hence evil, §. 146. Therefore the best means are adequate to the best end. God knows means adequate to the best end, §. 884. Certain and adequate means are attaining the end exactly (*ex asse*)@@1@@. God knows means attaining the best end exactly, §. 885.",[]],"baum:888":["The actuation of an end through means that attain it exactly, with the fewest, is the shortest way@@1@@ (*via brevissima*). The use of the best means is always the shortest way. For then the end is actuated through means attaining it exactly, §. 887, each of which is most fecund, §. 886, none superfluous, §. 887. Hence they will be the fewest that can be, §. 161. God knows every shortest way, §. 887.",[]],"baum:889":["Omniscience@@1@@ (*omniscientia*) is knowledge of all things. God is most omniscient, §. 873. When we say he is such, we venerate him as such as §. 863-888 exhibit.",[]],"baum:890":["God intuits all perfections or imperfections of all things most distinctly (*distinctissime*), §. 889, 871. Whatever is in God is the greatest ground (*ratio*), §. 23, 812. Hence the intuition (*intuitus*) of perfections or imperfections in the same is maximally lively, §. 669, 873. Therefore satisfaction (*complacentia*) and dissatisfaction (*displicentia*) belong to God, §. 655, the highest, §. 812.",[]],"baum:891":["The satisfaction and dissatisfaction of God are nothing but most true, §. 880, most distinct, §. 870, most rational (*rationalissimae*), §. 822; it is never totally indifferent, §. 653, nor ever partially toward any thing, §. 654, nor does it have pleasure (*voluptas*), nor displeasure (*taedium*), §. 656, nor sensitive appetite, nor aversion, nor instincts, nor flights, §. 677, nor affects, §. 678, nor apparent pleasures, nor displeasures, § 655.",[]],"baum:892":["Since God intuits himself most distinctly as only good, as best, most holy, §. 866, 828, he draws the purest pleasure (*voluptas*) from himself, §. 661. This is the highest acquiescence (*acquiescentia*) of God in himself, §. 682. Exemplary theology (*theologia exemplaris*) is most sweet, §. 866. God has mere displeasure (*taedium*) from no thing, §. 891, 661. His dissatisfaction and all satisfaction is immutable, §. 839. Hence he knows no transitory pleasures and displeasures, §. 662, nothing is troublesome to him, §. 658. Nor does his highest dissatisfaction obscure the highest pleasure in any way, §. 870, nor can anything outside him actuate in him any pleasure or displeasure, §. 851.",[]],"baum:893":["In God there is a free science (*scientia libera*) of this world, although there could be both of none and of another, §. 881. Yet the free science of this world does not exist in God except by the power (*vis*) of God, §. 851, 197. Therefore God determined his power to actuating the free science not of none, not of another, but of this world. Therefore God desires (*appetit*) and averts (*aversatur*), §. 663. Now he does not desire, does not avert sensitively, §. 891. Yet his desire and aversion follow cognition, §. 668, 822. Therefore he wills and does not will (*vult et non vult*), has will (*voluntas*) and nonwill (*noluntas*), §. 689, the highest, §. 812, i.e. following the highest omniscience most perfectly, §. 668, 889.",[]],"baum:894":["Will or nonwill following the mathematical cognition of the understanding (*intellectus*), or desiring in proportion to the degree of goodness distinctly cognized in the things to be desired, averting in proportion to the degree of imperfection distinctly cognized in the things to be averted, is proportional to the understanding@@1@@, which, if it presents a thing as good or bad as it is, the will or nonwill is proportional to the objects@@2@@. Therefore proportionality (*proportionalitas*) belongs to the divine will and nonwill, §. 893, the highest, §. 812, as it follows the most distinct and highest and infallible cognition of the degrees of perfection and imperfection, §. 879, 883.",[]],"baum:895":["God, while he wills, §. 393, acts, §. 210. All actions of God can depend on a sufficient principle (*principium*) internal to the acting God, §. 833, and do depend, §. 851. Therefore spontaneity (*spontaneitas*) belongs to the divine actions and to God himself, §. 704, and the highest, §. 872, while he suffices for the most numerous, greatest actions, §. 706, 832. The most spontaneous actions of God will be either immanent (*immanentes*) or transient (*transeuntes*), §. 211.",[]],"baum:896":["God is free from absolute internal coercion (*coactio*), §. 710, 702, and physical, §. 710, 859, and external, both ideal and real, §. 707, 851, and thus also moral, §. 723, of motives, §. 726, to unwilling actions by violence in a certain respect (*secundum quid*), §. 714, from allurements, threats, persuasions, dissuasions, extortions, §. 728.",[]],"baum:897":["All actions are placed in the power (*potestas*) of God, §. 708, 859. If therefore a certain action and whatever action opposed to it is possible (*possibilis*), each in itself, §. 15, i.e. if a certain action is contingent (*contingens*) in itself, §. 104, hence also has an opposite action contingent in itself, §. 104, each is placed in the power of God. Therefore all actions contingent in themselves are free for God with respect to execution and indifferent as to the exercise of the act (*exercitium actus*), §. 708, because with respect to God the physical possibility of each is the highest, §. 844. Hence the actuation of this universe, or of none, or of another, was free for God with respect to execution, §. 835. He was indifferent, as to the exercise of the act, both toward the best world and toward the most imperfect, and the same is, and will be. §. 849.",[]],"baum:898":["Since God determines himself in acting according to pleasure (*pro lubitu*), §. 893, 712, and this distinct, §. 893, he has freedom (*libertas*), §. 719, and the highest, §. 812, i.e. actuating the greatest, most numerous actions according to most distinct pleasure, §. 725. Fatalism (*fatalismus*), the opinion taking away divine freedom, is an error.",[]],"baum:899":["God wills most freely, §. 898. Therefore the good (*bonum*), §. 719, 665. God averts most freely, §. 898. Therefore the evil (*malum*), §. 719, 665. Volition (*volitio*) or love of the good, hatred or nolition (*nolitio*) of the evil in God are infinite, §. 844, 1) extensively, while he loves every good, hates every evil, §. 898, 889, 2) protensively, while they are eternal, §. 849, and 3) intensively, while most proportional, §. 894. Divine volition or nolition, infinite extensively, because in God there can be no dead and merely speculative science, §. 873, is borne toward objects 1) of simple intelligence (*simplex intelligentia*), universals, which exhibit notions to us, and whatever is in them of good or evil, toward which God cannot be totally indifferent, §. 891, 2) of middle science (*scientia media*), the actuals of another universe, 3) of free science (*scientia libera*), §. 874-876. The will of God, insofar as it desires the objects of free science, or the actuals of this universe, is called consequent@@1@@, insofar as it is borne toward universals and actuals of another universe, antecedent@@2@@, by reduction, §. 826, 695, the former efficient, §. 671, both efficacious, the antecedent also, not only because serious, but also because it is in the motives of the decree, §. 675.",[]],"baum:900":["Will is inscrutable (*imperscrutabilis*)@@1@@, whose impulsive causes (*causae impulsivae*) are incomprehensible. Now the impulsive causes of the divine will are his most distinct pleasure, §. 898, hence the internal perfection in God, §. 37, incomprehensible to us, §. 862. He who conceives some motives of God in some degree of distinction does not scrutinize the divine will, §. 862. Hence neither does one intending some intellection of the divine motives feign to himself the will of God scrutable, §. 664. The more impulsive causes a certain will has, the less fully they can be cognized, the more inscrutable is the will, §. 160. Hence when the will of God is said to be most inscrutable to us, §. 812, we think 1) that most of his motives are not fully cognized, 2) that any of them is very far from our comprehension, §. 862, 898.",[]],"baum:901":["The conformity of a free action with its ground (*ratio*), hence with the law, §. 83, moral, §. 723, is the rectitude (*rectitudo*) of the same@@1@@; insofar therefore as all the most free actions of God, §. 898, 725, have the highest conformity, §. 176, with the law of the best, §. 822, moral, §. 723: Let the best be freely joined to the best, they are most right. The highest rectitude belongs to the divine will, §. 899, holiness (*sanctitas*), §. 828, moral, §. 723.",[]],"baum:902":["The most free actions of God are not absolutely necessary, while their opposite is possible in itself. §. 102. All actions of God transient into the world, §. 854, 211, have their opposite possible in itself, §. 361. Hence none of them is absolutely necessary. And since the opposite of all the most free actions of God can be actuated through the omnipotence of God, §. 897, none of them, nor therefore any action of God into the world, is physically necessary for him, §. 470, 859, and thus none is morally necessary in the sense as if through freedom the opposite of any would be rendered physically impossible for God, §. 723. Since nevertheless the highest freedom of God always determines itself most rightly, §. 901, while all his actions are morally most holy, they are morally necessary, §. 723, 724.",[]],"baum:903":["Goodness (*bonitas*)@@1@@ (benignity, *benignitas*) is the determination (*determinatio*) of the will to doing well to another. A benefit (*beneficium*)@@2@@ is an action more useful to another proceeding from goodness. The least goodness would be the least propensity or disposition of the will of conferring one least good on a single least, i.e. least worthy, §. 161. Therefore the more, the more numerous, the greater benefits, on the more numerous, the more worthy, goodness desires to confer, the greater it is, §. 160. A benefit is a greater good. §. 336, 187.",[]],"baum:904":["God wills benefits to be conferred on others, §. 903, 899. Therefore he is benign, §. 903, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most benign, we venerate one infinitely and eternally, §. 899, and immutably, §. 839, loving to confer the most numerous, greatest benefits on the most numerous, most worthy. §. 903.",[]],"baum:905":["Love of human beings is philanthropy (*philanthropia*)@@1@@, willing the proportionate mutual love of the beloved is jealousy (*zelotypia*)@@2@@, which, together with mercy, favor, benevolence, clemency, are universal in God and greatest, §. 684, 904. And since constant intense love is fidelity (*fidelitas*)@@3@@, God, loving eternally, is infinitely, in the highest degree faithful, §. 904, 812.",[]],"baum:906":["Goodness toward persons or spirits, proportional, is justice (*iustitia*)@@1@@, cf. E. §. 317. The least justice would be the least goodness following a least, least clear, least certain, least lively mathematical cognition, §. 903, 161. Therefore the more 1) intensely it loves, 2) the more, 3) the greater, 4) on the more numerous to confer benefits in proportion to the degree of perfection or imperfection cognized in the spirit, 5) the more clearly, 6) the more certainly, 7) the more ardently that degree is cognized, the greater is justice, §. 903, 160. God is just, §. 904, 894, in the highest degree, §. 812. When we call God most just, we venerate his highest goodness, §. 904, most proportional, §. 894, according to the most distinct, and infallible, and most lively cognition of the degrees of perfection or imperfection met with in individual spirits, most ready to confer the most numerous, greatest benefits on the most numerous.",[]],"baum:907":["Reward (*praemium*)@@1@@ (remuneration, *remuneratio*) is a contingent good for a person on account of moral good conferred. Justice in conferring rewards is remunerative (*remuneratoria*)@@2@@, which we venerate as highest in God, §. 906, 812, while we look up to him as most ready to confer most proportional rewards for all, even the least, moral goods of all spirits.",[]],"baum:908":["Punishment (*poena*)@@1@@ is a contingent evil of a person inflicted on account of moral evil. Both punishments and certain rewards (*praemia*) can be sufficiently conceived from the essence of sin and the nature of the sinner, or from the essence of moral good and the nature of one acting well, and they will be natural (*naturalia*). Certain rewards cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another conferring them, and are arbitrary (*arbitraria*)@@2@@. Certain punishments cannot be sufficiently conceived except from the choice of another punishing, and are arbitrary@@3@@.",[]],"baum:909":["He in whom there are moral evils is a sinner (*peccator*)@@1@@. He in whom certain moral evils are not is, with respect to them, innocent in the broader sense (*innocens latius dictus*)@@2@@ (blameless, *insons*), cf. E. §. 319. The sinner, other things equal, is not as good as the innocent, §. 187. Therefore he is also not loved as much by the most proportionate goodness, §. 906. Therefore God wills to confer certain benefits on the innocent which he does not will to confer on the sinner, §. 904. Toward these benefits not to be conferred on the sinner God is not indifferent, §. 891, hence by his most distinct pleasure he is averse to conferring them on the sinner, §. 898, 669. Therefore he wills the opposite of benefits of this kind, §. 663, 690. The opposites of benefits are evils, §. 81, 903, and these contingent, §. 146. Therefore God wills certain contingent evils to be inflicted on the sinner on account of moral evils, i.e. punishments, §. 908.",[]],"baum:910":["Justice in inflicting punishments is punitive (*punitiva*)@@1@@ (vindictive, avenging, vindicatory, nemesis); punitive justice befits God, §. 909, and in the highest degree, §. 812, since it is most ready to punish most proportionately all the sins of all sinners, §. 906. Philosophical Dippelianism, an opinion removing the punitive justice of God, is an error.",[]],"baum:911":["The opposite of a natural reward is a natural punishment, the opposite of an arbitrary reward is an arbitrary punishment, §. 908. Therefore the remunerative justice of God dispenses arbitrary rewards, the vindicative justice imposes arbitrary punishments, §. 907, 909. Natural punishments themselves are arbitrary with respect to him, without whose choice the nature of the sinner would not have a sufficient ground, §. 908. The same ground holds for natural rewards.",[]],"baum:912":["The complex of causes posited outside a spirit concurring in its prosperity or misery is good and bad fortune (*fortuna*)@@1@@; what is actualized through fortune is fortuitous (*fortuita*)@@2@@, and these are contingent, §. 146, 787. Goods of this kind conferred on a spirit on account of moral goods are rewards, §. 907, and since they are not to be sufficiently cognized except from the choice of God, §. 854, they are arbitrary, §. 908. Fortuitous evils inflicted on a spirit are misfortunes (*infortunia*)@@3@@. Misfortunes inflicted on a spirit on account of moral evils are punishments, and since they are not to be sufficiently cognized except from the choice of God, §. 854, they are arbitrary punishments, §. 908.",[]],"baum:913":["Miraculous and supernatural rewards and punishments are in themselves possible, §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, they are arbitrary with respect to divine omnipotence, §. 833, 834, §. 908, 898, but not solely so, §. 911, 912.",[]],"baum:914":["Of an event (*eventus*), whatever in it is real and positive is called the remote material (*materiale remotum*)@@1@@, the thoroughgoing determination the proximate material (*materiale proximum*)@@2@@, and whatever in finite things is negative in it the formal (*formale*)@@3@@. Hence evils, and thus also punishments, §. 908, are either considered materially and formally at once, and are, like all finite things, partly good, partly evil taken precisely or formally, §. 264, and are loved by God insofar as they are good, God hates them insofar as they are evil, infinitely, §. 899; or they are considered only materially, as remote material, and are positive and real, only an object of divine love, §. 899; or only formally, and are nothing but either negations strictly so called, or privations, §. 137, and are only an object of divine hatred, §. 899; or as proximate material and formal.",[]],"baum:915":["God wills by punitive justice, §. 910, the remote material of the punishment, and whatever of reality is in the proximate, but not the formal, §. 914. Now, that the same befall this sinner, not another, but him to whom they are most proportionate, is reality, §. 36, 909. This, therefore, God wills, §. 899. Hence also the distinction of contingent evils, §. 146, and thus of punishments, §. 908, as well as of sins, §. 788, into privative and positive is to be judged, §. 525.",[]],"baum:916":["Longanimity (*longanimitas*)@@1@@ (the patience of a judge) is justice punishing only on an occasion seen as the best. God, infallibly knowing the best opportunities of all punishments, §. 889, 879, will also this proximate material of any punishment, since it is reality, §. 36, most proportionately, §. 894, 914. Hence he is supremely longanimous.",[]],"baum:917":["Impartiality is aversion to deciding from apparent stimuli. In God not even stimuli, §. 898, much less apparent ones, are possible, §. 889, 12, and since his most holy will is averse both to them themselves and to a decree from them, §. 902, he is most impartial.",[]],"baum:918":["Impartial justice is equity (*aequitas*)@@1@@. God, most just, §. 906, and most impartial, §. 917, is most equitable.",[]],"baum:919":["Sincerity (*sinceritas*)@@1@@ is goodness in signifying its mind, and this is in God, §. 904, the highest, §. 812, since he is most ready to signify his mind by the most apt signs, to all to whom, and only as much as, the highest wisdom urges him to signify his mind, §. 884-888.",[]],"baum:920":["Veracity (*veracitas*)@@1@@ is sincerity in speech. Therefore, since divine wisdom judges it best to signify the divine mind through speech, God is most veracious, §. 919.",[]],"baum:921":["The least duration of the least nature would be the least life. The greater the nature, the greater the duration, and the greater the life, until the greatest sum of duration of the most perfect nature is reached, §. 430, 161. The highest life is to be attributed to God, §. 859, 850.",[]],"baum:922":["Since the greatest life of God is absolutely necessary, §. 921, because it is his essence, §. 816, and existence, §. 823, 780, God is not only immortal, but alone has absolute immortality, §. 781.",[]],"baum:923":["With the highest metaphysical and physical perfection in God is joined the highest moral perfection, §. 806, 859, through his highest rectitude, §. 901; most distinctly conscious of these to himself, §. 866, and enjoying from them the highest pleasure, §. 892, he is most blessed, §. 787.",[]],"baum:924":["God is happy, §. 923, 787, in the highest degree, §. 812, since 1) all moral corruption and misery is not only absent from him, §. 813, but cannot even be in him, physically, §. 859, or morally, §. 902, 2) independently and from himself, §. 851, 3) without any change both of the goods themselves and of the intuition of them, he is most happy, §. 839.",[]],"baum:925":["A being more perfect than another is higher (*superius*)@@1@@ than it. God is the supreme spirit, §. 889, 796, a supramundane being (*ens supramundanum*)@@2@@, insofar as he has greater perfection than any whole world, even the best, §. 361, 843. And since several most perfect beings posited outside one another are impossible, §. 846, God is the absolutely highest being.",[]],"baum:926":["An efficient cause actualizes an effect, §. 319, 210. God is the efficient cause of this universe, §. 854. Therefore God actualized this universe, either from eternity, i.e. in such a way that this world did not have a beginning, or in time or not from eternity, §. 10; in neither case did a part of the world preexist the world, §. 371, 394. Therefore in both cases the world is actualized out of nothing, §. 228, and it is God who actualized it out of nothing, §. 854. To actualize something out of nothing is to create (*creare*)@@1@@. Therefore God is the creator of this universe.",[]],"baum:927":["Creation by emanation (*creatio per emanationem*)@@1@@ would be the actualization of the universe from the essence of God; by creation of this kind 1) the world would not be actualized out of nothing, §. 926. For the essence of God is a necessary being, §. 109, 816. Which is against §. 926. 2) either the whole essence of God, or a part of it, could be changed into the universe, §. 370, which is against §. 839. 3) a part of God would be posited outside God, §. 388, and God would be composite, §. 225, which is against §. 838. In still more ways it is evident that creation by emanation is impossible, §. 7.",[]],"baum:928":["All monads of this universe are finite beings, §. 396, hence all are beings from another, §. 308, and none can be actualized except out of nothing, §. 229, 236. Therefore all monads of this universe are created, §. 926. A being which cannot exist except through creation is a creature (*creatura*)@@1@@. Therefore all monads of this universe are creatures, and indeed of God, §. 381, 854. Whatever in this universe is substantial is a creature of God, §. 396.",[]],"baum:929":["Every spirit of this universe, §. 404, all human souls, §. 744, 741, all elements of this world are creatures of God, §. 928, 423, each actualized by simultaneous creation, §. 238, and instantaneous, §. 299, 228. Now the bodies of this world are aggregates of elements, §. 155, 420, hence whatever in bodies is substantial, as many as have parts subsisting through themselves, are creatures of God, §. 928.",[]],"baum:930":["What cannot exist except as the caused of another cannot endure except as the caused of another, §. 299. Hence the world and all its parts cannot endure except as beings from another, §. 928, 375. Therefore a creation actualizing an independent duration in creatures is impossible, and not an object of divine omnipotence, §. 833. The creation of this universe is possible, §. 926, 57. Therefore through the creation of this universe no independent duration in it has been actualized.",[]],"baum:931":["Whatever does not pertain to the existence of the world and of its substantial parts is not created, §. 926. Now the essences of creatures, and hence also their metaphysical evil, §. 146, do not pertain to their existence, §. 132, 134, hence neither the essences nor the metaphysical evils of creatures are created, unless you wish all the actual determinations of a creature to be called created, against the notion of §. 926.",[]],"baum:932":["That this universe not exist, that no universe exist, is in itself possible, §. 370, 361. All things possible in themselves are also possible for God, §. 859. Hence God could, with regard to omnipotence, not create a universe, or create another than this one, with the existence of which this one would not have existed, §. 379, 926. Yet he created this one, impassible, §. 851, through the highest spontaneity, §. 895, by a creation free with regard to execution, §. 897. Therefore most freely, §. 898.",[]],"baum:933":["God created this world most freely (*liberrime*), §. 932. Therefore he willed to create it (*creare voluit*), §. 893, he willed efficiently (*efficienter*), §. 671, hence also fully (*plene*), because he is infallible, §. 879, and thus from complete motives (*motivis completis*) consequently. Therefore God decreed (*decrevit*) to create this world, §. 695, and indeed efficaciously (*efficaciter*) in every signification, §. 675.",[]],"baum:934":["God decreed the creation of this world, §. 933, with the most proportionate will (*voluntate proportionalissima*), §. 894. Hence he decreed the existence of this world according to the degree of goodness (*bonitatis*) cognized in it, §. 926. He did not decree the existence of another world with the most proportionate will, §. 379. Therefore God did not cognize so great a goodness in the existence of any other world as he cognized in the existence of this world, §. 70. Now the cognition of God is most distinct and maximally infallible, §. 879. Therefore the existence of this world, preferred and chosen before all the remaining ones, §. 697, is the best that the existence of a world can be, §. 187.",[]],"baum:935":["With the existence of this world posited, the highest perfection (*perfectio*) that can be in a world is posited, §. 187, 934. In whatever world the highest perfection that can be in a world is posited, that one is the most perfect, §. 185. Therefore this world is the most perfect of all possible ones.",[]],"baum:936":["In this world there are the most of the greatest, the greatest of the most, parts that can be in a world, with as great an agreement (*consensus*) as can be in a world, §. 436, 935. Hence the world is extensively, protensively and intensively the greatest of worlds, §. 437, really finite indeed, yet so that it can be called infinite in many ways, §. 440, the best, §. 443, in it the greatest universal nexus, §. 441, order, §. 444, and the greatest harmony that can be, §. 462.",[]],"baum:937":["Posit a world totally equal to this world in perfection: it would have to be posited outside it, otherwise it would be a part of this world, §. 354, 155. The perfection of this world cannot be outside it, nor could the perfection of one perfect in a wholly equal way be outside it, §. 194. If therefore such a world were possible, two perfections totally equal to each other outside one another would be possible, which is against §. 272. Therefore the most perfect world is unique, §. 77.",[]],"baum:938":["Of this, that this world is the best, we can be certain, §. 935, 936. Therefore this doctrine is not a philosophical hypothesis. Those who demand a demonstration of it a posteriori either understand by demonstration a posteriori that which has in its premises an intuitive judgment, and they have it, §. 933, into whose premises enters: this world exists, an intuitive judgment, or they demand to experience the discrepancy of this and a worse world, hence to be posited outside this world, §. 548, a marvelous ecstasy, §. 552.",[]],"baum:939":["Whatever God created, he willed to create, §. 932. Now, however, he did not will at all the formal (*formale*) of contingent and moral evils in particular, §. 914. Therefore he did not will to create the same either, §. 926. God is not the creator of any contingent evil, hence not of any moral evil regarded formally.",[]],"baum:940":["An author@@1@@ (*auctor*) is the cause of a free action, and both the action and its causata are effects of the author, or deeds@@2@@ (*facta*). Now God created the world most freely, §. 932. Therefore God is the author of the creation and of this world. God is not the author except of those things which he wills, §. 719, 891. Now God wills the formal of no contingent evil, of no sin, §. 914. Therefore God is the author of no contingent evil, of no sin regarded formally. A moral cause strictly so called@@3@@ (*causa moralis stricte dicta*) is an author through the free determination of another, such as one enticing, threatening, persuading, dissuading, extorting, §. 728. Now a moral cause strictly so called is an author. Therefore God is not a moral cause strictly so called of any sin and contingent evil regarded formally.",[]],"baum:941":["One who actuates impulsive causes toward moral evil regarded formally is a tempter to evil@@1@@ (*tentator ad malum*). If God were to actuate impulsive causes toward moral evil regarded formally, he would become its moral cause strictly so called. Therefore God is never a tempter to evil, §. 940.",[]],"baum:942":["The recognition of a greater perfection in something is honor@@1@@ (*honor*). Greater honor is glory@@2@@ (*gloria*), cf. §. 684. The glory of God is therefore the greater cognition of his highest perfections. The more people, the more, therefore the more clearly, the more certainly, the more ardently, the more truly, the more perfections, the greater, of someone they cognize, the greater is the glory, §. 160. Hence the highest glory of God is the clearest, truest, most certain, most ardent cognition of the most and greatest of his perfections in the most, §. 161. The glory of God is a good (*bonum*), §. 866.",[]],"baum:943":["Through this world the highest perfections of God can be cognized more clearly, more truly, more certainly, more ardently, §. 333, 375. Therefore this world is useful (*utilis*) for the glory of God for those able to cognize the same from it, §. 336. This usefulness is actuated by the creation both of the world itself and of all spirits in it, §. 926. Therefore the creator of the universe uses it for his glory to be actuated, which he cognizes most distinctly as good, §. 889, 942. Therefore God in creating had an end, §. 341.",[]],"baum:944":["God created the world according to his highest knowledge of the final nexus, §. 943, hence most wisely (*sapientissime*), §. 882. Hence he knew and actuated, in creating it, the best ends of the best world, all of them in the most perfect subordination and coordination, §. 883, their best means, §. 884, most certain, §. 885, most fecund, §. 886, and exactly consequent to them, §. 887, by the shortest way, §. 888, §. 894. Further it is clear that God created no contingent evil regarded formally, §. 146, 914.",[]],"baum:945":["The end of God in creating the world was not some perfection internal to him. For through the world it is impossible that any of them be either actuated or augmented, §. 851, but God does not will impossibilities, §. 891, 893. Now whatever is, besides God, is a world, or a part of a world, §. 846, 354. Therefore the end of God in creating the world was the perfection of creatures, §. 928.",[]],"baum:946":["The end of God in creating the universe was as great a perfection of creatures as is possible in the best world, §. 945, 944. Therefore all usefulnesses of created things, §. 336, all their uses, §. 338, are ends of God, §. 944. The knowledge of the divine ends in creatures is teleology (*teleologia*), both physical, of bodies, and pneumatic, exhibiting the ends of spirits.",[]],"baum:947":["The good determination of a spirit from motives of divine glory is the illustration of divine glory (worship of God)@@1@@ (*illustratio gloriae divinae (cultus dei)*). The glory of God and its illustration are religion (*religio*). Now the glory of God is useful for its worship, §. 336, 712. Glory and worship for religion, §. 336. Therefore the ends of creation were the worship of God and religion, §. 942, 946.",[]],"baum:948":["The prosperity and beatitude or happiness of spirits is useful for the glory of God, §. 787, 942, its illustration and religion, §. 947, 336. Therefore as great a prosperity and beatitude or happiness of spirits as is possible in the best world was the end of creation, §. 946, 942.",[]],"baum:949":["All creatures, whether regarded as means or as ends, are useful for the glory of God, this for the illustration of divine glory, §. 943, 947, hence all things bordering on religion in creation are subordinated to it, §. 315, 947. Therefore religion is the ultimate end of creation, §. 343.",[]],"baum:950":["This world endures, §. 299, but at no moment independently, §. 930. Therefore it cannot endure except as the causatum of something posited outside itself, even for one moment, §. 307. Therefore an extramundane force operates its duration at any moment of duration whatever, §. 210. This is God, §. 855, 839. Therefore God operates the duration of the universe at any moment of duration whatever. The actuation of duration is conservation@@1@@ (*conservatio*). Therefore God is the conserver of this universe.",[]],"baum:951":["Conservation is the continuous influx of God, §. 950, 895, and a real one at that, §. 212, because its existence cannot be the effect of anything finite, §. 308. The same is creation, §. 926. Whence conservation is not badly called continued creation.",[]],"baum:952":["What has not been created is not conserved, §. 950, 926. Therefore neither the essences of things, nor the metaphysical evil of finite things, §. 931, nor the formal of contingent evil, nor moral evil regarded formally is conserved by God, §. 939.",[]],"baum:953":["Whatever cannot exist except through creation cannot endure except through conservation, §. 950, 926. Now all monads of this world, and whatever is substantial in it, is a creature of God. Therefore all monads of the world, and whatever is substantial in this world, is conserved by God at every moment of its duration, §. 928.",[]],"baum:954":["Since the efficient causes, besides God, are all the substances of this world, §. 319, 846, and are subordinated to God, §. 928, he is the efficient cause simply first, all the rest second, §. 315, 28. Now all actions of finite substances are at the same time passions from other finite substances influencing them, § 451. Therefore God concurs (*concurrit*), as efficient cause, in all actions of finite substances mediately, §. 314, 320. But since at the same time all passions of finite substances from other finite substances are their actions, §. 463, not only then when they are conceived as acting chiefly, but also then when they are noted to suffer, God, at that very moment at which they are changed, actuating by conserving the sufficient ground of this change of theirs, their force, §. 953, concurs immediately, as efficient cause, in all actions of finite substances, §. 320, for to the present existence of a finite substance its action pertains, §. 210, 55.",[]],"baum:955":["The immediate concurrence of God is his presence, §. 223. Therefore God is proximately present to all substances of this world, §. 954. That which is proximately present to all and single substantial parts of something is said to be intimately present@@1@@ (*intime praesens*) to that very thing. Now God is proximately present to all substantial parts of bodies in this universe, §. 421. Therefore God is intimately present to all bodies in this universe.",[]],"baum:956":["God is proximately present to every monad of this world, intimately present to every body, §. 955, and that at every moment, and as regards all actions of creatures, §. 954. Therefore God is most omnipresent (*omnipraesentissimus*).",[]],"baum:957":["Wherever God is, there he is whole, and undivided, §. 842. Therefore also as to substance (*substantia*) and essence (*essentia*), §. 830. 816. Therefore God is present to all monads (*monades*) and bodies (*corpora*) of this world, as to substance and essence, §. 955.",[]],"baum:958":["The conservation (*conservatio*) of the forces (*vires*) of this universe, whatever they may be, in the very act of them, is the physical concurrence of God (*concursus dei physicus*), and this, because and insofar as it extends to the single actions of single substances, is called general@@1@@ (universal).",[]],"baum:959":["All concurrence of God is most free (*liberrimus*), §. 932. Therefore if he concurred in the formal (*formale*) of any evil action, he would become its author (*auctor*), §. 940. Therefore he concurs in all physically and morally evil actions, as to the material (*materiale*), §. 958, but not as to the formal, §. 940.",[]],"baum:960":["The concurrence of a moral cause (*causa moralis*) strictly so called is moral@@1@@. God actuates the motives (*motiva*) of worship of himself, §. 947. Therefore he concurs morally in certain actions in this universe, §. 940. The moral concurrence of God, when added to the general, is special@@2@@. Therefore God concurs specially in certain actions in this universe.",[]],"baum:961":["God is not a tempter to evil, §. 941. Therefore he does not concur morally in the formal of any morally evil actions, §. 940. Therefore not specially either, §. 960.",[]],"baum:962":["The supernatural concurrence of God (*concursus dei supernaturalis*), when added to the special, is most special@@1@@, and is both possible in itself (*in se possibilis*), §. 475, and hypothetically, §. 860, and actual in this world, as often as some action in it cannot be actuated equally well through the special concurrence of God, §. 498, 935.",[]],"baum:963":["Government@@1@@ (*gubernatio*) is an action by which several means are successively actuated toward some further end. Therefore the better the means, the more numerous the best ones, and hence also those attaining the end by the shortest way, §. 888, the better the ends, the further the ends toward which they are actuated, the more perfect the government, §. 185. The ends that God had in creating, he has and actuates in conserving, §. 951, 944. While therefore we venerate him as the supreme governor of the universe, we worship him who at every moment actuates the best means, and the most numerous of those proceeding by the shortest ways, leading to the best ends and finally to the ultimate aim (*scopus*) of this universe. When God in governing 1) sets a certain limit to the forces (and hence the actions, §. 331) of a created thing, he is said to determine in the stricter sense@@2@@, 2) subordinates to his ends the action of the created thing not undertaken by it toward the same, he is said to direct in the stricter sense@@3@@.",[]],"baum:964":["The first sin (*peccatum*) is called a fall@@1@@ (*lapsus*), and the power of falling, liability to fall@@2@@ (*labilitas*); this is either absolute or hypothetical, §. 15, 16. The opposite of hypothetical liability to fall is confirmation in the good@@3@@.",[]],"baum:965":["Absolute liability to fall pertains to the essence of a finite spirit, §. 40, 964, hence is in it absolutely necessary, §. 106, a metaphysical evil, §. 146, which was not concreated with any spirit, §. 931, the opposite of which could not have been concreated with any creature, §. 833. A falling spirit acts, §. 964, 788. Therefore God concurs in the material of the fall, §. 918, but in the formal neither physically nor morally, §. 909, 911. Therefore he does not become the author of the fall considered formally, nor a moral cause strictly so called, §. 940.",[]],"baum:966":["The fall is contingent in itself (*in se contingens*), §. 964, since it is a change of a finite thing, §. 361. Therefore the opposite of the fall is possible in itself, §. 104. Therefore the prevention of the fall is possible in itself, §. 221. Therefore God could have prevented every fall, §. 833.",[]],"baum:967":["Moral prevention@@1@@ (*impeditio moralis*) is the actuation of motives toward the opposite of some change. God has actuated very many motives for spirits toward their happiness, §. 948. The happiness (*felicitas*) of a spirit is the opposite of its fall, §. 81, 787. Therefore God prevents every fall morally.",[]],"baum:968":["Prevention which is not moral is called physical. A fall is a sin, §. 964, therefore a moral action, §. 788, therefore free in respect of execution, §. 719, 722, hence physically contingent, §. 709. Therefore, besides the freedom of the one who fell and the motives of it, §. 967, there were many other possibles, upon the positing of which the fall that followed would not have followed, §. 378, physical impediments of the fall, §. 81, 221. Therefore God could also have prevented the fall physically, §. 833.",[]],"baum:969":["God prevents every sin morally, §. 967; he can prevent it physically through omnipotence (*omnipotentia*), §. 968. Yet he does not prevent all physically. Non-prevention is permission@@1@@ (*permissio*). Therefore it is either physical, §. 968, or moral, §. 967.",[]],"baum:970":["God permits no fall, §. 967, no sin, morally, §. 969. Yet he permits certain sins in this world physically. Posit that these too were physically prevented: this world would not be, in which they would be physically prevented, but another, §. 378, less good, §. 937. Therefore God permits certain sins in this world physically, because the world in which they are permitted physically is the best means of religion, §. 949, 935.",[]],"baum:971":["Right (*ius*) (taken for the quality of a person) in the broader signification@@1@@ is a moral faculty (*facultas moralis*); and whoever has the right of deciding about some thing whatever pleases is its full master@@2@@ (*dominus*). Full dominion over persons is full authority@@3@@ (*potestas plena*).",[]],"baum:972":["God has a right to his most right actions, §. 901, 971. In them is the decree (*decretum*) about the world and its single creatures, §. 934. Therefore God is the full master of the world and of all creatures in it. Therefore he has full authority over the spirits of this world, §. 971.",[]],"baum:973":["Whoever has full authority over a spirit has the right of obligating it in whatever way he pleases. Whoever has the right of obligating some spirit in whatever way he pleases has the right of making laws for it. For the author of the obligation which a law enunciates is said to make the law@@1@@ (*legem ferre*). One having the right of making laws is a legislator@@2@@. Therefore God is the legislator of all spirits of this world, §. 971, 972.",[]],"baum:974":["Whoever alone has the highest power and authority over several spirits is their monarch, who has full authority, their despot. Now God alone has the highest power, §. 832, and authority over all created spirits, and that full, §. 971. Therefore his is the highest and greatest despotic monarchy, whose subjects all created spirits are, §. 844.",[]],"baum:975":["The providence of God@@1@@ (*providentia dei*) is an action by which he bestows on each created thing as many goods as the highest goodness can. Therefore by conserving, §. 950, concurring, §. 954, governing, §. 963, preventing evil morally, §. 967, and not permitting it physically except what to prevent would detract from his ends, §. 970, and by making the best laws for his state (*civitas*), §. 973, 974, God exercises providence, §. 903-920, through impulsive causes toward blessedness, the turning of hearts (*verticordia*)@@2@@, through chance events (*fortuita*), holy fortune@@3@@, through the goods implanted in the nature of each thing, nourishing nature. Epicureanism, the opinion removing the providence of God, is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:976":["The decree about the existence of the world is in God most free, §. 933, hence following the most distinct cognition of all the parts of this world and the most accurate cognition of all good and evil in them, §. 889, immutable, §. 839, eternal, §. 849, and irresistible, §. 844, 222. Which in a human manner can be conceived thus: Purpose (*propositum*), προθεσισ: Let the complex of the best compossibles outside God exist, §. 822, 901. Foresight (*praevisio*), προγνωσισ: This world is the best complex of the best compossibles outside God, §. 935. Decree (*decretum*), προορισμοσ: Let this best world exist.",[]],"baum:977":["More worlds than this, more finite things than those which are parts of this world, do not exist, §. 379, nor has God decreed more about contingents than what is comprehended in the decree about the existence of this world, §. 976. That decree, therefore, insofar as it extends to all realities of this universe, is called universal, insofar as it is that than which there are no more, unique, §. 77.",[]],"baum:978":["Particular decrees of God@@1@@ either are parts of the universal and unique decree, §. 977, or would be primitive@@2@@, either opposed to it or at least not comprehended under the universal. Such are not in God, §. 976.",[]],"baum:979":["All decrees of God are acts of a will most proportional to objects, §. 894, most perfectly following the knowledge (*scientia*) of degrees of perfection or imperfection in the object, whether in desiring or in averting, §. 976. The same is also evident thus: Posit that in some decree of God the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object was not at all a motive: either God was ignorant of it, which is against §. 875, or abstracted from it, which is against §. 870, or the cognition was of that kind, most distinct, but dead, which is against §. 890.",[]],"baum:980":["A decree of God is called absolute@@1@@ if its motive was neither the foreseen perfection nor the imperfection of the object; hypothetical@@2@@, on the contrary, if it follows the foreseen perfection or imperfection of the object; no decrees of God about contingents are absolute, all are hypothetical, §. 979. Theological absolutism is the opinion positing absolute decrees of God about contingents, and is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:981":["The decree about the eternal happiness of some spirit is predestination (*praedestinatio*) in the stricter sense@@1@@, whereas in the broader@@2@@ sense it is the decree about the future. The decree about the eternal unhappiness of some spirit is reprobation@@3@@, each hypothetical, §. 980. Predestinatianism, or absolutism in conceiving predestination in the stricter sense, is an error, §. 515.",[]],"baum:982":["Revelation in the broader sense@@1@@ (*revelatio*), cf. §. 986, 989, is the signification of the divine mind made by God to creatures. Therefore by divine revelation creatures are taught the divine cognition and volition, never all, §. 862, but always as much as shall have pleased providence, §. 975.",[]],"baum:983":["God is the author of universal nature, §. 940, 466. Whatever, therefore, a creature cognizes through its nature from universal nature about the mind of God, it cognizes through divine revelation, §. 982, which is natural, §. 469. Such a theology is natural theology, §. 800.",[]],"baum:984":["All finite things are means of cognizing the divine mind, §. 858, 880; all representative forces of this universe signify their creator, §. 929, the best, most wise, §. 933. The soul itself is a representative force of its God, §. 741, 744.",[]],"baum:985":["Through the soul itself, through the single monads to which it is present, hence also through single bodies, and senses, the divine mind is revealed naturally to the soul, §. 984; through the uses of singulars, the ends of God, §. 946; through cognition of the best, his will, §. 894.",[]],"baum:986":["Revelation in the stricter sense (*revelatio strictius dicta*)@@1@@, cf. §. 982, 989, is a supernatural divine revelation made to human beings through speech (*oratio*), a most special concurrence (*concursus*) to religion (*religio*), both possible in itself and hypothetically, and actual in this world, whenever religion cannot be actualized equally well through natural revelation alone, §. 962, 949.",[]],"baum:987":["Posit that in this world a natural revelation is to be made only to certain individuals, e.g. human beings; posit that under the same circumstances a revelation is to be made supernaturally to the same individuals, richer, nobler, clearer, truer, more certain, more ardent than the merely natural one, §. 669: a revelation in the strict sense (*revelatio stricte dicta*) can occur most conformably to the order of this world, §. 484, and it exists in the same, §. 986.",[]],"baum:988":["Posit some signification (*significatio*) of the divine mind in this world for a quite good religion, §. 947, but not to be actualized naturally, although it can be actualized supernaturally; a revelation in the strict sense (*revelatio stricte dicta*) will come about in this world, §. 496, 987.",[]],"baum:989":["Revelation in the strict sense of things naturally unknowable to creatures is revelation in the strictest sense (*revelatio strictissime dicta*)@@1@@, cf. §. 982, 986. Therefore whatever cognition (*cognitio*) of the divine mind in this world for a quite good religion is naturally unknowable to certain creatures, is actualized in the same through revelation in the strictest sense, §. 988.",[]],"baum:990":["If something is held to be a revelation of God in the strict sense (*revelatio dei stricte dicta*), and yet does not promote the glory of God and religion any more, and furnishes no richer, nor nobler, nor clearer, nor truer, nor more certain, nor more ardent cognition of God than the natural one, it is not a revelation of God in the strict sense, §. 986, 495.",[]],"baum:991":["In the mind of God there is no contradiction (*contradictio*), §. 822. Therefore whatever truly contradicts natural revelation is not a revelation of God in the strict sense, §. 983, 986, nor in the strictest, §. 989.",[]],"baum:992":["Revelation of God in the strict and strictest sense does not contradict the natural one, §. 991, hence not reason (*ratio*) taken objectively, insofar as it is cognized by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 646, 984.",[]],"baum:993":["Sacred faith (*fides sacra*) taken objectively@@1@@ is the complex of things to be believed in revelation in the strict sense, just as faith held in revelation in the strict sense is sacred faith taken subjectively@@2@@. Therefore not only things wholly naturally unknowable to creatures belong to sacred faith, §. 989, 986.",[]],"baum:994":["Sacred faith, taken objectively, insofar as it is believed by a human being through faith taken subjectively, does not contradict reason taken objectively, insofar as it is cognized by sound human reason taken subjectively, §. 992, 993.",[]],"baum:995":["Revelation in the strict sense signifies the supremely possible, §. 986, 822. Now what is against reason is impossible, §. 643. Therefore neither revelation of God in the strict sense, nor in the strictest, §. 989, nor sacred faith taken objectively are against reason. But what is against reason is neither revelation of God in the strict sense, nor in the strictest, nor sacred faith taken objectively.",[]],"baum:996":["Sacred mysteries (*mysteria sacra*)@@1@@ are things posited above the reason of creatures in sacred faith taken objectively. In every revelation in the strictest sense there are mysteries, §. 989, 644. Therefore they do not conflict with revelation in the strict sense, §. 989, nor are they against reason, §. 643.",[]],"baum:997":["In all his revelation God is most sincere, §. 919, hence, judging his wisdom to be the most apt sign of speech for signifying the divine mind to human beings, and that mind supernaturally actualized, he is most truthful in revelation in the strict sense, §. 920.",[]],"baum:998":["In revelation taken in the strict and strictest sense God is a witness, §. 986, 946, most sincere and most skillful, §. 997, 889, hence most worthy of faith, whose revelation in the strict sense gives the highest certainty of faith.",[]],"baum:999":["A naturalist (*naturalista*) in the stricter signification (cf. §. 493) is one who denies in this world a revelation of God in the strict sense. The (theoretical) atheist, who denies the existence of God, errs, §. 811, 854. Naturalism in the neutral signification posits atheism necessarily, §. 493.",[]],"baum:1000":["The naturalist in the stricter signification, denying that revelation in the strict sense exists in this world, because 1) it is a supernatural event, §. 986, hence seems to him impossible, errs, §. 860, 2) he thinks sacred faith to be against reason, hence impossible, errs, §. 995, 3) he denies providence toward the human race, and errs, §. 975, by his own fault, §. 982, 986, ignorant of the most benign God, §. 903, \"Whose honor, name, and praises shall ever remain.\"",[]]};
